var tipuesearch = {"pages":[{"title":" yaeos ","text":"yaeos Phase diagrams that close Continuation methods trace PT, Px and Tx envelopes through critical points,\nincluding three-phase and multiphase boundaries, critical lines and binary\nglobal phase diagrams. Models you can extend Write the residual Helmholtz or excess Gibbs energy and get fugacities,\nresidual properties and every equilibrium routine for free. Derivatives can\nbe analytical, hyperdual or generated with Tapenade. Fortran speed, Python reach A readable modern Fortran core built with fpm , wrapped as a pip install yaeos package for notebooks, fitting and plotting. Quick start The same calculation in both languages: build a Peng-Robinson model for\nmethane + n-butane, evaluate a pressure, a bubble point and the full PT phase\nenvelope. Units are K, bar, L and mol throughout. Fortran Python program quickstart use yaeos , only : pr , ArModel , PengRobinson76 , EquilibriumState , PTEnvel2 , & saturation_pressure , pt_envelope_2ph implicit none class ( ArModel ), allocatable :: model type ( EquilibriumState ) :: bubble type ( PTEnvel2 ) :: envelope real ( pr ) :: z ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: P ! Methane + n-butane with Peng-Robinson (1976): Tc [K], Pc [bar], omega model = PengRobinson76 ( tc = [ 19 0.564_pr , 42 5.12_pr ], & pc = [ 4 5.99_pr , 3 7.96_pr ], w = [ 0.0115478_pr , 0.200164_pr ]) z = [ 0.3_pr , 0.7_pr ] ! mol call model % pressure ( z , V = 1.0_pr , T = 30 0.0_pr , P = P ) bubble = saturation_pressure ( model , z , T = 30 0.0_pr , kind = \"bubble\" ) envelope = pt_envelope_2ph ( model , z , & saturation_pressure ( model , z , T = 20 0.0_pr , kind = \"dew\" )) print * , \"P(1 L, 300 K) =\" , P , \"bar\" print * , \"Bubble P(300 K) =\" , bubble % P , \"bar\" print * , \"Critical point:\" , envelope % cps ( 1 )% T , \"K\" , envelope % cps ( 1 )% P , \"bar\" end program quickstart import numpy as np from yaeos import PengRobinson76 # Methane + n-butane with Peng-Robinson (1976) model = PengRobinson76 ( critical_temperatures = [ 190.564 , 425.12 ], # K critical_pressures = [ 45.99 , 37.96 ], # bar acentric_factors = [ 0.0115478 , 0.200164 ], ) z = np . array ([ 0.3 , 0.7 ]) # mol P = model . pressure ( z , volume = 1.0 , temperature = 300.0 ) bubble = model . saturation_pressure ( z , temperature = 300.0 , kind = \"bubble\" ) envelope = model . phase_envelope_pt ( z , kind = \"dew\" , t0 = 200.0 , p0 = 1.0 ) print ( f \"P(1 L, 300 K) = { P : .4f } bar\" ) print ( f \"Bubble P(300 K) = { bubble [ 'P' ] : .4f } bar\" ) print ( \"Critical point:\" , envelope . critical_temperatures , envelope . critical_pressures ) envelope . plot () # requires matplotlib Both print a pressure of 15.91 bar, a bubble pressure of 57.37 bar and a\ncritical point near 401.5 K and 68.1 bar. The guide walks through each step and explains how to\nread the results. Install Fortran — add yaeos as an fpm dependency (BLAS and LAPACK are required, e.g. sudo apt install libblas-dev liblapack-dev ): [dependencies] yaeos = { git = \"https://github.com/ipqa-research/yaeos\" } Python — binary wheels are published on PyPI: pip install yaeos Models Residual Helmholtz ( ) Excess Gibbs ( ) Cubic EoS : SRK, PR76, PR78, RKPR, PSRK NRTL and NRTL-HV Alpha functions : Soave, RKPR, Mathias-Copeman UNIQUAC Mixing rules : QMR, QMRTD, CMR, CMRTD, MHV, HV, HV-NRTL, sDDLC UNIFAC VLE , Dortmund and PSRK GERG-2008 multifluid PC-SAFT Missing one? Add your own model by\nwriting its energy function. Explore the documentation Fundamentals Equations of state, fugacity and phase equilibrium for programmers who are new to thermodynamics. Equations of state Volumes, pressures, fugacities and residual properties from A&#94;r models. Excess Gibbs models Activity coefficients and excess properties from G&#94;E models. Phase equilibrium Flashes, saturation points, stability, envelopes and critical points. New models Implement a model with analytical derivatives or automatic differentiation. Developer handbook Architecture, numerics, the Python bridge, testing and workflow. Python API Reference for the yaeos Python package, with tutorials. Fortran API Every module, type and procedure, generated from the source. Contributing How to report issues, propose changes and follow the style guide. Citing and community yaeos is developed by the research group of Prof. Martín Cismondi-Duarte at IPQA (UNC-CONICET) and released under the Mozilla Public License 2.0 .\nIf yaeos is useful in your work, please cite it using the repository’s CITATION.cff (GitHub’s “Cite this repository” button exports BibTeX and APA). Questions,\nbug reports and ideas are welcome on the issue tracker . Developer Info Federico E. Benelli PhD student with focus on reservoir PVT simulation.","tags":"home","url":"index.html"},{"title":"PXEnvelMP – yaeos ","text":"type, public :: PXEnvelMP PXEnvelMP Multiphase pressure-composition envelope at fixed temperature. Description Stores converged multiphase states and their feed interpolation\ncoordinates, critical markers, and phase-kind labels. Examples Access the converged states through line%points . Inherits type~~pxenvelmp~~InheritsGraph type~pxenvelmp PXEnvelMP type~mppoint~2 MPPoint type~pxenvelmp->type~mppoint~2 points Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Type Type This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a derived type to the parent type which it\nextends. Dashed arrows point from a derived type to the other\ntypes it contains as a components, with a label listing the name(s) of\nsaid component(s). Components Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial real(kind=pr), public, allocatable :: Pc (:) Critical pressure [bar] real(kind=pr), public, allocatable :: ac (:) Critical alphas. real(kind=pr), public, allocatable :: alpha (:) Molar relation between two mixtures. type( MPPoint ), public, allocatable :: points (:) Array of converged points. real(kind=pr), public, allocatable :: z0 (:) Original mixture mole fractions. real(kind=pr), public, allocatable :: zi (:) Other mixture mole fractions Type-Bound Procedures procedure, public, nopass :: get_values_from_X private  subroutine get_values_from_X (X, np, z0, zi, beta_w, x_l, w, betas, P, alpha) Extract the values of the variables from the vector X. Read more… Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=pr), intent(in) :: X (:) Vector of variables. integer, intent(in) :: np Number of main phases. real(kind=pr), intent(in) :: z0 (:) Initial mixture composition. real(kind=pr), intent(in) :: zi (:) Second mixture composition. real(kind=pr), intent(in) :: beta_w Reference phase beta. real(kind=pr), intent(out) :: x_l (np,size(z0)) Mole fractions of the main phases. real(kind=pr), intent(out) :: w (size(z0)) Mole fractions of the incipient phase. real(kind=pr), intent(out) :: betas (np) Fractions of the main phases. real(kind=pr), intent(out) :: P Pressure [bar]. real(kind=pr), intent(out) :: alpha . procedure, public, nopass :: solve_point private  subroutine solve_point (model, z0, zi, np, T, beta_w, x_kinds, w_kind, X, ns, S, dXdS, F, df, iters, max_iterations) Solve the system of equations for a multiphase point. Read more… Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( ArModel ), intent(in) :: model Model to use. real(kind=pr), intent(in) :: z0 (:) First mixture composition. real(kind=pr), intent(in) :: zi (:) Second mixture composition. integer, intent(in) :: np Number of main phases real(kind=pr), intent(in) :: T Temperature [K] real(kind=pr), intent(in) :: beta_w Fraction of the reference (incipient) phase character(len=14), intent(in) :: x_kinds (np) Volume-root kind of each main phase (e.g. \"liquid\" , \"vapor\" , \"stable\" ). character(len=14), intent(in) :: w_kind Volume-root kind of the reference (incipient) phase. real(kind=pr), intent(inout) :: X (:) Vector of variables integer, intent(in) :: ns Number of specification real(kind=pr), intent(in) :: S Specification value real(kind=pr), intent(in) :: dXdS