tor. procedure( process ), optional :: update_specification Optional callback to select the next specification and step. If absent,\nthe tangent component of largest magnitude is selected and the step is\nscaled using that component: Read more… procedure( process ), optional :: postprocess Optional callback to modify the state or step after specification\nselection, for example to enforce bounds or change step direction. procedure( continuation_solver ), optional :: solver Optional point corrector. The built-in Newton solver is used by default. procedure( continuation_stopper ), optional :: stop Optional callback evaluated after each accepted point; true terminates\ncontinuation. Return Value real(kind=pr), (max_points,size(X0)) Stored solution vectors, one per row. Subroutines public  subroutine full_newton (fun, iters, X, ns, S, dS, dXdS, point, max_iters, F, df, dfds, tol) Correct one predicted continuation point with Newton’s method.\nThe correction solves at fixed specification value,\nhalving the Newton step until . It stops\nwhen or tol .\nIf iteration 10 is reached (for points after the first), is reset\nto the previous point plus 10% of the predictor step. On failure iters ends at max_iters + 1 . Read more… Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name procedure( continuation_function ) :: fun Residual/Jacobian callback. integer, intent(out) :: iters Newton iterations used for this point. real(kind=pr), intent(inout) :: X (:) Predicted vector; corrected solution on return. integer, intent(in) :: ns Index of the specified variable. real(kind=pr), intent(in) :: S Fixed specification value for this correction. real(kind=pr), intent(in) :: dS Step used to form the predictor. real(kind=pr), intent(in) :: dXdS (:) Tangent used by the predictor and recovery logic. integer, intent(in) :: point One-based continuation point number. integer, intent(in) :: max_iters Maximum Newton iterations for correction. real(kind=pr), intent(out) :: F (:) Residual at the corrected point. real(kind=pr), intent(out) :: df (:,:) Jacobian . real(kind=pr), intent(out) :: dfds (:) Derivative . real(kind=pr), intent(in) :: tol Residual (max-norm) convergence tolerance.","tags":"","url":"module/yaeos__math_continuation.html"},{"title":"hyperdual_mod – yaeos","text":"Hyperdual arithmetic Type and overloaded operations for exact first- and second-order directional derivatives. Description A hyperdual value stores a primal value and two independent tangent seeds: The nilpotent rules make the\nTaylor expansion exact through the mixed second derivative. Overloaded\narithmetic and elementary functions propagate these coefficients, so\nderivatives are obtained without a finite-difference step-size error.\nFor a scalar function the propagation rule is Seeding , gives in f1 / f2 and in f12 ; seeding different variables in and gives mixed second derivatives. Examples type ( hyperdual ) :: x , y x = hyperdual ( f0 = 2.0_pr , f1 = 1.0_pr , f2 = 1.0_pr ) y = exp ( x ) print * , y % f0 , y % f1 , y % f2 , y % f12 References Fike, J. A., and A. C. Alonso (2011). The Development of Hyper-Dual\nNumbers for Exact Second-Derivative Calculations. AIAA 49th Aerospace\nSciences Meeting. DOI: 10.2514/6.2011-886.\nRehner, P., and G. Bauer (2021). Application of Generalized (Hyper-) Dual\nNumbers in Equation of State Modeling. Frontiers in Chemical Engineering.\nDOI: 10.3389/fceng.2021.758090. Uses yaeos__constants module~~hyperdual_mod~~UsesGraph module~hyperdual_mod hyperdual_mod module~yaeos__constants yaeos__constants module~hyperdual_mod->module~yaeos__constants iso_fortran_env iso_fortran_env module~yaeos__constants->iso_fortran_env Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Module Module Submodule Submodule Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a submodule to the (sub)module which it is\ndescended from. Dashed arrows point from a module or program unit to