> Compare operands using less-than-or-equal. !> !> # Description !> Returns a logical result based on primal values; derivative slots do not affect comparisons. !> !> # Examples !> ```fortran !> logical_value = x < y !> ``` logical function le_id ( lhs , rhs ) integer , intent ( in ) :: lhs !! First input operand. type ( hyperdual ), intent ( in ) :: rhs !! Second input operand. le_id = lhs <= rhs % f0 end function le_id !------------------------------------------------------------------- !--- Functions for the greater than operator. ---------------------- !------------------------------------------------------------------- !> # gt_dd !> Compare operands using greater-than. !> !> # Description !> Returns a logical result based on primal values; derivative slots do not affect comparisons. !> !> # Examples !> ```fortran !> logical_value = x < y !> ``` logical function gt_dd ( lhs , rhs ) type ( hyperdual ), intent ( in ) :: lhs !! Left-hand operand. type ( hyperdual ), intent ( in ) :: rhs !! Right-hand operand. gt_dd = lhs % f0 > rhs % f0 end function gt_dd !> # gt_dr !> Compare operands using greater-than. !> !> # Description !> Returns a logical result based on primal values; derivative slots do not affect comparisons. !> !> # Examples !> ```fortran !> logical_value = x < y !> ``` logical function gt_dr ( lhs , rhs ) type ( hyperdual ), intent ( in ) :: lhs !! First input operand. real ( pr ), intent ( in ) :: rhs !! Second input operand. gt_dr = lhs % f0 > rhs end function gt_dr !> # gt_rd !> Compare operands using greater-than. !> !> # Description !> Returns a logical result based on primal values; derivative slots do not affect comparisons. !> !> # Examples !> ```fortran !> logical_value = x < y !> ``` logical function gt_rd ( lhs , rhs ) real ( pr ), intent ( in ) :: lhs !! First input operand. type ( hyperdual ), intent ( in ) :: rhs !! Second input operand. gt_rd = lhs > rhs % f0 end function gt_rd !> # gt_di !> Compare operands using greater-than. !> !> # Description !> Returns a logical result based on primal values; derivative slots do not affect comparisons. !> !> # Examples !> ```fortran !> logical_value = x < y !> ``` logical function gt_di ( lhs , rhs ) type ( hyperdual ), intent ( in ) :: lhs !! First input operand. integer , intent ( in ) :: rhs !! Second input operand. gt_di = lhs % f0 > rhs end function gt_di !> # gt_id !> Compare operands using greater-than. !> !> # Description !> Returns a logical result based on primal values; derivative slots do not affect comparisons. !> !> # Examples !> ```fortran !> logical_value = x < y !> ``` logical function gt_id ( lhs , rhs ) integer , intent ( in ) :: lhs !! First input operand. type ( hyperdual ), intent ( in ) :: rhs !! Second input operand. gt_id = lhs > rhs % f0 end function gt_id !------------------------------------------------------------------- !--- Functions for the greater than or equal operator. ------------- !------------------------------------------------------------------- !> # ge_dd !> Compare operands using greater-than-or-equal. !> !> # Description !> Returns a logical result based on primal values; derivative slots do not affect comparisons. !> !> # Examples !> ```fortran !> logical_value = x < y !> ``` logical function ge_dd ( lhs , rhs ) type ( hyperdual ), intent ( in ) :: lhs !! Left-hand operand. type ( hyperdual ), intent ( in ) :: rhs !! Right-hand operand. ge_dd = lhs % f0 >= rhs % f0 end function ge_dd !> # ge_dr !> Compare operands using greater-than-or-equal. !> !> # Description !> Returns a logical result based on primal values; derivative slots do not affect comparisons. !> !> # Examples !> ```fortran !> logical_value = x < y !> ``` logical function ge_dr ( lhs , rhs ) type ( hyperdual ), intent ( in ) :: lhs !! First input operand. real ( pr ), intent ( in ) :: rhs !! Second input operand. ge_dr = lhs % f0 >= rhs end function ge_dr !> # ge_rd !> Compare operands using greater-than-or-equal. !> !> # Description !> Returns a logical result based on primal values; derivative slots do not affect comparisons. !> !> # Examples !> ```fortran !> logical_value = x < y !> ``` logical function ge_rd ( lhs , rhs ) real ( pr ), intent ( in ) :: lhs !! First input operand. type ( hyperdual ), intent ( in ) :: rhs !! Second input operand. ge_rd = lhs >= rhs % f0 end function ge_rd !> # ge_di !> Compare operands using greater-than-or-equal. !> !> # Description !> Returns a logical result based on primal values; derivative slots do not affect comparisons. !> !> # Examples !> ```fortran !> logical_value = x < y !> ``` logical function ge_di ( lhs , rhs ) type ( hyperdual ), intent ( in ) :: lhs !! First input operand. integer , intent ( in ) :: rhs !! Second input operand. ge_di = lhs % f0 >= rhs end function ge_di !> # ge_id !> Compare operands using greater-than-or-equal. !> !> # Description !> Returns a logical result based on primal values; derivative slots do not affect comparisons. !> !> # Examples !> ```fortran !> logical_value = x < y !> ``` logical function ge_id ( lhs , rhs ) integer , intent ( in ) :: lhs !! First input operand. type ( hyperdual ), intent ( in ) :: rhs !! Second input operand. ge_id = lhs >= rhs % f0 end function ge_id !------------------------------------------------------------------- !--- Math functions. ----------------------------------------------- !------------------------------------------------------------------- ! Absolute value function. !> # absHyperDual !> Evaluate the absolute value of a hyperdual value. !> !> # Description !> Uses the primal sign to propagate derivative coefficients through abs. !> !> # Examples !> ```fortran !> y = abs(x) !> ``` elemental function absHyperDual ( v1 ) result ( v2 ) type ( hyperdual ), intent ( in ) :: v1 !! First input operand. type ( hyperdual ) :: v2 if ( v1 % f0 >= 0.0 ) then v2 % f0 = v1 % f0 v2 % f1 = v1 % f1 v2 % f2 = v1 % f2 v2 % f12 = v1 % f12 else v2 % f0 = - v1 % f0 v2 % f1 = - v1 % f1 v2 % f2 = - v1 % f2 v2 % f12 = - v1 % f12 endif end function absHyperDual ! Integer function. !> # intHyperDual !> Convert a hyperdual value to an integer-valued result. !> !> # Description !> Applies integer conversion to the primal value; this discontinuous operation is not intended for derivative propagation. !> !> # Examples !> ```fortran !> y = int(x) !> ``` elemental function intHyperDual ( v1 ) result ( v2 ) type ( hyperdual ), intent ( in ) :: v1 !! First input operand. integer :: v2 v2 = int ( v1 % f0 ) end function intHyperDual ! Nearest integer function. !> # nintHyperDual !> Round a hyperdual value to the nearest integer. !> !> # Description !> Applies nearest-integer conversion to the primal value; this discontinuous operation is not intended for derivative propagation. !> !> # Examples !> ```fortran !> y = nint(x) !> ``` elemental function nintHyperDual ( v1 ) result ( v2 ) type ( hyperdual ), intent ( in ) :: v1 !! First input operand. integer :: v2 v2 = nint ( v1 % f0 ) end function nintHyperDual ! Real function. !> # realHyperDual !> Convert a hyperdual value to a real-valued result. !> !> # Description !> Returns the primal real value; derivative information is not retained. !> !> # Examples !> ```fortran !> y = real(x) !> ``` elemental function realHyperDual ( v1 ) result ( v2 ) type ( hyperdual ), intent ( in ) :: v1 !! First input operand. real ( pr ) :: v2 v2 = v1 % f0 end function realHyperDual ! Functions for the sign function. !> # sign_dd !> Apply the Fortran SIGN intrinsic to hyperdual operands. !> !> # Description !> Selects the sign from primal values and propagates the selected magnitude. !> !> # Examples !> ```fortran !> y = sign(x, sign_source) !> ``` elemental function sign_dd ( v1 , v2 ) result ( v3 ) type ( hyperdual ), intent ( in ) :: v1 !! First input operand. type ( hyperdual ), intent ( in ) :: v2 !! Second input operand. type ( hyperdual ) :: v3 real ( pr ) :: ssign if ( v2 % f0 < 0.0 ) then ssign = - 1.0 else ssign = 1.0 endif v3 = ssign * v1 end function sign_dd !> # sign_dr !> Apply the Fortran SIGN intrinsic to hyperdual operands. !> !> # Description !> Selects the sign from primal values and propagates the selected magnitude. !> !> # Examples !> ```fortran !> y = sign(x, sign_source) !> ``` elemental function sign_dr ( v1 , v2 ) result ( v3 ) type ( hyperdual ), intent ( in ) :: v1 !! First input operand. real ( pr ), intent ( in ) :: v2 !! Second input operand. type ( hyperdual ) :: v3 real ( pr ) :: ssign if ( v2 < 0.0 ) then ssign = - 1.0 else ssign = 1.0 endif v3 = ssign * v1 end function sign_dr !> # sign_rd !> Apply the Fortran SIGN intrinsic to hyperdual operands. !> !> # Description !> Selects the sign from primal values and propagates the selected magnitude. !> !> # Examples !> ```fortran !> y = sign(x, sign_source) !> ``` elemental function sign_rd ( v1 , v2 ) result ( v3 ) real ( pr ), intent ( in ) :: v1 !! First input operand. type ( hyperdual ), intent ( in ) :: v2 !! Second input operand. type ( hyperdual ) :: v3 real ( pr ) :: ssign if ( v2 % f0 < 0.0 ) then ssign = - 1.0 else ssign = 1.0 endif v3 = ssign * v1 end function sign_rd ! Sine function. !> # sinHyperDual !> Evaluate the sin intrinsic for a hyperdual value. !> !> # Description !> Applies the intrinsic and its derivatives to the hyperdual coefficients. !> !> # Examples !> ```fortran !> y = sin(x) !> ``` elemental function sinHyperDual ( v1 ) result ( v2 ) type ( hyperdual ), intent ( in ) :: v1 !! First input operand. type ( hyperdual ) :: v2 real ( pr ) :: f real ( pr ) :: dx f = sin ( v1 % f0 ) dx = cos ( v1 % f0 ) v2 % f0 = f v2 % f1 = dx * v1 % f1 v2 % f2 = dx * v1 % f2 v2 % f12 = dx * v1 % f12 - f * v1 % f1 * v1 % f2 end function sinHyperDual ! Cosine function. !> # cosHyperDual !> Evaluate the cos intrinsic for a hyperdual value. !> !> # Description !> Applies the intrinsic and its derivatives to the hyperdual coefficients. !> !> # Examples !> ```fortran !> y = cos(x) !> ``` elemental function cosHyperDual ( v1 ) result ( v2 ) type ( hyperdual ), intent ( in ) :: v1 !! First input operand. type ( hyperdual ) :: v2 real ( pr ) :: f real ( pr ) :: dx f = cos ( v1 % f0 ) dx = - sin ( v1 % f0 ) v2 % f0 = f v2 % f1 = dx * v1 % f1 v2 % f2 = dx * v1 % f2 v2 % f12 = dx * v1 % f12 - f * v1 % f1 * v1 % f2 end function cosHyperDual ! Tangent function. !> # tanHyperDual !> Evaluate the tan intrinsic for a hyperdual value. !> !> # Description !> Applies the intrinsic and its derivatives to the hyperdual coefficients. !> !> # Examples !> ```fortran !> y = tan(x) !> ``` elemental function tanHyperDual ( v1 ) result ( v2 ) type ( hyperdual ), intent ( in ) :: v1 !! First input operand. type ( hyperdual ) :: v2 real ( pr ) :: f real ( pr ) :: dx f = tan ( v1 % f0 ) dx = f * f + 1.0_pr v2 % f0 = f v2 % f1 = dx * v1 % f1 v2 % f2 = dx * v1 % f2 v2 % f12 = dx * v1 % f12 + v1 % f1 * v1 % f2 * 2.0_pr * f * dx end function tanHyperDual ! Sqrt function !> # sqrtHyperDual !> Evaluate the sqrt intrinsic for a hyperdual value. !> !> # Description !> Applies the intrinsic and its derivatives to the hyperdual coefficients. !> !> # Examples !> ```fortran !> y = sqrt(x) !> ``` elemental function sqrtHyperDual ( v1 ) result ( v2 ) type ( hyperdual ), intent ( in ) :: v1 !! First input operand. type ( hyperdual ) :: v2 real ( pr ), parameter :: expo = 3.0_pr / 2.0_pr real ( pr ) :: square square = sqrt ( v1 % f0 ) v2 % f0 = square v2 % f1 = 0.5_pr / square * v1 % f1 v2 % f2 = 0.5_pr / square * v1 % f2 v2 % f12 = 0.5_pr * v1 % f12 / square - 0.25_pr * v1 % f1 * v1 % f2 / ( v1 % f0 ** expo ) end function sqrtHyperDual ! Log function !> # logHyperDual !> Evaluate the log intrinsic for a hyperdual value. !> !> # Description !> Applies the intrinsic and its derivatives to the hyperdual coefficients. !> !> # Examples !> ```fortran !> y = log(x) !> ``` elemental function logHyperDual ( v1 ) result ( v2 ) type ( hyperdual ), intent ( in ) :: v1 !! First input operand. type ( hyperdual ) :: v2 real ( pr ) :: dx1 real ( pr ) :: dx2 dx1 = v1 % f1 / v1 % f0 dx2 = v1 % f2 / v1 % f0 v2 % f0 = log ( v1 % f0 ) v2 % f1 = dx1 v2 % f2 = dx2 v2 % f12 = v1 % f12 / v1 % f0 - ( dx1 * dx2 ) end function logHyperDual ! Log10 function !> # log10HyperDual !> Evaluate the log10 intrinsic for a hyperdual value. !> !> # Description !> Applies the intrinsic and its derivatives to the hyperdual coefficients. !> !> # Examples !> ```fortran !> y = log10(x) !> ``` elemental function log10HyperDual ( v1 ) result ( v2 ) type ( hyperdual ), intent ( in ) :: v1 !! First input operand. type ( hyperdual ) :: v2 v2 = log ( v1 ) / log ( 1 0.0_pr ) end function log10HyperDual ! Exp function !> # expHyperDual !> Evaluate the exp intrinsic for a hyperdual value. !> !> # Description !> Applies the intrinsic and its derivatives to the hyperdual coefficients. !> !> # Examples !> ```fortran !> y = exp(x) !> ``` elemental function expHyperDual ( v1 ) result ( v2 ) type ( hyperdual ), intent ( in ) :: v1 !! First input operand. type ( hyperdual ) :: v2 real ( pr ) :: dx dx = exp ( v1 % f0 ) v2 % f0 = dx v2 % f1 = dx * v1 % f1 v2 % f2 = dx * v1 % f2 v2 % f12 = dx * ( v1 % f12 + v1 % f1 * v1 % f2 ) end function expHyperDual ! Sinh function !> # sinhHyperDual !> Evaluate the sinh intrinsic for a hyperdual value. !> !> # Description !> Applies the intrinsic and its derivatives to the hyperdual coefficients. !> !> # Examples !> ```fortran !> y = sinh(x) !> ``` elemental function sinhHyperDual ( v1 ) result ( v2 ) type ( hyperdual ), intent ( in ) :: v1 !! First input operand. type ( hyperdual ) :: t1 type ( hyperdual ) :: t2 type ( hyperdual ) :: v2 t1 = exp ( v1 ) t2 = exp ( - v1 ) v2 = 0.5_pr * ( t1 - t2 ) end function sinhHyperDual ! Cosh function !> # coshHyperDual !> Evaluate the cosh intrinsic for a hyperdual value. !> !> # Description !> Applies the intrinsic and its derivatives to the hyperdual coefficients. !> !> # Examples !> ```fortran !> y = cosh(x) !> ``` elemental function coshHyperDual ( v1 ) result ( v2 ) type ( hyperdual ), intent ( in ) :: v1 !! First input operand. type ( hyperdual ) :: t1 type ( hyperdual ) :: t2 type ( hyperdual ) :: v2 t1 = exp ( v1 ) t2 = exp ( - v1 ) v2 = 0.5_pr * ( t1 + t2 ) end function coshHyperDual ! Tanh function !> # tanhHyperDual !> Evaluate the tanh intrinsic for a hyperdual value. !> !> # Description !> Applies the intrinsic and its derivatives to the hyperdual coefficients. !> !> # Examples !> ```fortran !> y = tanh(x) !> ``` elemental function tanhHyperDual ( v1 ) result ( v2 ) type ( hyperdual ), intent ( in ) :: v1 !! First input operand. type ( hyperdual ) :: t1 type ( hyperdual ) :: t2 type ( hyperdual ) :: v2 t1 = exp ( v1 ) t2 = exp ( - v1 ) v2 = ( t1 - t2 ) / ( t1 + t2 ) end function tanhHyperDual ! Acos function !> # acosHyperDual !> Evaluate the acos intrinsic for a hyperdual value. !> !> # Description !> Applies the intrinsic and its derivatives to the hyperdual coefficients. !> !> # Examples !> ```fortran !> y = acos(x) !> ``` elemental function acosHyperDual ( v1 ) result ( v2 ) type ( hyperdual ), intent ( in ) :: v1 !! First input operand. type ( hyperdual ) :: v2 real ( pr ) :: deriv real ( pr ) :: deriv1 deriv1 = 1.0_pr - v1 % f0 * v1 % f0 deriv = - 1.0_pr / sqrt ( deriv1 ) v2 % f0 = acos ( v1 % f0 ) v2 % f1 = deriv * v1 % f1 v2 % f2 = deriv * v1 % f2 v2 % f12 = deriv * v1 % f12 & & + v1 % f1 * v1 % f2 * ( - v1 % f0 * deriv1 ** ( - 1.5_pr )) end function acosHyperDual ! Asin function !> # asinHyperDual !> Evaluate the asin intrinsic for a hyperdual value. !> !> # Description !> Applies the intrinsic and its derivatives to the hyperdual coefficients. !> !> # Examples !> ```fortran !> y = asin(x) !> ``` elemental function asinHyperDual ( v1 ) result ( v2 ) type ( hyperdual ), intent ( in ) :: v1 !! First input operand. type ( hyperdual ) :: v2 real ( pr ) :: deriv real ( pr ) :: deriv1 deriv1 = 1.0_pr - v1 % f0 * v1 % f0 deriv = 1.0_pr / sqrt ( deriv1 ) v2 % f0 = asin ( v1 % f0 ) v2 % f1 = deriv * v1 % f1 v2 % f2 = deriv * v1 % f2 v2 % f12 = deriv * v1 % f12 & & + v1 % f1 * v1 % f2 * ( v1 % f0 * deriv1 ** ( - 1.5_pr )) end function asinHyperDual ! Atan function !> # atanHyperDual !> Evaluate the atan intrinsic for a hyperdual value. !> !> # Description !> Applies the intrinsic and its derivatives to the hyperdual coefficients. !> !> # Examples !> ```fortran !> y = atan(x) !> ``` elemental function atanHyperDual ( v1 ) result ( v2 ) type ( hyperdual ), intent ( in ) :: v1 !! First input operand. type ( hyperdual ) :: v2 real ( pr ) :: deriv real ( pr ) :: deriv1 deriv1 = 1.0_pr + v1 % f0 * v1 % f0 deriv = 1.0_pr / deriv1 v2 % f0 = atan ( v1 % f0 ) v2 % f1 = deriv * v1 % f1 v2 % f2 = deriv * v1 % f2 v2 % f12 = deriv * v1 % f12 & & + v1 % f1 * v1 % f2 * ( - 2.0_pr * v1 % f0 / ( deriv1 * deriv1 )) end function atanHyperDual ! Atan2 function !> # atan2HyperDual !> Evaluate `atan2(v1, v2)` for hyperdual arguments. !> !> # Description !> Only the primal value and the \\varepsilon_1 coefficient `f1` are !> propagated; `f2` and `f12` of the result are left at zero, so this !> function must not be used where second derivatives are needed. !> !> # Examples !> ```fortran !> z = atan2(y, x) !> ``` elemental function atan2HyperDual ( v1 , v2 ) result ( v3 ) type ( hyperdual ), intent ( in ) :: v1 !! First input operand. type ( hyperdual ), intent ( in ) :: v2 !! Second input operand. type ( hyperdual ) :: v3 real ( pr ) :: a real ( pr ) :: b real ( pr ) :: c real ( pr ) :: d a = v1 % f0 b = v1 % f1 c = v2 % f0 d = v2 % f1 v3 % f0 = atan2 ( a , c ) v3 % f1 = ( c * b - a * d ) / ( a * a + c * c ) end function atan2HyperDual ! Max functions !> # max_dd !> Select the maximum hyperdual operand. !> !> # Description !> Selects the branch using primal values and returns the selected hyperdual operand. !> !> # Examples !> ```fortran !> z = max(x, y) !> ``` elemental function max_dd ( v1 , v2 ) result ( v3 ) type ( hyperdual ), intent ( in ) :: v1 !! First input operand. type ( hyperdual ), intent ( in ) :: v2 !! Second input operand. type ( hyperdual ) :: v3 if ( v1 % f0 > v2 % f0 ) then v3 = v1 else v3 = v2 endif end function max_dd !> # max_ddd !> Select the maximum hyperdual operand. !> !> # Description !> Selects the branch using primal values and returns the selected hyperdual operand. !> !> # Examples !> ```fortran !> w = max(x, y, z) !> ``` elemental function max_ddd ( v1 , v2 , v3 ) result ( v4 ) type ( hyperdual ), intent ( in ) :: v1 !! First input operand. type ( hyperdual ), intent ( in ) :: v2 !! Second input operand. type ( hyperdual ), intent ( in ) :: v3 !! Result of the operation. type ( hyperdual ) :: v4 if ( v1 % f0 > v2 % f0 ) then v4 = v1 else v4 = v2 endif if ( v3 % f0 > v4 % f0 ) v4 = v3 end function max_ddd !> # max_dr !> Select the maximum hyperdual operand. !> !> # Description !> Selects the branch using primal values and returns the selected hyperdual operand. !> !> # Examples !> ```fortran !> z = max(x, y) !> ``` elemental function max_dr ( v1 , v2 ) result ( v3 ) type ( hyperdual ), intent ( in ) :: v1 !! First input operand. real ( pr ), intent ( in ) :: v2 !! Second input operand. type ( hyperdual ) :: v3 if ( v1 % f0 > v2 ) then v3 = v1 else v3 = v2 endif end function max_dr !> # max_rd !> Select the maximum hyperdual operand. !> !> # Description !> Selects the branch using primal values and returns the selected hyperdual operand. !> !> # Examples !> ```fortran !> z = max(x, y) !> ``` elemental function max_rd ( v1 , v2 ) result ( v3 ) real ( pr ), intent ( in ) :: v1 !! First input operand. type ( hyperdual ), intent ( in ) :: v2 !! Second input operand. type ( hyperdual ) :: v3 if ( v1 > v2 % f0 ) then v3 = v1 else v3 = v2 endif end function max_rd ! Min functions !> # min_dd !> Select the minimum hyperdual operand. !> !> # Description !> Selects the branch using primal values and returns the selected hyperdual operand. !> !> # Examples !> ```fortran !> z = min(x, y) !> ``` elemental function min_dd ( v1 , v2 ) result ( v3 ) type ( hyperdual ), intent ( in ) :: v1 !! First input operand. type ( hyperdual ), intent ( in ) :: v2 !! Second input operand. type ( hyperdual ) :: v3 if ( v1 % f0 < v2 % f0 ) then v3 = v1 else v3 = v2 endif end function min_dd !> # min_dr !> Select the minimum hyperdual operand. !> !> # Description !> Selects the branch using primal values and returns the selected hyperdual operand. !> !> # Examples !> ```fortran !> z = min(x, y) !> ``` elemental function min_dr ( v1 , v2 ) result ( v3 ) type ( hyperdual ), intent ( in ) :: v1 !! First input operand. real ( pr ), intent ( in ) :: v2 !! Second input operand. type ( hyperdual ) :: v3 if ( v1 % f0 < v2 ) then v3 = v1 else v3 = v2 endif end function min_dr !> # min_rd !> Select the minimum hyperdual operand. !> !> # Description !> Selects the branch using primal values and returns the selected hyperdual operand. !> !> # Examples !> ```fortran !> z = min(x, y) !> ``` elemental function min_rd ( v1 , v2 ) result ( v3 ) real ( pr ), intent ( in ) :: v1 !! First input operand. type ( hyperdual ), intent ( in ) :: v2 !! Second input operand. type ( hyperdual ) :: v3 if ( v1 < v2 % f0 ) then v3 = v1 else v3 = v2 endif end function min_rd end module","tags":"","url":"sourcefile/hyperdual.f90.html"},{"title":"base.f90 – yaeos","text":"This file depends on sourcefile~~base.f90~~EfferentGraph sourcefile~base.f90 base.f90 sourcefile~substance.f90 substance.f90 sourcefile~base.f90->sourcefile~substance.f90 sourcefile~constants.f90 constants.f90 sourcefile~substance.f90->sourcefile~constants.f90 Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Source File Source File This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a file to a file which it depends on. A file\nis dependent upon another if the latter must be compiled before the former\ncan be. Files dependent on this one sourcefile~~base.f90~~AfferentGraph sourcefile~base.f90 base.f90 sourcefile~ar_models.f90 ar_models.f90 sourcefile~ar_models.f90->sourcefile~base.f90 s