+ argon 1 1 1 1 20 21 Helium + argon 1 1 1 1 Binary departure functions The departure function of pair is and it enters the mixture energy multiplied by . All\ncoefficients are dimensionless. Only the 15 pairs below have a departure\nfunction; for every other pair and the term is skipped. Seven pairs\nhave a specific function. The other eight share one generalized function and\ndiffer only in . Pair Departure function 1 2 Methane + nitrogen 1 Specific 1 3 Methane + carbon dioxide 1 Specific 1 4 Methane + ethane 1 Specific 1 5 Methane + propane 1 Specific 1 6 Methane + n-butane 1 Generalized 1 7 Methane + isobutane 0.771035405688 Generalized 1 15 Methane + hydrogen 1 Specific 2 3 Nitrogen + carbon dioxide 1 Specific 2 4 Nitrogen + ethane 1 Specific 4 5 Ethane + propane 0.13042476515 Generalized 4 6 Ethane + n-butane 0.281570073085 Generalized 4 7 Ethane + isobutane 0.260632376098 Generalized 5 6 Propane + n-butane 0.0312572600489 Generalized 5 7 Propane + isobutane -0.0551609771024 Generalized 6 7 n-Butane + isobutane -0.0551240293009 Generalized , , and apply only\nto the exponential terms and are shown as “–” for the polynomial terms. The\ngeneralized function and the methane + hydrogen function have polynomial\nterms only. Methane + nitrogen (1-2) , . 1 -0.0098038985517335 1 0 – – – – 2 0.00042487270143005 4 1.85 – – – – 3 -0.034800214576142 1 7.85 1 0.5 1 0.5 4 -0.13333813013896 2 5.4 1 0.5 1 0.5 5 -0.011993694974627 2 0 0.25 0.5 2.5 0.5 6 0.069243379775168 2 0.75 0 0.5 3 0.5 7 -0.31022508148249 2 2.8 0 0.5 3 0.5 8 0.24495491753226 2 4.45 0 0.5 3 0.5 9 0.22369816716981 3 4.25 0 0.5 3 0.5 Methane + carbon dioxide (1-3) , . 1 -0.10859387354942 1 2.6 – – – – 2 0.080228576727389 2 1.95 – – – – 3 -0.0093303985115717 3 0 – – – – 4 0.040989274005848 1 3.95 1 0.5 1 0.5 5 -0.24338019772494 2 7.95 0.5 0.5 2 0.5 6 0.23855347281124 3 8 0 0.5 3 0.5 Methane + ethane (1-4) , . 1 -0.00080926050298746 3 0.65 – – – – 2 -0.00075381925080059 4 1.55 – – – – 3 -0.041618768891219 1 3.1 1 0.5 1 0.5 4 -0.23452173681569 2 5.9 1 0.5 1 0.5 5 0.14003840584586 2 7.05 1 0.5 1 0.5 6 0.063281744807738 2 3.35 0.875 0.5 1.25 0.5 7 -0.034660425848809 2 1.2 0.75 0.5 1.5 0.5 8 -0.23918747334251 2 5.8 0.5 0.5 2 0.5 9 0.0019855255066891 2 2.7 0 0.5 3 0.5 10 6.1777746171555 3 0.45 0 0.5 3 0.5 11 -6.9575358271105 3 0.55 0 0.5 3 0.5 12 1.0630185306388 3 1.95 0 0.5 3 0.5 Methane + propane (1-5) , . 1 0.013746429958576 3 1.85 – – – – 2 -0.0074425012129552 3 3.95 – – – – 3 -0.0045516600213685 4 0 – – – – 4 -0.0054546603350237 4 1.85 – – – – 5 0.0023682016824471 4 3.85 – – – – 6 0.18007763721438 1 5.25 0.25 0.5 0.75 0.5 7 -0.44773942932486 1 3.85 0.25 0.5 1 0.5 8 0.0193273748882 1 0.2 0 0.5 2 0.5 9 -0.30632197804624 2 6.5 0 0.5 3 0.5 Methane + hydrogen (1-15) , . 1 -0.25157134971934 1 2 – – – – 2 -0.0062203841111983 3 -1 – – – – 3 0.088850315184396 3 1.75 – – – – 4 -0.035592212573239 4 1.4 – – – – Nitrogen + carbon dioxide (2-3) , . 1 0.28661625028399 2 1.85 – – – – 2 -0.10919833861247 3 1.4 – – – – 3 -1.137403208227 1 3.2 0.25 0.5 0.75 0.5 4 0.76580544237358 1 2.5 0.25 0.5 1 0.5 5 0.0042638000926819 1 8 0 0.5 2 0.5 6 0.17673538204534 2 3.75 0 0.5 3 0.5 Nitrogen + ethane (2-4) , . 1 -0.47376518126608 2 0 – – – – 2 0.48961193461001 2 0.05 – – – – 3 -0.0057011062090535 3 0 – – – – 4 -0.1996682004132 1 3.65 1 0.5 1 0.5 5 -0.69411103101723 2 4.9 1 0.5 1 0.5 6 0.69226192739021 2 4.45 0.875 0.5 1.25 0.5 Generalized departure function Shared by the pairs 1-6, 1-7, 4-5, 4-6, 4-7, 5-6, 5-7, 6-7; each pair scales it by its own . , . 1 2.5574776844118 1 1 – – – – 2 -7.9846357136353 1 1.55 – – – – 3 4.7859131465806 1 1.7 – – – – 4 -0.73265392369587 2 0.25 – – – – 5 1.3805471345312 2 1.35 – – – – 6 0.28349603476365 3 0 – – – – 7 -0.49087385940425 3 1.25 – – – – 8 -0.10291888921447 4 0 – – – – 9 0.11836314681968 4 0.7 – – – – 10 5.5527385721943e-5 4 5.4 – – – – Next reading GERG2008 for how to build the model, the mixture\n  equations these parameters enter, and its limitations. Thermodynamic fundamentals for units and amount\n  conventions. References Kunz, O., & Wagner, W. (2012). The GERG-2008 wide-range equation of state for\n  natural gases and other mixtures: an expansion of GERG-2004. Journal of\n  Chemical & Engineering Data , 57(11), 3032-3091.","tags":"","url":"page/usage/eos/gerg2008_parameters.html"},{"title":"PC-SAFT – yaeos","text":"PC-SAFT Theory Hard-chain contribution Chain contribution Dispersion contribution Units Example How init_pcsaft works A second example: saturation-temperature scan Implementation details Notes and recommendations Next reading References PC-SAFT The Perturbed-Chain Statistical Associating Fluid Theory (PC-SAFT) equation of\nstate is available as PCSAFT . It is constructed with init_pcsaft . Use it when a molecular-size/chain description and suitable PC-SAFT parameter\nsets are available, for example for non-associating chain molecules. Rather\nthan treating a molecule as one object with a single covolume, PC-SAFT represents\nit as connected segments. Crowding resists compression, connectivity changes\nthe reference fluid, and dispersion attractions lower the free energy. The name includes “associating”, but this implementation has no association\nterm . It is not a general-purpose water/alcohol model, nor does it add a\nseparate polar contribution. A parameter set fitted for another PC-SAFT variant\nmay rely on terms absent here. The following is a constructor snippet , with parameter arrays defined below: use yaeos , only : pr , PcSaft , init_pcsaft type ( PcSaft ) :: model model = init_pcsaft ( m , sigma , epsilon_k , kij ) Theory PC-SAFT is a residual Helmholtz model based on a perturbation expansion around a\nhard-chain reference fluid. Let be total residual Helmholtz energy, the number of molecules, Boltzmann’s constant, and absolute\ntemperature. Write , with the total\nmole amount and the gas constant. Superscripts hc , hs , and disp mean hard chain, hard sphere, and dispersion, respectively. Then Hard-chain contribution Let be molecular mole fraction, the dimensionless\nsegment count, and its mixture average. Let be the dimensionless same-component hard-sphere contact value.\nThe hard-chain term already combines the hard-sphere reference and connectivity: Do not add a separate chain term again. Here is the\ndimensionless hard-sphere reference per segment , not a second whole-molecule\nHelmholtz contribution. For the size mixture it uses the following mixture\nhard-sphere expression (reducing to Carnahan-Starling for equal diameters).\nThe moments are defined immediately below: Let be molecular number density in Å⁻³, the segment\ndiameter in Å, and the segment energy parameter in K\n(the input epsilon_k ). Define the temperature-dependent effective diameter . The moments are In particular, , not .\nThe packing fraction is dimensionless; the other moments\nhave units Å to the power . The contact function uses effective\ndiameter , not the uncorrected : Chain contribution The chain contribution is included in the hard-chain term through the factors. It accounts for the connectivity of the segments that\nform each molecule. Dispersion contribution The unlike diameter is in Å, the unlike energy parameter is in K, and is a dimensionless binary correction.\nThe combining rules are The dimensionless integrals and and reference compressibility\ncorrection depend on packing fraction and mean chain length. The\nimplemented second-order dispersion expression is The temperature factors and must not be omitted. Define the\ndimensionless polynomial coefficients , from\nthe Gross-Sadowski tables (stored in the source as a_coeffs and b_coeffs ,\neach with three rows). Then with the mean-segment dependence and the same form for . The packing fraction is the third\nmoment , and Although the polynomial coefficients have no explicit temperature argument, the\nintegrals are not temperature-independent at fixed density: uses\nthe temperature-dependent . Dispersion uses , while\nthe packing and contact terms use ; these are different quantities in\nthis implementation. Units The internal number density is in molecules per Å³, and the parameters are (segments per molecule), (segment diameter in Angstrom),\nand (segment energy in K). The binary interaction matrix is dimensionless. The model parameters are: Argument Meaning Units m Number of segments per molecule - sigma Segment diameter Angstrom epsilon_k Segment energy divided by Boltzmann’s constant K kij Optional binary interaction matrix - m , sigma , and epsilon_k are arrays with one entry per component. kij is an optional nc x nc matrix; when it is omitted, all binary interaction\nparameters are set to zero. Use positive m , sigma , and epsilon_k , with consistent parameter provenance. m is an effective segment count and may be noninteger. All arrays and both\naxes of kij must use the same component order. Supply symmetric binary\ncorrections with zero diagonals; do not transfer cubic-EOS kij values to\nPC-SAFT without validation. The public property API still uses mol, total L, K, and bar , not Å³:\nthe code converts number density using Avogadro’s constant and 1 L = 10&#94;27 Å&#94;3 . sigma is the exception to the usual macroscopic unit set;\nit is not in metres or litres. Example The following complete program uses the methane/n-butane parameter set shown\nin Gross and Sadowski (2001). The zero-binary-correction mixture is a starting\npoint, not a fitted prediction for this mixture. It first evaluates pressure\nat a prescribed homogeneous state and then asks a separate solver for a bubble\npoint at the same temperature. At a bubble point n/sum(n) is the liquid\ncomposition and sat%y is the incipient vapor composition. program pcsaft_example use yaeos , only : pr , PcSaft , init_pcsaft use yaeos , only : EquilibriumState , saturation_pressure use , intrinsic :: ieee_arithmetic , only : ieee_is_finite implicit none type ( PcSaft ) :: model type ( EquilibriumState ) :: sat real ( pr ) :: m ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: sigma ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: epsilon_k ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: n ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: T real ( pr ) :: V real ( pr ) :: P ! Methane and n-butane m = [ 1.0000_pr , 2.3316_pr ] sigma = [ 3.7039_pr , 3.7086_pr ] epsilon_k = [ 15 0.03_pr , 22 2.88_pr ] model = init_pcsaft ( m , sigma , epsilon_k ) n = [ 0.4_pr , 0.6_pr ] T = 30 0.0_pr V = 1 0.0_pr ! total L for one mole call model % pressure ( n , V , T , P ) print * , \"Pressure: \" , P , \" bar\" sat = saturation_pressure ( model , n , T , kind = \"bubble\" ) if (. not . ieee_is_finite ( sat % P )) error stop \"Nonfinite bubble pressure\" if ( sat % P <= 0.0_pr ) error stop \"Invalid bubble pressure\" if (. not . all ( ieee_is_finite ( sat % y ))) error stop \"Nonfinite vapor composition\" print * , \"Saturation pressure: \" , sat % P , \" bar\" print * , \"Vapor composition: \" , sat % y end program pcsaft_example At this low-density pressure-evaluation state, compare pressure with the\nideal-gas value sum(n)*R*T/V ( R is exported by yaeos ). Increase volume\nto check approach to the ideal-gas limit. Pressure evaluated at a chosen volume\nis not the saturation pressure, and need not equal sat%P .\nThe finite/positive checks are necessary but insufficient: also check\nnormalization, nonnegative phase compositions, distinct liquid/vapor roots\naway from criticality, and equality of component fugacities. See the saturation guide . How init_pcsaft works PC-SAFT is a residual Helmholtz model, but many yaeos routines also need\ncritical constants and acentric factors. init_pcsaft computes them from the\nPC-SAFT parameters: find_critical_points_all_components calculates the pure-component critical\n   temperature, pressure, and volume from the PC-SAFT equation. Psat_pure evaluates the saturation pressure at . The acentric factor is obtained from the definition . This initialization is more expensive than constructing a cubic model, because\nit requires solving the critical point and a saturation point for each\ncomponent. It is done once per model. These are model-predicted critical constants, not additional experimental\ninputs. The initializer obtains a converged array internally but does not\nreturn it to callers. Check that model%components%Tc , %Pc , and %w are\nfinite and physically plausible before trusting downstream initial guesses.\nUnusual parameter sets may fail during this initialization, before any mixture\nproperty call is made. A second example: saturation-temperature scan program pcsaft_saturation_scan use yaeos , only : pr , PcSaft , init_pcsaft use yaeos , only : EquilibriumState , saturation_temperature use , intrinsic :: ieee_arithmetic , only : ieee_is_finite implicit none type ( PcSaft ) :: model type ( EquilibriumState ) :: sat real ( pr ) :: m ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: sigma ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: epsilon_k ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: n ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: Pvals ( 5 ) integer :: i m = [ 1.0000_pr , 2.3316_pr ] sigma = [ 3.7039_pr , 3.7086_pr ] epsilon_k = [ 15 0.03_pr , 22 2.88_pr ] model = init_pcsaft ( m , sigma , epsilon_k ) n = [ 0.4_pr , 0.6_pr ] Pvals = [ 1.0_pr , 5.0_pr , 1 0.0_pr , 2 0.0_pr , 4 0.0_pr ] do i = 1 , size ( Pvals ) sat = saturation_temperature ( model , n , P = Pvals ( i ), kind = \"bubble\" , t0 = 25 0.0_pr ) if (. not . ieee_is_finite ( sat % T )) error stop \"Nonfinite saturation temperature\" if ( sat % T <= 0.0_pr ) error stop \"Invalid saturation temperature\" print * , \"P =\" , Pvals ( i ), \"bar, Tsat =\" , sat % T , \"K\" end do end program pcsaft_saturation_scan This retained illustration is a scan of bubble temperature, not a phase\nenvelope or a validation dataset. The program is complete and each point is a\nseparate nonlinear solve. A fixed t0 may fail in another pressure range;\ncontinuing from the previous converged point can be more reliable. Implementation details PcSaft extends ArModelTapenade . The residual Helmholtz energy and its\nderivatives are generated with Tapenade, so the model provides the full set of\nfirst and second derivatives required by the phase-equilibrium routines. The model implements: ar , ar_d , ar_b , ar_d_b , and ar_d_d for the Tapenade interface. A specialized volume solver based on Michelsen’s method. get_v0 , which returns the total segment volume. This is the physical lower\n   scale used to initialize the liquid root, not a universal stable-liquid bound. The PC-SAFT contribution contains the hard-sphere, chain, and dispersion terms\nof the Gross-Sadowski formulation. The current implementation does not include\nthe association term, so it should not be used for associating fluids such as\nwater or alcohols without an additional model. Notes and recommendations Use the model-specific units: sigma in Angstrom and epsilon_k in kelvin. Provide kij for mixtures when binary interaction parameters are available.\n   Use the usual symmetric convention with zero diagonals. The segment number m can be a real number; it is not restricted to integers. The initialization calculates critical constants numerically. If a component\n  does not have a stable critical point under PC-SAFT, init_pcsaft will not\n  produce reliable acentric factors. For associating fluids, the association contribution is not implemented in\n  this version. The model is significantly more expensive than a cubic equation of state.\n  For phase-envelope calculations, provide good initial guesses to the\n   saturation and flash routines. Finite positive pressure does not establish phase stability. The packing\nfraction must stay below the hard-sphere singularity at one, and approaching\nthat singularity is not a route to a trustworthy compressed-liquid prediction.\nDistinguish missing physical contributions or unsuitable parameters from\nvolume-root and phase-equilibrium convergence failures. Next reading See the EOS property guide , thermodynamic fundamentals , saturation points , and flash calculations . For a lower-input baseline,\ncompare the cubic models using the same state and composition. References Gross, J., & Sadowski, G. (2001). Perturbed-Chain SAFT: An Equation of State\n  Based on a Perturbation Theory for Chain Molecules. Industrial & Engineering\n  Chemistry Research , 40(4), 1244-1260.","tags":"","url":"page/usage/eos/pc_saft.html"},{"title":"Gibbs Excess Models – yaeos","text":"Gibbs Excess Models What problem does an excess-Gibbs model solve? Activity, activity coefficient, and fugacity Choosing a model Inputs, units, and shared methods Worked example: reading a result Routines and Methods \\ln \\gamma_i Example \\ln \\gamma_i  derivatives Excess enthalpy  (H&#94;E) Example Excess entropy  (S&#94;E) Example Excess heat capacity  (C_{p}&#94;{E}) Example Checks, limits, and next steps References Gibbs Excess Models What problem does an excess-Gibbs model solve? Mixing water with an alcohol does not simply dilute two otherwise unchanged\nliquids: each molecule encounters new neighbors. An excess-Gibbs model describes\nhow these unlike contacts change the chemical potential (the driving force for\ntransfer or separation) relative to an ideal liquid solution. In yaeos , these\nmodels extend GeModel and calculate activity coefficients and excess thermal\nproperties from temperature and amounts. They do not calculate liquid density\nor vapor properties on their own. An excess property compares a real mixture with an ideal solution at the\nsame temperature, pressure, and composition: Unlike excess enthalpy, excess Gibbs energy and excess entropy are not the\ntotal changes on mixing. An ideal solution already has an entropy of mixing: A residual property in an equation of state instead measures departure from\nan ideal gas reference. It is not interchangeable with an excess property. Also,\nthe word “residual” inside UNIQUAC/UNIFAC names just their energetic contribution\nto the excess Gibbs energy, not an ideal-gas departure. The thermodynamics essentials page develops this distinction\nin more depth, including why an excess quantity cannot be added to a residual\none. Activity, activity coefficient, and fugacity With a pure-liquid standard state, the dimensionless activity is , and . The activity coefficient corrects ideal dilution: it is 1 in an ideal solution. A value above 1 raises\nthe chemical potential relative to that ideal solution; below 1 lowers it.\nIt is not a mole fraction and need not lie between 0 and 1. Fugacity is a pressure-like representation of chemical potential. In a\nliquid, ; in a vapor described by an EOS, . Here is a fugacity coefficient , not\nan activity coefficient. Vapor-liquid equilibrium requires equal fugacities,\nso activity coefficients alone are not a complete vapor-liquid calculation:\npure-component reference fugacities and a vapor model are also needed. A\nliquid-liquid calculation with the same standard states can cancel those\nreference terms, but still requires stability and phase-split calculations. Choosing a model Available information / goal Starting point Reliable fitted binary liquid-mixture parameters NRTL or UNIQUAC Molecular structures, but no fitted molecular pair parameters UNIFAC family , provided all required groups and interactions are covered An excess model intended for Huron-Vidal cubic-EOS coupling NRTL-HV , with matching EOS covolumes and fitted parameters Parameterized models can represent the data used to fit them well, but the\nmodel name alone supplies no parameters. Group-contribution models reuse\ninteractions between chemical fragments across molecules; they are predictive\nestimates, not a guarantee of accuracy for a new system. Choose using measured\ndata near your temperature/composition and the intended equilibrium type, not\njust whether a constructor accepts the inputs. Inputs, units, and shared methods n(:) contains component mole amounts , with n >= 0 and sum(n) > 0 .\n  Component order must match all model vectors and matrix indices. A mole-fraction\n  vector is valid as a one-mole basis; mass fractions are not interchangeable. T is absolute temperature in kelvin , never degrees Celsius. ln_activity_coefficient returns natural logarithms ; use exp to get . Optional derivatives are with respect to T or mole amounts,\n  not independently varied mole fractions. The gas constant yaeos__constants uses bar L/(mol K). Thus extensive Ge and He are in bar L , and Se and Cpe are in bar L/K when n is in mol. Multiply by 100 to convert bar L to joules. Divide by sum(n) for\n  molar properties. Scaling every amount by the same factor leaves activity coefficients unchanged\n  and scales extensive properties by that factor. Mole-number derivatives depend\n  on the chosen amount basis (the dlngammadn matrix scales inversely). These implementations have no explicit pressure argument in their excess-property\nmethods. They cannot supply pressure-dependent liquid volumes or replace a full\nEOS at high pressure. Each concrete model supplies excess_gibbs ; the shared\nmethods derive the properties below from it. Because the shared methods are inherited from GeModel , the same calls work\nfor every model on this site. That uniformity is a convenience, not a statement\nthat the models are interchangeable: they differ in how they describe\nnonideality, in how many parameters they need, and in where they are reliable. Worked example: reading a result The complete program below evaluates an ethanol–water mixture with UNIFAC. It is\nworth running once and reading the output carefully, because the numbers are\neasily misread. program excess_properties use yaeos , only : pr , Groups , setup_unifac , UNIFAC implicit none type ( UNIFAC ) :: model integer , parameter :: nc = 2 type ( Groups ) :: molecules ( nc ) real ( pr ) :: n ( nc ) real ( pr ) :: T real ( pr ) :: ln_gamma ( nc ) real ( pr ) :: dln_gamma_dT ( nc ) real ( pr ) :: dln_gamma_dn ( nc , nc ) real ( pr ) :: Ge real ( pr ) :: He real ( pr ) :: Se real ( pr ) :: Cpe T = 29 8.15_pr n = [ 0.5_pr , 0.5_pr ] ! Ethanol, then water: output arrays follow this same order. molecules ( 1 )% groups_ids = [ 1 , 2 , 14 ] molecules ( 1 )% number_of_groups = [ 1 , 1 , 1 ] molecules ( 2 )% groups_ids = [ 16 ] molecules ( 2 )% number_of_groups = [ 1 ] ! setup UNIFAC model model = setup_unifac ( molecules ) ! Calculate ln_gamma and its derivatives call model % ln_activity_coefficient ( n , T , ln_gamma , dln_gamma_dT , dln_gamma_dn ) print * , \"ln_gamma = \" , ln_gamma print * , \"dln_gamma_dT = \" , dln_gamma_dT print * , \"dln_gamma_dn = \" , dln_gamma_dn print * , \"gamma = \" , exp ( ln_gamma ) call model % excess_gibbs ( n , T , Ge = Ge ) call model % excess_enthalpy ( n , T , He = He ) call model % excess_entropy ( n , T , Se = Se ) call model % excess_Cp ( n , T , Cpe ) print * , \"GE, HE [bar L] = \" , Ge , He print * , \"SE, CpE [bar L/K] = \" , Se , Cpe print * , \"GE - (HE - T*SE), near zero = \" , Ge - ( He - T * Se ) end program excess_properties This is a complete program. The first output entry refers to ethanol and the\nsecond to water. Negative ln_gamma is allowed; exp(ln_gamma) must be positive.\nThe final energy identity is a useful consistency check, but does not validate\nthe model against experiments. A positive He means mixing absorbs heat relative\nto ideal-solution enthalpy at that temperature; Cpe is only the excess , not\nthe total heat capacity. To interpret the output, work through it in this order. ln_gamma is a logarithm. exp(ln_gamma) gives the coefficient. A\n   coefficient above 1 means the component is less stable in the mixture than\n   ideal dilution would predict; below 1 means more stable. Neither the\n   logarithm nor the coefficient is a mole fraction, and neither is bounded by 1. Multiply by the mole fraction for an activity. x_i * gamma_i is the\n   dimensionless activity, the quantity that appears in the chemical potential.\n   A large gamma_i for a component with a small x_i may still mean a small\n   activity. The energy outputs are extensive. Ge and He are in bar·L for the\n   whole n vector (1 mol here, so numerically molar); Se and Cpe are in\n   bar·L/K. Multiply by 100 for J and J/K. Divide by sum(n) for molar values.\n   A positive Ge means mixing is less favorable than ideal mixing; a positive He means mixing absorbs heat. dln_gamma_dn is a mole-number derivative , so it scales inversely with\n   the amount basis: multiplying n by ten divides these entries by ten, while ln_gamma itself is unchanged. dln_gamma_dT is unaffected by scaling. The identity check. Ge - (He - T*Se) must vanish at the level of\n   round-off, because . A nonzero value means a bug in the\n   model or in your call, not a physical finding. What this example does not tell you is whether the prediction is accurate.\nThat requires comparing with measured data for the same mixture. Routines and Methods Activity coefficients are calculated from the excess Gibbs energy using the\nfollowing expression: In words: the activity coefficient is the sensitivity of the excess Gibbs energy\nto adding a little more of component i . A mixture whose excess Gibbs energy\nresponds strongly to extra i has a large ln gamma_i . Because G&#94;E is\nextensive and the derivative is taken at fixed amounts of the other\ncomponents, ln gamma_i depends only on composition and temperature, not on how\nmuch material is present. Example The complete program in Worked example shows\nthe call in context. The essential part is: call model % ln_activity_coefficient ( n , T , ln_gamma , dln_gamma_dT , dln_gamma_dn ) Pass n (mole amounts) and T (kelvin). The three outputs are optional; asking\nonly for lngamma is cheaper because the derivative kernels are skipped. derivatives The derivatives of the activity coefficients with respect to the mole numbers: The temperature derivative of the activity coefficients: You may notice that yaeos calculates the derivatives of the .\nIf for some reason you need the derivatives of the activity coefficients, you\nmay find useful the following expressions: The complete program that produces these numbers is in Worked example . Excess enthalpy From the Gibbs-Helmholtz equation [1]: We can calculate the excess enthalpy from the excess Gibbs energy as: The derivatives of the excess enthalpy can be calculated as: Example The following is an extension snippet , not a separate program. Put declarations\nwith the declarations of the complete example, and calls before its end program . real ( pr ) :: HE_value real ( pr ) :: dHE_dT real ( pr ) :: dHE_dn ( nc ) ! Calculate excess enthalpy and its derivatives call model % excess_enthalpy ( n , T , HE_value , dHE_dT , dHE_dn ) print * , \"HE = \" , HE_value print * , \"dHE_dT = \" , dHE_dT print * , \"dHE_dn = \" , dHE_dn Excess entropy Finally, excess entropy can be calculated from the excess Gibbs energy and\nexcess enthalpy as: Replacing : The derivatives of the excess entropy can be calculated as: Example This is another extension snippet ; separate its declarations and executable\nstatements when inserting it in the complete example. real ( pr ) :: SE_value real ( pr ) :: dSE_dT real ( pr ) :: dSE_dn ( nc ) ! Calculate excess entropy and its derivatives call model % excess_entropy ( n , T , SE_value , dSE_dT , dSE_dn ) print * , \"S_E = \" , SE_value print * , \"dSE_dT = \" , dSE_dT print * , \"dSE_dn = \" , dSE_dn Excess heat capacity Excess heat capacity can be calculated as: Example Extension snippet (declaration belongs above the first executable statement): real ( pr ) :: C_p_E ! Calculate excess heat capacity call model % excess_Cp ( n , T , C_p_E ) print * , \"C_p_E = \" , C_p_E Checks, limits, and next steps Before using a phase-equilibrium result, work through the inputs once more.\nConfirm the component order matches the model, that the temperature is inside\nthe range the parameters were fitted for, that the signs and units of the\nparameters are what you think they are, and, for the group-contribution models,\nthat every interaction your mixture needs is actually covered. Scale n and\nconfirm the activity coefficients do not change. In the pure-component limit the\ncoefficient of the present component should approach 1 for a consistently\nreferenced model; a trace solute need not have a coefficient of 1. Never\nevaluate an all-zero amount vector. A large activity coefficient does not by itself prove that a liquid splits.\nWhether a feed is unstable is a separate question, answered by a stability\nanalysis and a phase-equilibrium calculation; see the phase-equilibrium guide . And a converged\nprediction is not an accurate one: compare with measured data before drawing\nconclusions from it. For EOS coupling, continue to the cubic mixing rules . Standard NRTL, UNIQUAC, and\nUNIFAC are not general electrolyte, reacting-mixture, or near-critical models,\nand they supply no volumetric properties on their own. References [1] https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gibbs%E2%80%93Helmholtz_equation","tags":"","url":"page/usage/excessmodels/index.html"},{"title":"NRTL – yaeos","text":"NRTL Theory Parameters Usage Worked example: a liquid-liquid split Notes Common mistakes and next steps References NRTL The Non-Random-Two-Liquid (NRTL) model presented by Renon and Prausnitz is an\nactivity-coefficient model for the excess Gibbs energy of liquid mixtures. It\nuses local compositions to represent the non-randomness of the mixture. Use NRTL when you have binary interaction parameters fitted for your components\nand the property of interest (for example vapor-liquid or liquid-liquid data).\nIt is useful for strongly nonideal liquids, including systems that may demix,\nbut does not discover parameters from chemical names or molecular structure.\nFor structure-based estimates, see the UNIFAC models . Intuitively, the neighbors around a molecule need not have the same composition\nas the bulk liquid: favorable contacts can be overrepresented. NRTL uses a\ndirectional interaction measure and a non-randomness parameter to weight those local contacts. The result is an excess Gibbs energy,\nrelative to ideal liquid mixing, not a residual ideal-gas departure. Its\nderivatives give ; the dimensionless activity is , not alone. See the shared introduction for the distinction from fugacity coefficients. Theory The model averages interaction energies around each component, using local\ncomposition weights rather than just bulk mole fractions. With and , its excess Gibbs energy is with where , , and are the model parameters.\nThe negative sign and the product in are\nessential; this is not or .\nIn yaeos , the matrices a , b , and c store , , and , respectively. The activity coefficient of component follows from Parameters The model is constructed from three matrices: Matrix Meaning a Dimensionless interaction parameters b temperature-dependent interaction parameters [K] c Dimensionless non-randomness parameters T is in kelvin. Store matrix element in a(i,j) , b(i,j) , and c(i,j) ; the column (:,i) enters the local average around component i in\nthe equation above. The two directions generally differ. Keep the same order\nin n and every matrix, and normally set diagonal interaction coefficients to\nzero. Non-randomness parameters are often symmetric, but must follow your fit. Published NRTL tables do not all use this convention. If a source defines with a constant energy difference, convert it\nto b(i,j) = Delta_g(i,j)/R and set a=0 , using a gas constant with matching\nenergy units. Do not enter J/mol directly as a kelvin-valued b , add another\nminus sign without checking the source definition, or reuse UNIQUAC parameters. Usage This complete program illustrates matrix orientation and the ideal-interaction\nlimit. The nonzero numbers are illustrative , not fitted parameters for any\nnamed chemical pair. program nrtl_activity use yaeos , only : pr , NRTL implicit none type ( NRTL ) :: model real ( pr ) :: a ( 2 , 2 ) real ( pr ) :: b ( 2 , 2 ) real ( pr ) :: c ( 2 , 2 ) real ( pr ) :: n ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: T real ( pr ) :: ln_gamma ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: ln_gamma_scaled ( 2 ) a = 0.0_pr b = 0.0_pr b ( 1 , 2 ) = 20 0.0_pr b ( 2 , 1 ) = - 10 0.0_pr c = 0.3_pr model = NRTL ( a , b , c ) n = [ 0.4_pr , 0.6_pr ] T = 30 0.0_pr call model % ln_activity_coefficient ( n , T , ln_gamma ) call model % ln_activity_coefficient ( 1 0.0_pr * n , T , ln_gamma_scaled ) print * , \"ln(gamma), components 1 and 2 = \" , ln_gamma print * , \"gamma = \" , exp ( ln_gamma ) print * , \"Scaling difference, near zero = \" , ln_gamma_scaled - ln_gamma b = 0.0_pr model = NRTL ( a , b , c ) call model % ln_activity_coefficient ( n , T , ln_gamma ) print * , \"Zero-interaction ln(gamma), near zero = \" , ln_gamma end program nrtl_activity exp(ln_gamma) is positive and may exceed 1; negative logarithms are normal.\nScaling all amounts should leave coefficients unchanged. When both a and b vanish, this NRTL model becomes ideal (all coefficients are 1), regardless of c . Fortran reshape fills columns first; explicit off-diagonal assignments\nabove avoid silently transposing directional parameters. Worked example: a liquid-liquid split An activity model earns its keep when it predicts that a mixture separates. The\nfollowing program uses NRTL for a strongly nonideal binary and searches for a\ntwo-liquid split with the library’s stability and flash routines. The parameters here are illustrative : they produce a split, but they are not\na fit to any named chemical pair. program nrtl_lle use yaeos , only : pr , NRTL , EquilibriumState , flash , min_tpd implicit none integer , parameter :: nc = 2 type ( NRTL ) :: model type ( EquilibriumState ) :: result real ( pr ) :: a ( nc , nc ) real ( pr ) :: b ( nc , nc ) real ( pr ) :: c ( nc , nc ) real ( pr ) :: n ( nc ) real ( pr ) :: w ( nc ) real ( pr ) :: T real ( pr ) :: P real ( pr ) :: mintpd integer :: iters a = 0.0_pr b = 0.0_pr c = 0.0_pr a ( 1 , 2 ) = 5.0_pr a ( 2 , 1 ) = 5.0_pr c ( 1 , 2 ) = 0.3_pr c ( 2 , 1 ) = 0.3_pr model = NRTL ( a , b , c ) n = [ 0.3_pr , 0.7_pr ] T = 30 0.0_pr P = 1.0_pr ! Minimum of the reduced tangent-plane distance call min_tpd ( model , n , P = P , T = T , mintpd = mintpd , w = w ) print * , \"mintpd = \" , mintpd print * , \"trial composition = \" , w if ( mintpd < 0.0_pr ) then ! The trial composition gives the initial K-factors result = flash ( model , n , T , k0 = w / n , iters = iters ) print * , \"kind = \" , result % kind print * , \"x = \" , result % x print * , \"y = \" , result % y print * , \"beta = \" , result % beta else print * , \"Feed is stable in this search.\" end if end program nrtl_lle How to read this: min_tpd minimizes a tangent-plane distance measure at fixed T and P . A negative mintpd means the feed is unstable to a small perturbation, i.e.\n  a second phase wants to appear. A positive value means the search found no\n  instability; it is not proof that the feed is globally stable. flash for an activity model solves the isofugacity and mass-balance\n  equations at a specified temperature. It requires the initial K-factors k0 ; without them the routine stops with an error rather than guessing.\n  Use the trial composition from min_tpd for that purpose. result%kind reports the outcome. Only read x , y , and beta when it\n  says \"split\" . The pressure and the two phase volumes are not set for an\n  activity model and must not be read. x and y are the two liquid phases (for an activity model the\n  “heavy”/”light” labels carry no density meaning, and x is not necessarily\n  the larger phase), and beta is the mole fraction of the feed in the y phase. Check the material balance yourself. The same workflow is described in the phase-equilibrium guide ; the flash page covers the activity-model branch in\ndetail. Notes NRTL is a GeModel : it provides activity coefficients and excess thermal\nproperties, but no volumetric properties, and the flash routines solve only\nthe isofugacity and mass-balance equations. Values such as 0.2 or 0.3 are common choices of . They are not\nuniversal constants, and they are not an instruction to replace the value used\nin a parameter fit: the non-randomness parameter is part of the fit, and\nchanging it changes the model’s predictions. The three matrices need not be symmetric, but the diagonal entries are usually\nzero. The diagonal represents a component’s interaction with itself, which\ncarries no information about unlike pairs. Ordinary NRTL and NRTL-HV share the same mathematical form\nwhen all covolumes are equal: with a unit covolume for every component,\nNRTL-HV reproduces NRTL exactly when gji0 = b_matrix * R and gjiT = a_matrix * R . Use this as a numerical cross-check when converting a\nparameter set, not as a claim that the two models are interchangeable for\nunequal covolumes. Common mistakes and next steps Do not extrapolate a good room-temperature binary fit across a large\ntemperature range without checks. The temperature dependence of NRTL is\nstrictly linear in , so it cannot represent a change in interaction\nenthalpy over the range, and the prediction drifts silently. Large values of a+b/T can also overflow the exponential, and an apparently smooth curve can\nstill predict the wrong phase behavior. NRTL does not include ionic\nspeciation, chemical reaction, or density physics by itself. A good sequence of work is: compare activity coefficients and excess enthalpies\nagainst your parameter source using the shared property methods ; then use stability and phase-equilibrium calculations to determine whether a composition is stable, remembering that a single call\nto an activity method is not a flash calculation; and finally compare the\nresult with data. For vapor-liquid equilibrium you also need the liquid\nstandard-state and vapor fugacity information, not just activity coefficients.\nFor a cubic EOS with Huron-Vidal mixing, read NRTL-HV rather\nthan assuming the two parameterizations are interchangeable. References H. Renon and J. M. Prausnitz, “Local compositions in thermodynamic excess\n   functions for liquid mixtures,” AIChE Journal, vol. 14, no. 1, pp. 135-144,\n   1968, doi: 10.1002/aic.690140124.","tags":"","url":"page/usage/excessmodels/nrtl.html"},{"title":"NRTL-HV – yaeos","text":"NRTL-HV When to use it Equation and actual parameter convention Complete property example Cross-check against ordinary NRTL Checks, limits, and next steps Derivatives Compositional derivatives Temperature derivatives Crossed derivative References NRTL-HV The NRTL model modified by Huron and Vidal is a variation of the original\nnon-random two-liquid model. This variation is intended to be used combined with\ncubic Equations of State, using the Huron-Vidal mixing rule.  The major\ndifference with respect to the original NRTL model is that it includes the\nrepulsive parameter of the cubic EoS in the expression for the excess Gibbs\nenergy. This allows the model to be simplified to the classic Van der Waals\n1-fluid quadratic mixing rules when giving special values to the parameters. When to use it Choose this model when your parameter set was developed for a Huron-Vidal\ncubic-EOS formulation. Ordinary NRTL weights neighbors by their\namounts and interaction factors; NRTL-HV additionally weights them by pure-fluid covolumes , which represent excluded-volume size in the cubic EOS.\nThis is not a liquid molar-volume calculation. In yaeos the public type is NRTLHV , while the ordinary type is NRTL . NRTL-HV itself remains a GeModel : its derivatives give activity coefficients\nrelative to an ideal liquid, not fugacity coefficients relative to an ideal gas.\nAn activity is ; a liquid fugacity also requires its pure-liquid\nreference fugacity. Coupling the excess model to a cubic EOS is a separate step\nthrough the mixing rule , not a side effect of calling\nthe constructor. Equation and actual parameter convention The general expression for the excess Gibbs energy is given by: Where the parameters are defined as follows: In this implementation: Here is the already-referenced interaction difference.\nIf a source supplies raw energies , subtract the reference\nbefore constructing this model: the implementation does not subtract a\ndiagonal energy. The non-randomness parameter controls the local\nweighting and is often symmetric, but must match the fitted parameter set. Constructor argument Meaning and units b(:) Pure-component cubic covolumes, L/mol alpha(:,:) Dimensionless non-randomness parameters gji0(:,:) Constant referenced energy differences, bar L/mol gjiT(:,:) Coefficients multiplying T , bar L/(mol K) Thus , with T in kelvin and the library gas constant R in bar L/(mol K). Divide J/mol by 100\nto obtain bar L/mol (and likewise for J/(mol K)). These arrays are not the\ndimensionless a and kelvin-valued b matrices of the ordinary NRTL constructor.\nAll matrices have shape (nc,nc) and need not be symmetric. (:,i) describes\ncontacts in the local average around component i . Use zero diagonal energy\ndifferences and keep component order consistent with n and the covolume vector. Complete property example The following artificial binary example tests the API and its ideal-interaction\nlimit; it is not a parameter set for named chemicals. For EOS work, obtain b from the same pure-component cubic model used for coupling. program nrtlhv_activity use yaeos , only : pr , NRTLHV implicit none type ( NRTLHV ) :: model real ( pr ) :: b ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: alpha ( 2 , 2 ) real ( pr ) :: gji0 ( 2 , 2 ) real ( pr ) :: gjiT ( 2 , 2 ) real ( pr ) :: n ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: T real ( pr ) :: ln_gamma ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: Ge b = [ 0.04_pr , 0.06_pr ] alpha = 0.3_pr gji0 = 0.0_pr gji0 ( 1 , 2 ) = 1 0.0_pr gji0 ( 2 , 1 ) = - 5.0_pr gjiT = 0.0_pr n = [ 0.4_pr , 0.6_pr ] T = 30 0.0_pr model = NRTLHV ( b = b , alpha = alpha , gji0 = gji0 , gjiT = gjiT ) call model % ln_activity_coefficient ( n , T , ln_gamma ) call model % excess_gibbs ( n , T , Ge = Ge ) print * , \"gamma, components 1 and 2 = \" , exp ( ln_gamma ) print * , \"GE [bar L] = \" , Ge gji0 = 0.0_pr model = NRTLHV ( b = b , alpha = alpha , gji0 = gji0 , gjiT = gjiT ) call model % ln_activity_coefficient ( n , T , ln_gamma ) print * , \"Zero-interaction ln(gamma), near zero = \" , ln_gamma end program nrtlhv_activity The output coefficient order follows n . Coefficients must be positive, not\nnecessarily less than 1. With both energy matrices zero, and the\ncoefficients are 1, even when the covolumes differ. This ideal- excess check is\nnot the same as the special parameter choices needed to recover quadratic\nmixing in an EOS. Cross-check against ordinary NRTL When every component has the same covolume, the covolume weighting drops out\nof the local averages and NRTL-HV reduces to ordinary NRTL for the same and . Because the two models use different parameter forms,\nthe conversion is: Ordinary NRTL NRTL-HV equivalent a(i,j) (dimensionless) gjiT(i,j) = a(i,j) * R b(i,j) (K) gji0(i,j) = b(i,j) * R c(i,j) (dimensionless) alpha(i,j) = c(i,j) — b(i) = 1 for every component With those substitutions the two models give identical activity coefficients.\nThis is a useful diagnostic when converting a parameter set: if the results\ndiffer, a unit or a matrix direction is wrong. Checks, limits, and next steps Use positive covolumes and nonnegative mole amounts with a positive total.\nWrong energy units, reversed matrix directions, and subtracting the diagonal\ntwice are particularly damaging mistakes. Equal covolumes reduce the weighting\nto ordinary NRTL for the same and , which offers another\ncross-check. Do not transplant a fitted ordinary-NRTL parameter set into an\nunequal-covolume model and assume unchanged predictions. The special parameter choices that recover the classic van der Waals one-fluid\nquadratic mixing rule inside a cubic EOS (a specific set of diagonal and\noff-diagonal energies, and zero non-randomness) are implemented in the Huron-Vidal mixing rule , not by this model object. No density, vapor phase, ionic chemistry, or pressure-dependent excess properties\nare supplied by this standalone model. Validate the fitted temperature range,\nthen continue to shared property methods and cubic mixing rules . The derivative details below are\noptional background; application code should call the model methods. Derivatives In this section we present the first and second order derivatives of the excess\nGibbs energy. To simplify the expressions we will define the following\nparameters: With this definitions, the excess Gibbs energy can be rewritten as: Compositional derivatives The first order derivatives of the excess Gibbs energy with respect to the\nnumber of moles of component are given by: Which can be rewritten in terms of the defined parameters as: The second order derivatives of the excess Gibbs energy with respect to the number of moles of components and are given by: Temperature derivatives To simplify the equations, we present the derivatives of each variable as for first order derivatives wrt to temperature and for\nsecond order derivatives. First derivative Second derivative Crossed derivative References Huron, M.-J., and Vidal, J. (1979). New mixing rules in simple equations of\n   state for representing vapour-liquid equilibria of strongly non-ideal\n   mixtures. Fluid Phase Equilibria, 3, 255–271.","tags":"","url":"page/usage/excessmodels/nrtl-hv.html"},{"title":"UNIQUAC – yaeos","text":"UNIQUAC Physical meaning and equation Constructor inputs and temperature convention Derivative reference Temperature derivatives Cross temperature-compositional derivative Compositional derivatives Examples Adding temperature-dependent parameters Checks, common errors, and limits References UNIQUAC UNIQUAC ( uni versal qua si c hemical) is an excess-Gibbs model for\nnonideal liquid mixtures. It separates two reasons why real mixing differs from\nideal dilution: molecules occupy different amounts of space, and unlike\nneighbors interact differently from like neighbors. Use it when you have\nconsistent molecular size/area parameters and fitted directional interaction\nparameters for your components. It does not infer these from chemical names. Physical meaning and equation The first two terms below are combinatorial : they account for molecular size\nand shape. The final residual term describes energetic contacts. Here\n“residual” is a part of the excess model, not an EOS departure from an ideal gas.\nThe complete is relative to an ideal liquid solution; it is not the total\nGibbs energy of mixing. Differentiation gives , where activity\nis . Fugacity also needs a reference fugacity, as explained in\nthe shared introduction . With the usual coordination number , UNIQUAC adds the three contributions: The mole fraction , volume fraction , and surface fraction use the same amounts but different weights: The dimensionless molecular parameters and represent relative\nvolume and surface area, respectively. They are not measured volumes in L/mol\nor areas in square metres. The contact factor weights how the\nsurface of component l interacts with component k ; matrix direction matters. Two conventions are worth stating explicitly, because both are easy to get\nwrong when transcribing a published table. Indices. This is the molecular form of UNIQUAC: the sums run over\n  components, not over functional groups. r_k and q_k belong to whole\n  molecules, and tau(l,k) couples component l to component k . The\n  interaction matrices are therefore (nc,nc) , with nc the number of\n  components. Reference state. The residual term uses a pure-component reference, so\n  the diagonal coefficients are normally zero: tau(k,k) = 1 for a pure\n  component, and the excess Gibbs energy of a pure component vanishes. Off-\n  diagonal coefficients are directional and generally not symmetric. Constructor inputs and temperature convention Use setup_uniquac(qs, rs, aij, bij, cij, dij, eij) , preferably with keywords.\nThe first array is area ( qs ) and the second is volume ( rs ). Both have\nlength nc , should be positive, and must use the same component order as n .\nEach optional interaction array has shape (nc,nc) ; omitted arrays are zero. Coefficient in ln(tau) Units with T in kelvin aij Dimensionless bij/T bij in K cij*log(T) Dimensionless cij ; logarithm uses the numerical kelvin value, equivalent to ln(T/1 K) dij*T dij in 1/K eij*T**2 eij in 1/K² These are coefficients of , not of . In particular,\nfor a constant tabulated in kelvin, pass bij(i,j) = -Delta_U(i,j)/R ; the minus sign belongs in the conversion. Normally\nall diagonal coefficients are zero so . Off-diagonal coefficients\nare directional and generally not symmetric; tau(l,k) enters the sum for\ncomponent k . Match your source’s definition before deciding to transpose it.\nDo not reuse NRTL coefficients simply because some arguments have similar names. The runnable example is below the optional derivative reference. For a\nstructure-based model without molecular binary fits, see UNIFAC , which uses a related physical decomposition\nbut different parameters. Derivative reference The methods evaluate derivatives for you. The rewritten expressions below avoid\nratios such as 0/0 in when a component amount is zero, as long\nas the total amount and weighted sums remain positive. Some of the model’s terms can be simplified to reduce the complexity of the\nderivatives. This also allows the model to be evaluated at mole vectors n where some of the amounts are zero. Being the total number of moles in the system. The expression for the\nExcess Gibbs free energy can be rewritten as: Temperature derivatives : Cross temperature-compositional derivative Compositional derivatives : derivatives are not really needed, but we also provide them. : : Differentiating each term of the first compositional derivative respect to we get: Examples Example from: Gmehling et al. (2012) [2] An example of a mixture of water–ethanol–benzene at 298.15 K with\nconstant [K]: Water Ethanol Benzene 0 526.02 309.64 −318.06 0 −91.532 1325.1 302.57 0 Read that table as . Because the model expects the\ncoefficients of , and , a constant\nenergy difference becomes a coefficient of , namely bij(l,k) = -Delta_U(l,k)/R . That is exactly the minus sign in the code\nbelow. It is the single most common transcription error: entering the table\nvalues directly produces a model with the wrong sign of interaction. program uniquac_activity use yaeos , only : pr , setup_uniquac , UNIQUAC implicit none integer , parameter :: nc = 3 real ( pr ) :: rs ( nc ) real ( pr ) :: qs ( nc ) real ( pr ) :: b ( nc , nc ) real ( pr ) :: n ( nc ) real ( pr ) :: ln_gammas ( nc ) real ( pr ) :: T real ( pr ) :: Ge type ( UNIQUAC ) :: model rs = [ 0.92_pr , 2.1055_pr , 3.1878_pr ] qs = [ 1.4_pr , 1.972_pr , 2.4_pr ] T = 29 8.15_pr ! Calculate bij from DUij. We need -DU/R to get bij b ( 1 ,:) = [ 0.0_pr , - 52 6.02_pr , - 30 9.64_pr ] b ( 2 ,:) = [ 31 8.06_pr , 0.0_pr , 9 1.532_pr ] b ( 3 ,:) = [ - 132 5.1_pr , - 30 2.57_pr , 0.0_pr ] model = setup_uniquac ( qs = qs , rs = rs , bij = b ) n = [ 2.0_pr , 2.0_pr , 8.0_pr ] call model % ln_activity_coefficient ( n , T , ln_gammas ) call model % excess_gibbs ( n , T , Ge = Ge ) print * , \"gamma: water, ethanol, benzene = \" , exp ( ln_gammas ) print * , \"GE per mole [bar L/mol] = \" , Ge / sum ( n ) print * , \"Composition = \" , n / sum ( n ) end program uniquac_activity This is a complete program. Table rows and explicit b(i,:) assignments use the\nsame water–ethanol–benzene order. Explicit rows avoid the column-major ordering\ntrap of reshape . The amounts total 12 mol, so the composition is not the raw n vector. Coefficients are dimensionless; they may be above or below 1, and\nthe output of ln_activity_coefficient must be exponentiated before interpreting\nit as gamma . No claim of a stable, single liquid follows from this property\nevaluation of a trial composition. The printed coefficients show how differently the components respond: water’s\ncoefficient is far above 1 while ethanol’s is below 1, and benzene sits near\nthe middle. Comparing these numbers with the values reported for the same\nsystem in the source reference is the most direct check that your parameter\ntable and sign convention were transcribed correctly. A sign error in bij reverses this pattern, so it shows up immediately here. Adding temperature-dependent parameters The five optional matrices allow a richer temperature dependence. Passing dij or eij adds a linear or quadratic term to , and cij adds a\nlogarithmic term. For example, to give one pair a temperature-dependent\ninteraction, add to the program above: real ( pr ) :: dij ( nc , nc ) real ( pr ) :: eij ( nc , nc ) dij = 0.0_pr eij = 0.0_pr dij ( 1 , 2 ) = 1.0e-3_pr model = setup_uniquac ( qs = qs , rs = rs , bij = b , dij = dij , eij = eij ) The units follow the table in Constructor inputs : dij in 1/K and eij in 1/K². aij adds a constant offset to .\nUse these terms only when your parameter source provides them; they are not\ndefaults that improve accuracy by themselves. Checks, common errors, and limits The most consequential mistake with UNIQUAC is a unit or sign error in the\ninteraction parameters, because the model still returns a plausible-looking\nnumber. Use kelvin, never Celsius. If your energy table is in J/mol, convert\nit with a matching gas constant before setting bij in kelvin, and remember the\nminus sign described earlier. Do not swap qs and rs : changing molecular\ngeometry is not equivalent to changing binary interaction energies, and the\ntwo errors are easy to make because the argument order is area first. A useful sanity check is the ideal limit. Unlike NRTL, setting the\ninteraction matrices to zero does not generally make UNIQUAC ideal, because\nthe combinatorial contribution survives: different-sized molecules still\noccupy different amounts of space. Identical r and q for every component\ntogether with zero interaction coefficients does give the ideal result, and is\nworth checking once when you first set up a model. Two further checks apply to every variant. Scaling all amounts must leave the\nactivity coefficients unchanged. At a pure composition, the present\ncomponent’s coefficient approaches 1 for properly referenced parameters;\ncoefficients of absent solutes need not approach 1, because a solute at\ninfinite dilution has its own limiting value. Finally, a fitted temperature dependence is not a license for extrapolation.\nVery large exponents can overflow, and the model has no general electrolyte or\nnear-critical treatment and supplies no density or vapor properties. Next, evaluate excess enthalpy and other shared properties and\ncompare with your parameter source. For phase splits, use stability and equilibrium methods , not the\nsign of Ge or the size of a single coefficient alone. References Maurer, G., & Prausnitz, J. M. (1978). On the derivation and extension of\n   the UNIQUAC equation. Fluid Phase Equilibria, 2(2), 91-99. Gmehling, Jurgen, Barbel Kolbe, Michael Kleiber, and Jurgen Rarey. Chemical\n   Thermodynamics for Process Simulation. 1st edition. Weinheim: Wiley-VCH,\n   2012. Caleb Bell and Contributors (2016-2024). Thermo: Chemical properties\n   component of Chemical Engineering Design Library (ChEDL)\n   https://github.com/CalebBell/thermo.","tags":"","url":"page/usage/excessmodels/uniquac.html"},{"title":"UNIFAC Models – yaeos","text":"UNIFAC Models Introduction Choosing a variant The physical picture Preparing inputs safely Subgroup numbers are variant-specific Combinatorial term derivatives Flory-Huggins Staverman-Guggenheim Fredenslund et al. (UNIFAC) Residual terms derivatives The compositional derivatives: The temperature derivatives: Temperature-compositional cross derivative: Examples Calculating activity coefficients Worked example: the same mixture through two variants Worked example: a liquid-liquid split Checks, limitations, and next steps References UNIFAC Models Introduction UNIFAC predicts liquid nonideality from a molecule’s functional groups rather\nthan a separate fitted interaction for every pair of whole molecules. This is\nuseful for screening solvents or estimating activity coefficients when binary\nmixture data are scarce. You supply a chemical decomposition; the constructor\ncombines it with a built-in table of group sizes, areas, and interactions. It is not a molecular simulator and does not use a chemical name or atom list\ndirectly. Molecules with the same subgroup counts are indistinguishable to a\ngiven UNIFAC variant, even if their detailed structures differ. A validated NRTL or UNIQUAC fit may be preferable for a\nwell-characterized binary system; UNIFAC trades that specificity for transfer\nof group parameters between compounds. Choosing a variant Variant Constructor returning type(UNIFAC) Important difference Original liquid-vapor setup_unifac(molecules) Original combinatorial term and single-coefficient group interaction law Dortmund modified setup_dortmund(molecules) Modified size term and temperature-dependent interaction fit PSRK UNIFAC setup_psrk(molecules) Group model/parameter set developed for PSRK EOS use; not itself a full EOS The shared Fortran type does not make these models interchangeable. Select\nthe matching group table, interaction set, and temperature function together.\nChanging only d or copying group IDs from another variant is not a valid model\nconversion. Published tables below are useful references; the parameter data\nbundled with your installed version determine what is actually available. Each UNIFAC model is differentiated by the list of available functional groups,\nthe interaction parameters between functional groups, the value of the exponent in the combinatorial term, and the function of temperature used in the\nresidual term. Check the section of each model for more specific details of\neach model. Let’s begin: UNIFAC (UNIQUAC Functional-group Activity Coefficients) is an Excess Gibbs\nfree energy model used to estimate activity coefficients in non-ideal mixtures.\nIt is particularly useful for predicting the phase behavior of chemical\nmixtures, including liquid-liquid equilibrium (LLE) and vapor-liquid\nequilibrium (VLE). In this model the Excess Gibbs free energy is calculated\nfrom the contribution of a combinatorial term and a residual term. Being: Combinatorial: Accounts for the size and shape of the molecules. Residual: Accounts for the energy interactions between different functional groups. “Residual” here names a contribution to excess Gibbs energy relative to an\nideal liquid, not a residual departure from an ideal gas as in an EOS.\nDifferentiating the sum gives . The dimensionless activity is , and converting to liquid fugacity requires a pure-liquid\nreference fugacity. See Gibbs Excess Models for units, reference\nstates, and why a liquid activity model alone is not a vapor-liquid flash model. Each substance of a mixture modeled with UNIFAC must be represented by a\nlist a functional groups and other list with the ocurrence of each functional\ngroup on the substance. The physical picture Two ideas explain almost everything about UNIFAC results. Size and shape. A big molecule dilutes itself more than a small one, and a\ncompact molecule presents a different surface than a floppy one. The\ncombinatorial term handles this with the two numbers (relative volume)\nand (relative surface area), which are sums of subgroup contributions .\nEthanol and 1-propanol differ in a single CH2 group, so their and differ by that group’s contributions, and their residual behavior differs only\nthrough the groups they contain. Energy of unlike contacts. Whether an OH group finds water or ethanol\nmolecules around it changes its chemical potential. The residual term handles\nthis with group-group interactions, and it is where the “residual” name comes\nfrom. Because interactions are stored between groups rather than molecules,\ninformation transfers: knowing that water likes ethanol’s OH group also tells\nyou something about water with any other alcohol. The practical consequence is that UNIFAC can rank or screen solvents without\nany mixture data, but its accuracy is inherited from the fitted group\ninteractions. A group pair that was never fitted contributes nothing useful,\nand no error message will tell you that. Preparing inputs safely Choose the variant before identifying fragments. A subgroup identifies a\n   specific fragment (for example CH3); a main group collects subgroups that\n   share interaction parameters. Pass subgroup IDs, not main-group IDs. For each component, set molecules(i)%groups_ids and molecules(i)%number_of_groups to integer arrays of equal length. Counts are\n   positive occurrences per molecule , not amounts in the mixture. List each\n   subgroup once with its total count and do not double-count atoms in compound\n   fragments (for example methanol has a dedicated CH3OH group). Check coverage of all required directional main-group interactions, not only\n   whether each subgroup exists. A missing interaction is not evidence of an\n   ideal or zero-energy contact. The constructors’ dimensional checks cannot\n   establish scientific coverage or suitability for your mixture. Construct the model once, then vary n(:) (mol) and T (K). Component order\n   in n follows molecules . Require nonnegative amounts and a positive total.\n   Do not substitute mass fractions or Celsius temperatures. Subgroup numbers are variant-specific The same integer does not mean the same fragment in different variants. In the\noriginal UNIFAC set, subgroup 118 is the sulfone fragment (CH2)2SU ; in the\nPSRK set, subgroup 118 is methane CH4 . Passing a number from one variant’s\ntable into another variant’s constructor therefore builds a model for a\ndifferent chemistry, and the program will not complain: the lookup falls back\nto whatever the parameter table happens to hold, and the results are silently\nwrong. Two practical consequences: Always take subgroup numbers from the table of the variant you are\n  constructing. Each variant page in this section has its own table. If a result looks absurd (a coefficient of order 10&#94;-700, or a coefficient\n  that jumps by orders of magnitude between two similar mixtures), suspect a\n  misidentified or out-of-table subgroup before suspecting the physics. Molecular size and area parameters are sums of subgroup contributions, and , where is the count of subgroup k in component i . R_k and Q_k are\ndimensionless relative volumes and areas, not physical SI measurements. The complete example below can be used without reading the derivative reference.\nThe reference explains how the implementation obtains thermal and compositional\nderivatives consistently from the same excess-energy function. Combinatorial term derivatives Combinatorial term has two parameters and that could be modified.\nThe parameter is always set to 10, and the parameter is set to 1\nfor the Classic Liquid-Vapor UNIFAC model and the PSRK-UNIFAC model, and 3/4\nfor the Dortmund-UNIFAC model. Both could be changed (they are attributes of\nthe UNIFAC objects ( z and d respectively)), but changing them creates a\ncustom model: it does not transfer the published validation of another variant. Calculate the UNIFAC combinatorial term of reduced Gibbs excess energy.\nThe subroutine uses the Flory-Huggins and Staverman-Guggenheim\ncombinatory terms as follows: Flory-Huggins Staverman-Guggenheim Fredenslund et al. (UNIFAC) The complete combinatorial term is given by the sum of the Flory-Huggins and\nStaverman-Guggenheim terms: Residual terms derivatives Evaluate the UNIFAC residual term. Check the temperature function .\nThis temperature function is different for each UNIFAC model. The residual\nGibbs excess energy and its derivatives are evaluated as: With: In the UNIFAC model, the values are calculated assuming\nthat the molecule “i” is pure, hence only the subgroups of the molecule\n“i” must be considered for the calculation. On the other hand, for the values, all the system’s subgroups are considered. The compositional derivatives: With: The temperature derivatives: With: Temperature-compositional cross derivative: With: Examples On each specific UNIFAC model section we will give more examples of each\nspecific model. Here we leave a simple example for the classic Liquid-Vapor\nUNIFAC model. Calculating activity coefficients We can instantiate a UNIFAC model with a mixture ethanol-water and evaluate\nthe logarithm of activity coefficients of the model for a 0.5 mole fraction of\neach, and a temperature of 298.0 K. program unifac_activity use yaeos , only : pr , Groups , UNIFAC , setup_unifac implicit none ! Variables declarations type ( UNIFAC ) :: model type ( Groups ) :: molecules ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: ln_gammas ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: n ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: T ! Variables instances ! Ethanol definition [CH3, CH2, OH] molecules ( 1 )% groups_ids = [ 1 , 2 , 14 ] ! Subgroups ids molecules ( 1 )% number_of_groups = [ 1 , 1 , 1 ] ! Subgroups occurrences ! Water definition [H2O] molecules ( 2 )% groups_ids = [ 16 ] molecules ( 2 )% number_of_groups = [ 1 ] ! Model setup model = setup_unifac ( molecules ) ! Calculate ln_gammas n = [ 0.5_pr , 0.5_pr ] T = 29 8.0_pr call model % ln_activity_coefficient ( n , T , ln_gammas ) print * , \"ln(gamma): ethanol, water = \" , ln_gammas print * , \"gamma = \" , exp ( ln_gammas ) print * , \"activity = \" , n / sum ( n ) * exp ( ln_gammas ) end program unifac_activity This is a complete program. Each output array is ordered ethanol, then water. ln_gammas are natural logarithms, exp(ln_gammas) are coefficients, and the\nfinal line multiplies by the mole fractions to obtain activities. Positive\nlogarithms mean coefficients greater than 1; neither coefficients nor activities\nshould be interpreted as percentages. Worked example: the same mixture through two variants Because the three variants share the same Fortran type, you can compare them\nwith one extra program. This is a good way to see what the “variant” choice\nactually changes. program unifac_variants use yaeos , only : pr , Groups , UNIFAC use yaeos , only : setup_unifac , setup_dortmund , setup_psrk implicit none type ( UNIFAC ) :: model_lv type ( UNIFAC ) :: model_do type ( UNIFAC ) :: model_ps type ( Groups ) :: molecules ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: ln_lv ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: ln_do ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: ln_ps ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: n ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: T molecules ( 1 )% groups_ids = [ 1 , 2 , 14 ] molecules ( 1 )% number_of_groups = [ 1 , 1 , 1 ] molecules ( 2 )% groups_ids = [ 16 ] molecules ( 2 )% number_of_groups = [ 1 ] n = [ 0.5_pr , 0.5_pr ] T = 29 8.0_pr model_lv = setup_unifac ( molecules ) model_do = setup_dortmund ( molecules ) model_ps = setup_psrk ( molecules ) call model_lv % ln_activity_coefficient ( n , T , ln_lv ) call model_do % ln_activity_coefficient ( n , T , ln_do ) call model_ps % ln_activity_coefficient ( n , T , ln_ps ) print * , \"original UNIFAC ln(gamma) = \" , ln_lv print * , \"Dortmund     ln(gamma) = \" , ln_do print * , \"PSRK         ln(gamma) = \" , ln_ps end program unifac_variants The Dortmund answer differs from the other two. That is expected: each variant\nhas its own fitted group interactions and its own geometry, so in general the\nnumbers are independent estimates rather than one number computed several ways.\nFor many ordinary mixtures the PSRK parameter set reuses the same size\nparameters and a values as the original model for the common main groups;\nfor this ethanol-water example the original and PSRK results coincide, while\nthe Dortmund set, with its modified size term and fitted interactions, differs.\nA visible difference is a reminder that these are predictions from different\ndata; it is not a bug. Worked example: a liquid-liquid split Activity models are usually used through the phase-equilibrium routines rather\nthan directly. The program below uses UNIFAC to find a two-liquid split of a\nternary feed, which is the classic use case for a group-contribution model:\nscreening which components separate without binary-specific fits. program unifac_lle use yaeos , only : pr , Groups , UNIFAC , setup_unifac use yaeos , only : EquilibriumState , flash , min_tpd implicit none integer , parameter :: nc = 3 type ( UNIFAC ) :: model type ( Groups ) :: molecules ( nc ) type ( EquilibriumState ) :: result real ( pr ) :: n ( nc ) real ( pr ) :: w ( nc ) real ( pr ) :: T real ( pr ) :: P real ( pr ) :: mintpd integer :: iters molecules ( 1 )% groups_ids = [ 1 , 42 ] molecules ( 1 )% number_of_groups = [ 1 , 1 ] molecules ( 2 )% groups_ids = [ 1 , 2 ] molecules ( 2 )% number_of_groups = [ 2 , 4 ] molecules ( 3 )% groups_ids = [ 16 ] molecules ( 3 )% number_of_groups = [ 1 ] model = setup_unifac ( molecules ) n = [ 0.2_pr , 0.7_pr , 0.1_pr ] T = 25 0.0_pr P = 1.0_pr call min_tpd ( model , n , P = P , T = T , mintpd = mintpd , w = w ) print * , \"mintpd = \" , mintpd if ( mintpd < 0.0_pr ) then result = flash ( model , n , T , k0 = w / n , iters = iters ) print * , \"kind = \" , result % kind print * , \"x = \" , result % x print * , \"y = \" , result % y print * , \"beta = \" , result % beta else print * , \"Feed is stable in this search.\" end if end program unifac_lle min_tpd looks for an instability of the feed; a negative value means the\nmixture wants to split. flash then solves the isofugacity and mass-balance\nequations at the given temperature, starting from the trial composition. For an\nactivity model it requires the initial K-factors k0 and cannot accept a\npressure or volume specification. Check result%kind before using x , y ,\nand beta ; the pressure and phase volumes are not set for a GeModel result. The flash page describes this workflow in\nmore detail, including how to initialize from a previous nearby calculation\nwhen building tables of phase equilibria. Checks, limitations, and next steps A few quick checks catch most mistakes before they become wrong results. Check the amount scaling. Multiply every amount by a common factor and\nconfirm that the activity coefficients do not change, while the extensive Ge , He , and Se scale with that factor. A model that fails this test has\na normalization problem. Check the pure-component limit. Approach a pure composition and confirm\nthat the coefficient of the present component tends to 1. This does not\nrequire a trace solute’s coefficient to approach 1: a solute at infinite\ndilution has its own limiting coefficient. The pure-group reference subtraction\nin the residual term enforces this standard-state convention, and it matters\neven for molecules made of several groups. Check the decomposition before the physics. When results look wrong, look\nat the group assignment first. A wrong OH subgroup, an omitted ring-specific\nfragment, or a double-counted compound fragment changes both the geometric and\nthe energetic contribution at once, so there is no way to detect it from the\noutput alone. Know the limits. Prediction quality depends on the fitted coverage of the\ngroups involved and on the temperature range of that fit. More temperature\ncoefficients do not guarantee reliable extrapolation. The standard variants are\nnot general electrolyte or speciation models, and neither liquid density nor\nvapor properties follow from an isolated UNIFAC object. Continue with the variant’s subgroup tables and examples, then use the shared excess-property methods for thermal properties and\nderivatives. Compare with experimental data before drawing process-design or\nphase-split conclusions. A trial liquid’s coefficients alone do not establish\nits stability; see phase equilibrium . References Dortmund Data Bank Software & Separation Technology Fredenslund, A., Jones, R. L., & Prausnitz, J. M. (1975). Group‐contribution\nestimation of activity coefficients in nonideal liquid mixtures. AIChE Journal,\n21(6), 1086–1099. https://doi.org/10.1002/aic.690210607 Skjold-Jorgensen, S., Kolbe, B., Gmehling, J., & Rasmussen, P. (1979).\nVapor-Liquid Equilibria by UNIFAC Group Contribution. Revision and Extension.\nIndustrial & Engineering Chemistry Process Design and Development, 18(4),\n714–722. https://doi.org/10.1021/i260072a024 Gmehling, J., Rasmussen, P., & Fredenslund, A. (1982). Vapor-liquid\nequilibriums by UNIFAC group contribution. Revision and extension. 2.\nIndustrial & Engineering Chemistry Process Design and Development, 21(1),\n118–127. https://doi.org/10.1021/i200016a021 Macedo, E. A., Weidlich, U., Gmehling, J., & Rasmussen, P. (1983).\nVapor-liquid equilibriums by UNIFAC group contribution. Revision and extension. 3.\nIndustrial & Engineering Chemistry Process Design and Development, 22(4),\n676–678. https://doi.org/10.1021/i200023a023 Tiegs, D., Rasmussen, P., Gmehling, J., & Fredenslund, A. (1987).\nVapor-liquid equilibria by UNIFAC group contribution. 4. Revision and\nextension. Industrial & Engineering Chemistry Research, 26(1), 159–161. https://doi.org/10.1021/ie00061a030 Hansen, H. K., Rasmussen, P., Fredenslund, A., Schiller, M., & Gmehling, J.\n(1991). Vapor-liquid equilibria by UNIFAC group contribution. 5. Revision and\nextension. Industrial & Engineering Chemistry Research, 30 (10), 2352–2355. https://doi.org/10.1021/ie00058a017 Wittig, R., Lohmann, J., & Gmehling, J. (2003). Vapor−Liquid Equilibria by\nUNIFAC Group Contribution. 6. Revision and Extension. Industrial & Engineering\nChemistry Research, 42(1), 183–188. https://doi.org/10.1021/ie020506l SINTEF - Thermopack","tags":"","url":"page/usage/excessmodels/unifac_models/index.html"},{"title":"UNIFAC-LV – yaeos","text":"Liquid-Vapor UNIFAC Model description Subgroups list Using ugropy to retrieve UNIFAC subgroups Examples Calculating activity coefficients Checks, common errors, and next steps References Liquid-Vapor UNIFAC Model description Choose original UNIFAC for a structure-based estimate of liquid activity\ncoefficients when the original group’s interaction table covers your chemistry.\nIt was developed for liquid-vapor data: do not assume that availability of a\nparameter automatically establishes accurate liquid-liquid equilibria. For a\nwell-measured binary, a validated molecular fit may be better; see the model selection guide . Like UNIQUAC , it combines a size/shape term with an energetic\nterm. Unlike a molecular UNIQUAC fit, it transfers interactions between\nfunctional groups. The prediction is an excess property relative to ideal\nliquid mixing, not an ideal-gas residual property. Its coefficients correct\nactivity, , and are not vapor fugacity coefficients. See\nthe family overview for reference states and input preparation. This is the original and classic Liquid-Vapor UNIFAC model. In this model, the\nparameters are defined as: The temperature function is defined with a single temperature \nconstant coefficient as follows: Here T is absolute temperature in K, is in K, and is dimensionless. A larger positive coefficient suppresses that\ndirectional contact factor at fixed temperature. In general .\nThese are group interaction coefficients, not ordinary NRTL a coefficients\nor molecular UNIQUAC coefficients. setup_unifac supplies them from its default\nparameter table; you normally provide only subgroup IDs and counts. The single-coefficient law means the original model has no explicit\ntemperature dependence beyond . That is a real limitation: a fit\nobtained at one temperature is used at another with no correction for how the\ninteraction enthalpy changes with temperature. If you need temperature\ndependence over a wide range, or accurate excess enthalpies, compare with Dortmund , whose interaction law has fitted linear and\nquadratic temperature terms. Subgroups list The list of the functional groups and its interaction parameters could be \naccessed on the DDBST web page: https://www.ddbst.com/published-parameters-unifac.html We reproduce here the list of functional groups. To instantiate a UNIFAC model\nyou must define which functional groups are used in a molecule by the Subgroup\nNumber column. The main-group labels explain shared interaction parameters; they are not the IDs to enter. R and Q are dimensionless relative size and area. This\npublished reference table is retained for convenience; verify availability and\ninteraction coverage in the installed library rather than assuming the web\ntable and the bundled parameter revision are identical. These numbers belong to the original UNIFAC variant only. Other variants\nreuse some of the same integers for different fragments, so do not copy a\nsubgroup number from the Dortmund or PSRK tables\ninto this model. Subgroup number Subgroup Name Maingroup R Q 1 CH3 [1]CH2 0.9011 0.848 2 CH2 [1]CH2 0.6744 0.54 3 CH [1]CH2 0.4469 0.228 4 C [1]CH2 0.2195 0 5 CH2=CH [2]C=C 1.3454 1.176 6 CH=CH [2]C=C 1.1167 0.867 7 CH2=C [2]C=C 1.1173 0.988 8 CH=C [2]C=C 0.8886 0.676 9 ACH [3]ACH 0.5313 0.4 10 AC [3]ACH 0.3652 0.12 11 ACCH3 [4]ACCH2 1.2663 0.968 12 ACCH2 [4]ACCH2 1.0396 0.66 13 ACCH [4]ACCH2 0.8121 0.348 14 OH [5]OH 1 1.2 15 CH3OH [6]CH3OH 1.4311 1.432 16 H2O [7]H2O 0.92 1.4 17 ACOH [8]ACOH 0.8952 0.68 18 CH3CO [9]CH2CO 1.6724 1.488 19 CH2CO [9]CH2CO 1.4457 1.18 20 HCO [10]HCO 0.998 0.948 21 CH3COO [11]CCOO 1.9031 1.728 22 CH2COO [11]CCOO 1.6764 1.42 23 HCOO [12]HCOO 1.242 1.188 24 CH3O [13]CH2O 1.145 1.088 25 CH2O [13]CH2O 0.9183 0.78 26 CHO [13]CH2O 0.6908 0.468 27 THF [13]CH2O 0.9183 1.1 28 CH3NH2 [14]CNH2 1.5959 1.544 29 CH2NH2 [14]CNH2 1.3692 1.236 30 CHNH2 [14]CNH2 1.1417 0.924 31 CH3NH [15]CNH 1.4337 1.244 32 CH2NH [15]CNH 1.207 0.936 33 CHNH [15]CNH 0.9795 0.624 34 CH3N [16] (C)3N 1.1865 0.94 35 CH2N [16] (C)3N 0.9597 0.632 36 ACNH2 [17]ACNH2 1.06 0.816 37 C5H5N [18]PYRIDINE 2.9993 2.113 38 C5H4N [18]PYRIDINE 2.8332 1.833 39 C5H3N [18]PYRIDINE 2.667 1.553 40 CH3CN [19]CCN 1.8701 1.724 41 CH2CN [19]CCN 1.6434 1.416 42 COOH [20]COOH 1.3013 1.224 43 HCOOH [20]COOH 1.528 1.532 44 CH2CL [21]CCL 1.4654 1.264 45 CHCL [21]CCL 1.238 0.952 46 CCL [21]CCL 1.0106 0.724 47 CH2CL2 [22]CCL2 2.2564 1.988 48 CHCL2 [22]CCL2 2.0606 1.684 49 CCL2 [22]CCL2 1.8016 1.448 50 CHCL3 [23]CCL3 2.87 2.41 51 CCL3 [23]CCL3 2.6401 2.184 52 CCL4 [24]CCL4 3.39 2.91 53 ACCL [25]ACCL 1.1562 0.844 54 CH3NO2 [26]CNO2 2.0086 1.868 55 CH2NO2 [26]CNO2 1.7818 1.56 56 CHNO2 [26]CNO2 1.5544 1.248 57 ACNO2 [27]ACNO2 1.4199 1.104 58 CS2 [28]CS2 2.057 1.65 59 CH3SH [29]CH3SH 1.877 1.676 60 CH2SH [29]CH3SH 1.651 1.368 61 FURFURAL [30]FURFURAL 3.168 2.484 62 DOH [31]DOH 2.4088 2.248 63 I [32]I 1.264 0.992 64 BR [33]BR 0.9492 0.832 65 CH=-C [34]C=-C 1.292 1.088 66 C=-C [34]C=-C 1.0613 0.784 67 DMSO [35]DMSO 2.8266 2.472 68 ACRY [36]ACRY 2.3144 2.052 69 CL-(C=C) [37]CLCC 0.791 0.724 70 C=C [2]C=C 0.6605 0.485 71 ACF [38]ACF 0.6948 0.524 72 DMF [39]DMF 3.0856 2.736 73 HCON(CH2)2 [39]DMF 2.6322 2.12 74 CF3 [40]CF2 1.406 1.38 75 CF2 [40]CF2 1.0105 0.92 76 CF [40]CF2 0.615 0.46 77 COO [41]COO 1.38 1.2 78 SIH3 [42]SIH2 1.6035 1.2632 79 SIH2 [42]SIH2 1.4443 1.0063 80 SIH [42]SIH2 1.2853 0.7494 81 SI [42]SIH2 1.047 0.4099 82 SIH2O [43]SIO 1.4838 1.0621 83 SIHO [43]SIO 1.303 0.7639 84 SIO [43]SIO 1.1044 0.4657 85 NMP [44]NMP 3.981 3.2 86 CCL3F [45]CCLF 3.0356 2.644 87 CCL2F [45]CCLF 2.2287 1.916 88 HCCL2F [45]CCLF 2.406 2.116 89 HCCLF [45]CCLF 1.6493 1.416 90 CCLF2 [45]CCLF 1.8174 1.648 91 HCCLF2 [45]CCLF 1.967 1.828 92 CCLF3 [45]CCLF 2.1721 2.1 93 CCL2F2 [45]CCLF 2.6243 2.376 94 AMH2 [46]CON(AM) 1.4515 1.248 95 AMHCH3 [46]CON(AM) 2.1905 1.796 96 AMHCH2 [46]CON(AM) 1.9637 1.488 97 AM(CH3)2 [46]CON(AM) 2.8589 2.428 98 AMCH3CH2 [46]CON(AM) 2.6322 2.12 99 AM(CH2)2 [46]CON(AM) 2.4054 1.812 100 C2H5O2 [47]OCCOH 2.1226 1.904 101 C2H4O2 [47]OCCOH 1.8952 1.592 102 CH3S [48]CH2S 1.613 1.368 103 CH2S [48]CH2S 1.3863 1.06 104 CHS [48]CH2S 1.1589 0.748 105 MORPH [49]MORPH 3.474 2.796 106 C4H4S [50]THIOPHEN 2.8569 2.14 107 C4H3S [50]THIOPHEN 2.6908 1.86 108 C4H2S [50]THIOPHEN 2.5247 1.58 109 NCO [51]NCO 1.0567 0.732 118 (CH2)2SU [55]SULFONES 2.6869 2.12 119 CH2CHSU [55]SULFONES 2.4595 1.808 178 IMIDAZOL [84]IMIDAZOL 2.026 0.868 179 BTI [85]BTI 5.774 4.932 Using ugropy to retrieve UNIFAC subgroups There is the possibility of using another library of our group ugropy to retrieve the UNIFAC \nsubgroups and not suffer the pain of typing the subgroup numbers and\nparameters by hand. The next Python snippet shows how you can use it. from ugropy import unifac , writers names = [ \"water\" , \"toluene\" , \"acetone\" ] groups = [ unifac . get_groups ( n ) . subgroups for n in names ] fortran_code = writers . to_yaeos ( groups , unifac ) print ( fortran_code ) Typical generated output is a setup snippet , not a complete program. Inspect\nthe proposed decomposition and ensure it uses the original UNIFAC variant;\nautomatic group assignment does not validate mixture predictions: use yaeos , only : Groups type ( Groups ) :: molecules ( 3 ) molecules ( 1 )% groups_ids = [ 16 ] molecules ( 1 )% number_of_groups = [ 1 ] molecules ( 2 )% groups_ids = [ 9 , 11 ] molecules ( 2 )% number_of_groups = [ 5 , 1 ] molecules ( 3 )% groups_ids = [ 1 , 18 ] molecules ( 3 )% number_of_groups = [ 1 , 1 ] Examples Here is an example of a fully instantiated UNIFACLV model. Please check the Gibbs Excess Models section in the user documentation to learn all the things\nyou can do with this model. Notice that here we are using the setup_unifac function to instantiate the\nmodel. Calculating activity coefficients We can instantiate a UNIFAC model with a mixture ethanol-water and evaluate\nthe logarithm of activity coefficients of the model for a 0.5 mole fraction of\neach, and a temperature of 298.0 K. program original_unifac_activity use yaeos , only : pr , Groups , UNIFAC , setup_unifac implicit none ! Variables declarations type ( UNIFAC ) :: model type ( Groups ) :: molecules ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: ln_gammas ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: n ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: T real ( pr ) :: Ge ! Variables instances ! Ethanol definition [CH3, CH2, OH] molecules ( 1 )% groups_ids = [ 1 , 2 , 14 ] ! Subgroups ids molecules ( 1 )% number_of_groups = [ 1 , 1 , 1 ] ! Subgroups occurrences ! Water definition [H2O] molecules ( 2 )% groups_ids = [ 16 ] molecules ( 2 )% number_of_groups = [ 1 ] ! Model setup model = setup_unifac ( molecules ) ! Calculate ln_gammas n = [ 0.5_pr , 0.5_pr ] T = 29 8.0_pr call model % ln_activity_coefficient ( n , T , ln_gammas ) call model % excess_gibbs ( n , T , Ge = Ge ) print * , \"ln(gamma): ethanol, water = \" , ln_gammas print * , \"gamma = \" , exp ( ln_gammas ) print * , \"Molar GE [bar L/mol] = \" , Ge / sum ( n ) end program original_unifac_activity This is a complete program. Ethanol uses one CH3, one CH2, and one OH fragment;\nwater uses one dedicated H2O subgroup. Counts are per molecule, independent of\nthe mixture amounts. Results follow ethanol–water order, with dimensionless\ncoefficients obtained by exponentiating the natural-log output. The printed\nmolar excess Gibbs energy uses the library’s bar L/mol convention (multiply by\n100 for J/mol); it is not the total Gibbs energy of mixing. Checks, common errors, and next steps The most common failures with this model are input errors that the program\ncannot detect. Make sure groups_ids and number_of_groups have the same\nlength, that counts are positive, and that each subgroup appears once with its\ntotal occurrence count. Check both directions of every interaction your\nmixtures need. An unavailable group-pair interaction is not a zero-energy\ncontact: it means the prediction for that pair has no basis, and nothing in\nthe output will say so. Two specific traps deserve naming. Do not enter main-group numbers in place of\nsubgroup numbers; the constructor will accept any integer it finds in its\ntable and produce a plausible-looking answer for the wrong fragment. And do not\ndecompose methanol as an ordinary CH3 plus an OH when the model defines a\ndedicated CH3OH subgroup, because that changes both the size and the interaction\nof the molecule. Use nonnegative mole amounts with a positive total and kelvin temperatures.\nScaling all amounts should leave gamma unchanged, and the pure component’s\ncoefficient should approach 1 as the mixture approaches that pure component. Know what the model does not do. It calculates no liquid density, no vapor\nfugacity, and no chemical speciation. Predictions outside a group’s fitted\nrange, particularly for very dilute solutes or for a different equilibrium type\nthan the one the parameters were fitted to, need experimental checks. Continue\nto the shared property methods for excess enthalpies and\nderivatives, or compare against Dortmund using its own group definitions and parameters. A full phase-equilibrium calculation requires more\nthan evaluating a single liquid composition. References Fredenslund, A., Jones, R. L., & Prausnitz, J. M. (1975). Group‐contribution\nestimation of activity coefficients in nonideal liquid mixtures. AIChE Journal,\n21(6), 1086–1099. https://doi.org/10.1002/aic.690210607 Skjold-Jorgensen, S., Kolbe, B., Gmehling, J., & Rasmussen, P. (1979).\nVapor-Liquid Equilibria by UNIFAC Group Contribution. Revision and Extension.\nIndustrial & Engineering Chemistry Process Design and Development, 18(4),\n714–722. https://doi.org/10.1021/i260072a024 Gmehling, J., Rasmussen, P., & Fredenslund, A. (1982). Vapor-liquid\nequilibriums by UNIFAC group contribution. Revision and extension. 2.\nIndustrial & Engineering Chemistry Process Design and Development, 21(1),\n118–127. https://doi.org/10.1021/i200016a021 Macedo, E. A., Weidlich, U., Gmehling, J., & Rasmussen, P. (1983).\nVapor-liquid equilibriums by UNIFAC group contribution. Revision and extension. 3.\nIndustrial & Engineering Chemistry Process Design and Development, 22(4),\n676–678. https://doi.org/10.1021/i200023a023 Tiegs, D., Rasmussen, P., Gmehling, J., & Fredenslund, A. (1987).\nVapor-liquid equilibria by UNIFAC group contribution. 4. Revision and\nextension. Industrial & Engineering Chemistry Research, 26(1), 159–161. https://doi.org/10.1021/ie00061a030 Hansen, H. K., Rasmussen, P., Fredenslund, A., Schiller, M., & Gmehling, J.\n(1991). Vapor-liquid equilibria by UNIFAC group contribution. 5. Revision and\nextension. Industrial & Engineering Chemistry Research, 30 (10), 2352–2355. https://doi.org/10.1021/ie00058a017 Wittig, R., Lohmann, J., & Gmehling, J. (2003). Vapor−Liquid Equilibria by\nUNIFAC Group Contribution. 6. Revision and Extension. Industrial & Engineering\nChemistry Research, 42(1), 183–188. https://doi.org/10.1021/ie020506l","tags":"","url":"page/usage/excessmodels/unifac_models/unifaclv.html"},{"title":"UNIFAC-Dortmund – yaeos","text":"Dortmund modified UNIFAC model Model description Subgroups list Using ugropy to retrieve UNIFAC-Dortmund subgroups Examples Calculating activity coefficients Checks, common errors, and next steps References Dortmund modified UNIFAC model Model description Modified UNIFAC (Dortmund) is a structure-based liquid activity model intended\nto improve the representation of mixtures across properties such as phase\nequilibria and excess enthalpies. It changes both the geometric mixing term and\nthe group interaction temperature law, with its own fitted parameter set .\nIt is a useful candidate when fitted molecular binary parameters are unavailable\nand the Dortmund group/interaction coverage is suitable, not an automatic\nupgrade for every mixture. The geometric term describes size/shape mismatch; the energetic (“residual”)\nterm describes unlike group contacts relative to their pure-molecule references.\nBoth contribute to excess Gibbs energy relative to an ideal liquid. This is\nnot the residual ideal-gas departure of an EOS. The resulting activity coefficient\nenters , not a vapor fugacity expression by itself. Read the family overview for model selection and reference-state details. This is the Dortmund modified UNIFAC model. In this model, the parameters are\ndefined as: The temperature function is defined with a quadratic temperature\nfunction as follows: Here T is in K; has units K, is dimensionless, and has units 1/K. It is , not the exponent itself , that\nis quadratic in temperature. The full exponent is . The two directions (j,k) and (k,j) generally\ndiffer. The constructor supplies these coefficients; do not import classic\nUNIFAC or molecular NRTL coefficients into this equation. setup_dortmund(molecules) returns the shared type(UNIFAC) , already configured\nwith the Dortmund geometry and interaction law. Merely changing model%d on\nan original UNIFAC model does not create a Dortmund model. Subgroups list The list of the functional groups and its interaction parameters could be \naccessed on the DDBST web page: https://www.ddbst.com/PublishedParametersUNIFACDO.html We reproduce here the list of functional groups. To instantiate a\nUNIFAC-Dortmund model you must define which functional groups are used in a\nmolecule by the Subgroup Number column. Enter subgroup numbers, not main-group numbers. R and Q are dimensionless\nrelative volumes and areas, not SI properties. Notice the separate primary,\nsecondary, and tertiary alcohol groups: identifying an OH bond alone is not\nenough. The table is a published reference, while actual availability is governed\nby the parameter data bundled with your installed version. Check all required\ndirectional interactions as well as the subgroup list. These numbers belong to the Dortmund variant only. The same integer can\nmean a different fragment in the original UNIFAC or PSRK tables, so take subgroup numbers from the table of the\nvariant you are actually constructing. Subgroup number Subgroup Name Main Group No. Main Group Name R Q 1 CH3 1 CH2 0.6325 1.0608 2 CH2 1 CH2 0.6325 0.7081 3 CH 1 CH2 0.6325 0.3554 4 C 1 CH2 0.6325 0 5 CH2=CH 2 C=C 1.2832 1.6016 6 CH=CH 2 C=C 1.2832 1.2489 7 CH2=C 2 C=C 1.2832 1.2489 8 CH=C 2 C=C 1.2832 0.8962 9 ACH 3 ACH 0.3763 0.4321 10 AC 3 ACH 0.3763 0.2113 11 ACCH3 4 ACCH2 0.91 0.949 12 ACCH2 4 ACCH2 0.91