.8663 82 OH (T) 5 OH 0.6895 0.8345 83 CY-CH2O 43 CY-CH2O 1.4046 1.4 84 TRIOXAN 43 CY-CH2O 1.0413 1.0116 85 CNH2 14 CH2NH2 1.6607 0.985 86 NMP 46 CY-CONC 3.981 3.2 87 NEP 46 CY-CONC 3.7543 2.892 88 NIPP 46 CY-CONC 3.5268 2.58 89 NTBP 46 CY-CONC 3.2994 2.352 91 CONH2 47 CONR 1.4515 1.248 92 CONHCH3 47 CONR 1.5 1.08 93 HCONHCH3 49 HCONR 2.4617 2.192 94 HCONHCH2 49 HCONR 2.4617 1.842 100 CONHCH2 47 CONR 1.5 1.08 101 AM(CH3)2 48 CONR2 2.4748 1.9643 102 AMCH3CH2 48 CONR2 2.2739 1.5754 103 AM(CH2)2 48 CONR2 2.0767 1.1866 104 AC2H2S 52 ACS 1.7943 1.34 105 AC2HS 52 ACS 1.6282 1.06 106 AC2S 52 ACS 1.4621 0.78 107 H2COCH 53 EPOXIDES 1.3601 1.8031 108 COCH 53 EPOXIDES 0.683 0.3418 109 HCOCH 53 EPOXIDES 0.9104 0.6538 110 (CH2)2SU 56 SULFONE 2.687 2.12 111 CH2SUCH 56 SULFONE 2.46 1.808 112 (CH3)2CB 55 CARBONAT 2.42 2.4976 113 (CH2)2CB 55 CARBONAT 2.42 2.0018 114 CH2CH3CB 55 CARBONAT 2.42 2.2497 119 H2COCH2 53 EPOXIDES 1.063 1.123 122 CH3S 61 CH2S 1.613 1.368 123 CH2S 61 CH2S 1.3863 1.06 124 CHS 61 CH2S 1.1589 0.748 153 H2COC 53 EPOXIDES 0.9104 0.6538 178 C3H2N2+ 84 IMIDAZOL 1.3662 0.6797 179 BTI- 85 BTI 5.621 5.9463 184 C3H3N2+ 84 IMIDAZOL 1.843 1.6997 189 C4H8N+ 87 PYRROL 2.7867 2.7723 195 BF4- 89 BF4 3.9628 0.6214 196 C5H5N+ 90 PYRIDIN 2.1094 2.5106 197 OTF- 91 OTF 3.371 2.0001 201 -S-S- 93 -S-S- 1.0678 2.244 209 SO4 98 SO4 0.9903 3.5249 210 HSO4 98 SO4 1.5654 3.8076 211 PF6 99 PF6 3.8183 3.6018 220 C5H4N+ 90 PYRIDIN 2.4873 2.4457 Using ugropy to retrieve UNIFAC-Dortmund subgroups There is the possibility of using another library of our group ugropy to retrieve the\nUNIFAC-Dortmund subgroups and not suffer the pain of typing the subgroup\nnumbers and parameters by hand. The next Python snippet shows how you can use\nit. from ugropy import dortmund , writers names = [ \"water\" , \"toluene\" , \"cyclohexane\" ] groups = [ dortmund . get_groups ( n ) . subgroups for n in names ] fortran_code = writers . to_yaeos ( groups , dortmund ) print ( fortran_code ) Typical output is a setup snippet , not a complete program. Review the\ndecomposition and confirm that the generated IDs are Dortmund IDs: use yaeos , only : Groups type ( Groups ) :: molecules ( 3 ) molecules ( 1 )% groups_ids = [ 16 ] molecules ( 1 )% number_of_groups = [ 1 ] molecules ( 2 )% groups_ids = [ 9 , 11 ] molecules ( 2 )% number_of_groups = [ 5 , 1 ] molecules ( 3 )% groups_ids = [ 78 ] molecules ( 3 )% number_of_groups = [ 6 ] Examples Here is an example of a fully instantiated UNIFAC-Dortmund model. Please check\nthe Gibbs Excess Models section in the user documentation to learn all the\nthings you can do with this model. Notice that here we are using the setup_dortmund function to instantiate\nthe model. Calculating activity coefficients We can instantiate a UNIFAC model with a mixture ethanol-water and evaluate\nthe logarithm of activity coefficients of the model for a 0.5 mole fraction of\neach, and a temperature of 298.0 K. program dortmund_activity use yaeos , only : pr , Groups , UNIFAC , setup_dortmund implicit none ! Variables declarations type ( UNIFAC ) :: model type ( Groups ) :: molecules ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: ln_gammas ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: n ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: T real ( pr ) :: He ! Variables instances ! Ethanol definition [CH3, CH2, primary OH] molecules ( 1 )% groups_ids = [ 1 , 2 , 14 ] ! Subgroups ids molecules ( 1 )% number_of_groups = [ 1 , 1 , 1 ] ! Subgroups occurrences ! Water definition [H2O] molecules ( 2 )% groups_ids = [ 16 ] molecules ( 2 )% number_of_groups = [ 1 ] ! Model setup model = setup_dortmund ( molecules ) ! Calculate ln_gammas n = [ 0.5_pr , 0.5_pr ] T = 29 8.0_pr call model % ln_activity_coefficient ( n , T , ln_gammas ) call model % excess_enthalpy ( n , T , He = He ) print * , \"ln(gamma): ethanol, water = \" , ln_gammas print * , \"gamma = \" , exp ( ln_gammas ) print * , \"Molar HE [bar L/mol] = \" , He / sum ( n ) end program dortmund_activity This complete program uses ethanol first and water second; all output arrays\nfollow that order. The subgroup counts are integers per molecule, while n contains mixture amounts in mol. Natural-log coefficients must be exponentiated\nto obtain gamma ; values need not be less than 1. The excess enthalpy is the\nenthalpy change on mixing relative to ideal-solution enthalpy at this temperature,\nnot the total liquid enthalpy. Multiply bar L/mol by 100 for J/mol. The sign of excess enthalpy is a model prediction, not a general test of whether\nhydrogen bonding occurs. Compare it with data for the same composition and\ntemperature; a surprising sign should prompt checks of group assignment,\nparameter coverage, and variant before interpretation. Checks, common errors, and next steps Keep the Dortmund subgroup geometry and interaction matrices together. Even\nwhere IDs coincide with classic UNIFAC, parameter values and some group\nmeanings differ, and the modified size exponent changes the\ncombinatorial contribution as well. The input errors that matter most here are group-assignment errors. Do not use\nthe secondary or tertiary OH groups for ethanol: the primary, secondary, and\ntertiary alcohol groups have different sizes and different interactions, so the\nchoice changes both contributions. Do not double-count atoms in compound\nfragments, and do not assume that an available subgroup guarantees that every\ninteraction your mixture needs is also available. groups_ids and counts must\nbe equally sized, counts positive, and each subgroup listed once. Use kelvin\nand mole amounts, not Celsius and mass fractions. Check amount-scaling invariance of gamma and the pure-component limit, then\ncompare with measured data near your intended range. The richer temperature law\nis fitted over a specific interval and can extrapolate poorly; it is also not a\nnear-critical, density, or general electrolyte treatment. See shared property methods for derivatives and phase-equilibrium methods for stability\nand phase splits. Coefficients at one trial composition are not a phase diagram. References Weidlich, U., & Gmehling, J. (1987). A modified UNIFAC model. 1. Prediction\n   of VLE, hE, and .gamma..infin. Industrial & Engineering Chemistry Research,\n   26(7), 1372-1381. https://doi.org/10.1021/ie00067a018 Gmehling, J., Li, J., & Schiller, M. (1993). A modified UNIFAC model. 2.\n   Present parameter matrix and results for different thermodynamic properties.\n   Industrial & Engineering Chemistry Research, 32(1), 178-193.\n   https://doi.org/10.1021/ie00013a024 Gmehling, J., Lohmann, J., Jakob, A., Li, J., & Joh, R. (1998). A Modified\n   UNIFAC (Dortmund) Model. 3. Revision and Extension. Industrial & Engineering\n   Chemistry Research, 37(12), 4876-4882. https://doi.org/10.1021/ie980347z Lohmann, J., & Gmehling, J. (2001). Modified UNIFAC (Dortmund). Reliable\n   Model for the Development of Thermal Separation Processes. JOURNAL OF\n   CHEMICAL ENGINEERING OF JAPAN, 34(1), 43-54.\n   https://doi.org/10.1252/jcej.34.43 Lohmann, J., Joh, R., & Gmehling, J. (2001). From UNIFAC to Modified UNIFAC\n   (Dortmund). Industrial & Engineering Chemistry Research, 40(3), 957-964.\n   https://doi.org/10.1021/ie0005710 Wittig, R., Lohmann, J., Joh, R., Horstmann, S., & Gmehling, J. (2001).\n   Vapor−Liquid Equilibria and Enthalpies of Mixing in a Temperature Range from\n   298.15 to 413.15 K for the Further Development of Modified UNIFAC\n   (Dortmund). Industrial & Engineering Chemistry Research, 40(24), 5831-5838.\n   https://doi.org/10.1021/ie010444j Gmehling, J., Wittig, R., Lohmann, J., & Joh, R. (2002). A Modified UNIFAC\n   (Dortmund) Model. 4. Revision and Extension. Industrial & Engineering\n   Chemistry Research, 41(6), 1678-1688. https://doi.org/10.1021/ie0108043 Wittig, R., Lohmann, J., & Gmehling, J. (2003). Prediction of phase\n   equilibria and excess properties for systems with sulfones. AIChE Journal,\n   49(2), 530-537. https://doi.org/10.1002/aic.690490223 Jakob, A., Grensemann, H., Lohmann, J., & Gmehling, J. (2006). Further\n   Development of Modified UNIFAC (Dortmund): Revision and Extension 5.\n   Industrial & Engineering Chemistry Research, 45(23), 7924-7933.\n   https://doi.org/10.1021/ie060355c Hector, T., & Gmehling, J. (2014). Present status of the modified UNIFAC\n    model for the prediction of phase equilibria and excess enthalpies for\n    systems with ionic liquids. Fluid Phase Equilibria, 371, 82-92.\n    https://doi.org/10.1016/j.fluid.2014.03.006 Constantinescu, D., & Gmehling, J. (2016). Further Development of Modified\n    UNIFAC (Dortmund): Revision and Extension 6. Journal of Chemical &\n    Engineering Data, 61(8), 2738-2748.\n    https://doi.org/10.1021/acs.jced.6b00136","tags":"","url":"page/usage/excessmodels/unifac_models/dortmund.html"},{"title":"UNIFAC-PSRK – yaeos","text":"Predictive Soave-Redlich-Kwong (PSRK) UNIFAC Model description Subgroups list Using ugropy to retrieve UNIFAC-PSRK subgroups Examples Calculating activity coefficients Checks, common errors, and next steps References Predictive Soave-Redlich-Kwong (PSRK) UNIFAC Model description This page describes the UNIFAC contribution used for PSRK , not a complete\nPredictive Soave–Redlich–Kwong equation of state. setup_psrk(molecules) returns type(UNIFAC) , an excess-Gibbs liquid activity model. It does not construct an\nSRK cubic, select its alpha function, attach a mixing rule, or calculate vapor\nproperties. Those choices must match the full PSRK formulation if that is your\ngoal; an arbitrary SRK/UNIFAC combination is not automatically PSRK. This group set is relevant to predictive EOS work involving gases and solvents;\nit includes dedicated light-gas groups absent from many original-UNIFAC examples.\nFor standalone liquid screening, compare the parameter coverage and validation\nof original UNIFAC and Dortmund rather than\nselecting PSRK only because a subgroup appears in its table. The model still combines geometric size/shape and energetic group-contact\ncontributions to excess Gibbs energy relative to an ideal liquid. Its internal\n“residual” contribution is not an EOS ideal-gas departure. The returned corrects liquid activity , not vapor pressure\ndirectly and not the vapor fugacity coefficient. See the family overview and shared introduction . This is the Predictive Soave-Redlich-Kwong (PSRK) UNIFAC model. In this model,\nthe parameters are defined as: The temperature function is defined with a quadratic temperature\nfunction as follows: T is in K, is in K, is dimensionless, and is in 1/K. The quadratic refers to ; the exponent is . Interaction coefficients are directional, so\nreversing the indices can change the prediction. PSRK and Dortmund share this\nfunctional form but not the same parameter set or combinatorial exponent.\nThe constructor loads the PSRK data; normally your inputs are just fragments\nand their counts. Subgroups list The list of the functional groups and its interaction parameters could be \naccessed on the DDBST web page: https://www.ddbst.com/psrk.html We reproduce here the list of functional groups. To instantiate a UNIFAC-PSRK\nmodel you must define which functional groups are used in a molecule by the\nSubgroup Number column. Pass subgroup IDs, not main-group IDs. R and Q are dimensionless relative\nsize and area. Dedicated whole-molecule groups such as CH4 and CO2 must not be\nreplaced by guessed atom-by-atom decompositions. The table is a published\nreference; the installed library’s bundled data determine actual availability.\nVerify directional interaction coverage as well as subgroup existence. These numbers belong to the PSRK variant only. For example, subgroup 118\nis methane CH4 here, while the same integer is the sulfone fragment (CH2)2SU in the original UNIFAC table. Take subgroup\nnumbers from the table of the variant you are actually constructing. Subgroup number Subgroup name Main group R Q 1 CH3 [1] CH2 0.9011 0.848 2 CH2 [1] CH2 0.6744 0.54 3 CH [1] CH2 0.4469 0.228 4 C [1] CH2 0.2195 0 5 CH2=CH [2] C=C 1.3454 1.1