 0.6948 0.524 72 DMF [39] DMF 3.0856 2.736 73 HCON(CH2)2 [39] DMF 2.6322 2.12 74 CF3 [40] CF2 1.406 1.38 75 CF2 [40] CF2 1.0105 0.92 76 CF [40] CF2 0.615 0.46 77 COO [41] COO 1.38 1.2 78 SIH3 [42] SIH2 1.6035 1.2632 79 SIH2 [42] SIH2 1.4443 1.0063 80 SIH [42] SIH2 1.2853 0.7494 81 SI [42] SIH2 1.047 0.4099 82 SIH2O [43] SIO 1.4838 1.0621 83 SIHO [43] SIO 1.303 0.7639 84 SIO [43] SIO 1.1044 0.4657 85 NMP [44] NMP 3.981 3.2 86 CCL3F [45] CCLF 3.0356 2.644 87 CCL2F [45] CCLF 2.2287 1.916 88 HCCL2F [45] CCLF 2.406 2.116 89 HCCLF [45] CCLF 1.6493 1.416 90 CCLF2 [45] CCLF 1.8174 1.648 91 HCCLF2 [45] CCLF 1.967 1.828 92 CCLF3 [45] CCLF 2.1721 2.1 93 CCL2F2 [45] CCLF 2.6243 2.376 94 AMH2 [46] CON (AM) 1.4515 1.248 95 AMHCH3 [46] CON (AM) 2.1905 1.796 96 AMHCH2 [46] CON (AM) 1.9637 1.488 97 AM(CH3)2 [46] CON (AM) 2.8589 2.428 98 AMCH3CH2 [46] CON (AM) 2.6322 2.12 99 AM(CH2)2 [46] CON (AM) 2.4054 1.812 100 C2H5O2 [47] OCCOH 2.1226 1.904 101 C2H4O2 [47] OCCOH 1.8952 1.592 102 CH3S [48] CH2S 1.613 1.368 103 CH2S [48] CH2S 1.3863 1.06 104 CHS [48] CH2S 1.1589 0.748 105 MORPH [49] MORPH 3.474 2.796 106 C4H4S [50] THIOPHEN 2.8569 2.14 107 C4H3S [50] THIOPHEN 2.6908 1.86 108 C4H2S [50] THIOPHEN 2.5247 1.58 109 H2C=CH2 [2] C=C 1.3564 1.3098 110 CH=-CH [34] C=-C 0.791 0.72 111 NH3 [55] NH3 0.851 0.778 112 CO [63] CO 0.711 0.828 113 H2 [62] H2 0.416 0.571 114 H2S [61] H2S 1.235 1.202 115 N2 [60] N2 0.856 0.93 116 AR [59] AR 1.177 1.116 117 CO2 [56] CO2 1.3 0.982 118 CH4 [57] CH4 1.1292 1.124 119 O2 [58] O2 0.733 0.849 120 D2 [62] H2 0.37 0.527 121 SO2 [65] SO2 1.343 1.164 122 NO [66] NO 0.716 0.62 123 N2O [67] N2O 0.98 0.888 124 SF6 [68] SF6 2.374 2.056 125 HE [69] HE 0.885 0.985 126 NE [70] NE 0.886 0.986 127 KR [71] KR 1.12 1.12 128 XE [72] XE 1.13 1.13 129 HF [73] HF 1.016 1.216 130 HCL [74] HCL 1.056 1.256 131 HBR [75] HBR 1.058 1.258 132 HI [76] HI 1.393 1.208 133 COS [77] COS 1.6785 1.316 134 CHSH [29] CH3SH 1.425 1.06 135 CSH [29] CH3SH 1.199 0.752 136 H2COCH [51] EPOXY 1.3652 1.008 137 HCOCH [51] EPOXY 1.1378 0.696 138 HCOC [51] EPOXY 0.9104 0.468 139 H2COCH2 [51] EPOXY 1.5926 1.32 140 H2COC [51] EPOXY 1.1378 0.78 141 COC [51] EPOXY 0.6829 0.24 142 F2 [78] F2 0.75 0.88 143 CL2 [79] CL2 1.53 1.44 144 BR2 [80] BR2 1.9 1.66 145 HCN [81] HCN 1.2 1.19 146 NO2 [82] NO2 1 1.1 147 CF4 [83] CF4 1.78 1.82 148 O3 [84] O3 1.1 1.27 149 CLNO [85] CLNO 1.48 1.34 152 CNH2 [14] CNH2 0.9147 0.614 Using ugropy to retrieve UNIFAC-PSRK subgroups There is the possibility of using another library of our group ugropy to retrieve the UNIFAC-PSRK\nsubgroups and not suffer the pain of typing the subgroup numbers and\nparameters by hand. The next Python snippet shows how you can use it. from ugropy import psrk , writers names = [ \"water\" , \"toluene\" , \"acetone\" ] groups = [ psrk . get_groups ( n ) . subgroups for n in names ] fortran_code = writers . to_yaeos ( groups , psrk ) print ( fortran_code ) Typical output is a setup snippet , not a complete program. Confirm that\nthe assignments use PSRK subgroup definitions before copying them: use yaeos , only : Groups type ( Groups ) :: molecules ( 3 ) molecules ( 1 )% groups_ids = [ 16 ] molecules ( 1 )% number_of_groups = [ 1 ] molecules ( 2 )% groups_ids = [ 9 , 11 ] molecules ( 2 )% number_of_groups = [ 5 , 1 ] molecules ( 3 )% groups_ids = [ 1 , 18 ] molecules ( 3 )% number_of_groups = [ 1 , 1 ] Examples Here is an example of a fully instantiated UNIFAC-PSRK model. Please check the Gibbs Excess Models section in the user documentation to learn all the things\nyou can do with this model. Notice that here we are using the setup_psrk function to instantiate the\nmodel. Calculating activity coefficients We can instantiate a UNIFAC model with a mixture ethanol-water and evaluate\nthe logarithm of activity coefficients of the model for a 0.5 mole fraction of\neach, and a temperature of 298.0 K. program psrk_unifac_activity use yaeos , only : pr , Groups , UNIFAC , setup_psrk implicit none ! Variables declarations type ( UNIFAC ) :: model type ( Groups ) :: molecules ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: ln_gammas ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: n ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: T real ( pr ) :: Ge ! Variables instances ! Ethanol definition [CH3, CH2, OH] molecules ( 1 )% groups_ids = [ 1 , 2 , 14 ] ! Subgroups ids molecules ( 1 )% number_of_groups = [ 1 , 1 , 1 ] ! Subgroups occurrences ! Water definition [H2O] molecules ( 2 )% groups_ids = [ 16 ] molecules ( 2 )% number_of_groups = [ 1 ] ! Model setup model = setup_psrk ( molecules ) ! Calculate ln_gammas n = [ 0.5_pr , 0.5_pr ] T = 29 8.0_pr call model % ln_activity_coefficient ( n , T , ln_gammas ) call model % excess_gibbs ( n , T , Ge = Ge ) print * , \"ln(gamma): ethanol, water = \" , ln_gammas print * , \"gamma = \" , exp ( ln_gammas ) print * , \"Molar GE [bar L/mol] = \" , Ge / sum ( n ) end program psrk_unifac_activity This complete program deliberately evaluates only the group model. Output order\nis ethanol then water, matching molecules and n . Counts describe fragments\nper molecule, not mixture composition. ln_gammas are natural logarithms; their\nexponentials are dimensionless activity coefficients. The molar excess energy\nis in bar L/mol (multiply by 100 for J/mol), not the full mixing Gibbs energy.\nNo vapor phase or gas solubility is solved here. Do not assume this program prints the same activity coefficients as original\nUNIFAC. Even if some parameter entries coincide, geometry, group definitions,\ninteraction tables, and temperature handling are variant-specific. Compare\nnumerical results only after checking the installed parameter tables and the\nexact groups and conventions used by both constructors. Checks, common errors, and next steps Use equally sized subgroup-ID/count arrays, list each subgroup once with a\npositive total count, and check that all required directed interactions are\navailable. Do not use an apparent zero or missing table entry as proof of ideal\nbehavior. Keep the PSRK data, temperature law, and geometry together; borrowed\ncoefficients from other variants invalidate the model definition. In\nparticular, subgroup numbers must come from the PSRK table on this page, since\nthe same integers mean different fragments in the other variants. Use kelvin and nonnegative mole amounts with positive total. Scaling all amounts\nshould not change gamma ; the present pure component should have a coefficient\napproaching 1. These checks test implementation and input consistency, not\naccuracy. Be clear about what the PSRK name implies and what it does not. High-pressure\nreliability of a full PSRK equation of state does not follow from evaluating\nthis pressure-independent group model, especially for supercritical light gases\nwhere an ordinary pure-liquid standard state needs careful treatment. If your\ngoal is a high-pressure vapor-liquid calculation, you need the equation of\nstate itself. For thermal properties see the shared GeModel methods . For EOS\ncoupling, continue to the cubic mixing rules and\nuse a consistent full formulation. Always validate phase-equilibrium predictions\nagainst data in the intended range. References Holderbaum T., “Die Vorausberechnung von Dampf-Flüssig-Gleichgewichten mit\n   einer Gruppenbeitragszustandsgleichung”, Thesis, Universität Dortmund, 1990 Holderbaum T., Gmehling J., “PSRK: Eine Zustandsgleichung zur Vorhersage von\n   Dampf/Flüssig- Gleichgewichten bei mittleren und hohen Drücken.”,\n   Chem.Ing.Tech. CIT, 63(1), 57-59, 1991 Holderbaum T., Gmehling J., “PSRK: A Group-Contribution Equation of State\n   based on UNIFAC”, Fluid Phase Equilib., 70, 251-265, 1991 Fischer K., “Die PSRK-Methode: Eine Zustandgleichung unter Verwendung des\n   UNIFAC-Gruppenbeitragsmodells”, Thesis, C.-v.-O. Universität Oldenburg, 1993 Fischer K., Gmehling J., “Further Development, Status and Results of the\n   PSRK Method for the Prediction of Vapor-Liquid Equilibria and Gas\n   Solubilities”, Fluid Phase Equilib., 112, 1-22, 1995 Fischer K., Gmehling J., “Further Development, Status and Results of the\n   PSRK Method for the Prediction of Vapor-Liquid Equilibria and Gas\n   Solubilities”, Fluid Phase Equilib., 121, 185-206, 1996 Gmehling J., Li J., Fischer K., “Further development of the PSRK model for\n   the prediction of gas solubilities and vapor-liquid-equilibria at low and\n   high pressures”, Fluid Phase Equilib., 141, 113-127, 1997 Horstmann S., Fischer K., Gmehling J., “PSRK group contribution equation of\n   state: revision and extension III”, Fluid Phase Equilib., 167, 173-186, 2000 Horstmann S., “Theoretische und experimentelle Untersuchungen zum\n   Hochdruckphasengleichgewichtsverhalten fluider Stoffgemische für die\n   Erweiterung der PSRK-Gruppenbeitragszustandsgleichung”, Thesis, C.-v.-O.\n   Universität Oldenburg, 2000 Horstmann S., Jabloniec A., Krafczyk J., Fischer K., Gmehling J., “PSRK\n    Group Contribution Equation of State: Comprehensive Revision and Extension\n    IV, Including Critical Constants and a-Function Parameters for 1000\n    Components”, Fluid Phase Equilib., 227(2), 157-164, 2005","tags":"","url":"page/usage/excessmodels/unifac_models/psrk.html"},{"title":"Phase Equilibrium – yaeos","text":"Imagine cooling a closed bottle containing methane and butane. Initially it\nmay contain only gas; after cooling, a liquid can appear. Both substances can\nbe present in both phases, but in different proportions. A component is a chemical substance in the model (methane, butane, …). A phase is a homogeneous portion of the system (a vapor, a liquid, or a\n  second immiscible liquid). Two components do not imply two phases. The feed composition describes the entire bottle, not either phase\n  separately. A phase fraction tells you how much material is in a phase,\n  whereas a phase composition tells you what that phase contains. These routines predict equilibrium according to the selected model. They do\nnot describe how fast droplets form, heat transfer, or the time required to\nreach equilibrium. In yaeos , most phase-equilibrium procedures return the EquilibriumState type, which holds the phase compositions, molar volumes,\npressure, temperature, phase fraction, and iteration information. Multiphase\ncalculations return MPEquilibriumState . Thermodynamic background Use mole fractions throughout: z = n/sum(n) converts component mole amounts n to a normalized feed z . Keep the component order identical to the model’s\ncritical constants and interaction parameters. Temperatures are in K,\npressures in bar, and phase molar volumes in L/mol. For a mixture at fixed temperature and pressure, phase equilibrium requires\nthe equality of the chemical potentials, or equivalently the fugacities, of\neach component in every phase: together with the overall mass balance where is the feed mole fraction, and are the heavy- and\nlight-phase mole fractions, and is the light-phase mole fraction.\nEliminating and with the K-factors gives the\nRachford-Rice equation: Here is the number of components, selects one component, and is its dimensionless fugacity coefficient, a measure of\nnon-ideal behavior. and label liquid and vapor. Equal fugacity\nmeans there is no thermodynamic driving force to transfer a component between\nthe phases. means component is enriched in the y phase,\nnot that it is absent from the x phase. The fraction is on a mole basis , not a mass or volume basis.\nFor a feed of moles, the phases contain and moles. Their volumes are therefore and . A small vapor mole fraction can occupy a large volume. For the GeModel flash and stability routines discussed here, activity\ncoefficients replace fugacity coefficients to describe liquid-liquid equilibrium. These routines do not automatically add vapor pressures or a\nvapor-phase model to create a vapor-liquid calculation. Thermodynamic background Calculation types Which calculation should I choose? A volume root is not a complete equilibrium result The EquilibriumState type General workflow Practical notes Calculation types The main phase-equilibrium calculations are: Calculation Specification Routine Two-phase flash flash , flash_second_order Two-phase flash flash Bubble/dew pressure Feed composition and ; solve and incipient composition saturation_pressure Bubble/dew temperature Feed composition and ; solve and incipient composition saturation_temperature Phase envelope Continuation from a saturation point pt_envelope_2ph Stability analysis Tangent-plane distance min_tpd Critical point Critical conditions critical_point Multiphase flash several phases pt_mp_flash The detailed pages are organized as follows: Flash calculations describes the and flashes,\n  initial K-factors, second-order flash, and flashes with activity-coefficient\n  models. Saturation points describes bubble, dew, and\n  liquid-liquid points, plus pure-component saturation lines. Phase envelopes describes the continuation method for\n  two-phase envelopes and isopleths. Stability analysis describes tangent-plane distance\n  calculations and how they are used to initialize flashes. Critical points describes critical-point and critical-line\n  calculations. Multiphase flash describes the calculation of systems with\n  more than two equilibrium phases. Which calculation should I choose? “How much liquid and vapor do I have at this T and P?” Use a flash. “At what pressure does the first bubble/drop appear at this T?” Use\n  saturation pressure. Fix pressure instead to find a saturation temperature.\n  At a boundary the new phase has zero limiting amount; saturation does not\n  determine a finite split inside the two-phase region. “Where is the boundary over a range of T and P?” Trace an envelope from\n  one checked saturation point. It follows a branch, not every possible\n  boundary of a complicated mixture. “Should this homogeneous feed split at all?” Start with stability\n  analysis. A failed two-phase flash is not the answer to that question. “Where do the two phases become identical?” Use critical calculations. “Could two liquids coexist with vapor?” Read the multiphase page,\n  including the current wrapper’s implementation limitations. A volume root is not a complete equilibrium result An equation of state can have several volumes at the same composition ,\ntemperature, and pressure. root_type=\"liquid\" or \"vapor\" selects a root; \"stable\" selects the preferred homogeneous root at that composition. This\ndoes not test whether a lower-Gibbs-energy state exists at different phase\ncompositions . Likewise, equality of fugacities and material balance give a candidate\nequilibrium. A local solver can converge to identical phases, a metastable\nsplit, or an equilibrium that is unstable to a third phase. Global equilibrium\nrequires the lowest appropriate thermodynamic potential among all admissible\nstates. Stability searches help test this but finite numerical searches are\nnot a proof that no other state exists. The EquilibriumState type A two-phase result contains: Field Meaning kind Routine-dependent: \"split\" , \"bubble\" , \"dew\" , \"liquid-liquid\" , \"critical\" , or \"failed\" x Heavy-phase mole fractions y Light-phase mole fractions Vx , Vy Phase molar volumes [L/mol] T , P Temperature [K] and pressure [bar] beta Mole fraction of the light phase iters Iterations used General workflow A typical phase-equilibrium workflow is: Build a model with the desired equation of state and mixing rule. Check the stability of the feed with min_tpd when the phase behaviour\n   is uncertain. Calculate a flash at the conditions of interest. Use the resulting compositions as initial guesses for nearby points. For a complete phase diagram, start from a saturation point and continue it\n   with the envelope routines. Start with the complete PT flash example . Its material-balance\nand fugacity checks are useful even when the solver reports success. Practical notes Check the routine’s result contract, finite positive conditions, normalized\n  nonnegative compositions, phase fractions, and component balance. Failure\n  handling is not uniform; critical and multiphase routines need extra checks. Use initial guesses ( k0 , p0 , t0 , y0 ) from previous calculations when\n  scanning a region. Continuation is more robust than independent calculations. Near critical points, compositions and volumes approach each other and phase\n  fractions become poorly determined. Try continuation, smaller steps, and\n  alternative initial guesses; changing solver does not remove this sensitivity. For liquid-liquid equilibria, perform a stability analysis first and consider\n  forcing the light phase to the liquid root in flash_second_order . Activity-coefficient models ( GeModel ) require an initial K-factor\n  estimate for flash ; min_tpd can provide one. flash_second_order initializes these models with stability analysis when k0 is absent. Validate model parameters against data in the temperature, pressure, and\n  composition region of interest. A converged cubic-model water/hydrocarbon\n  split, for example, is not by itself a validated prediction of that system.","tags":"","url":"page/usage/phase_equilibrium/index.html"},{"title":"Flash calculations – yaeos","text":"Flash calculations Theory flash PT flash VT flash Initial K-factors flash_no_beta_limits Second-order flash Flash with activity-coefficient models Multiphase flash Practical notes Flash calculations Flash calculations determine how a feed of known composition splits into two or\nmore equilibrium phases at specified conditions. They are one of the most common\nphase-equilibrium calculations in process and reservoir modelling. In yaeos , flash routines return the EquilibriumState derived type, which\ncontains the phase compositions, molar volumes, pressure, temperature, phase\nfraction, and the number of iterations used. The two main specifications are: : flash with p_spec and T . : flash with v_spec and T . Theory A two-phase flash solves the isofugacity condition and the mass balance\nsimultaneously. With K-factors , the mass balance gives the\nRachford-Rice equation: which is solved for the light-phase fraction . The K-factors are updated\nfrom the fugacity coefficients: Here z is the normalized feed composition, x and y are the heavy- and\nlight-phase compositions, and beta is the mole fraction of feed in the y phase. Once K and beta are known, Both phase compositions must sum to one. The balance z = (1-beta)*x + beta*y must hold component by component, not just in total. For an ArModel , fugacity coefficients are evaluated from the equation of\nstate. For a GeModel , these flash routines use activity coefficients in\nboth phases: this is a liquid-liquid calculation, not a vapor-liquid flash\nwith automatically supplied pure-component vapor pressures. flash The flash function uses successive substitution. Its interface is: type ( EquilibriumState ) function flash ( model , z , T , v_spec , p_spec , k0 , iters ) class ( BaseModel ), intent ( in ) :: model real ( pr ), intent ( in ) :: z (:) real ( pr ), intent ( in ) :: T real ( pr ), optional , intent ( in ) :: v_spec real ( pr ), optional , intent ( in ) :: p_spec real ( pr ), optional , intent ( in ) :: k0 (:) integer , optional , intent ( out ) :: iters end function flash For an ArModel , provide exactly one of p_spec and v_spec . Pass\nnormalized mole fractions z ; unlike saturation routines, this routine does\nnot normalize the feed for you. For a GeModel , omit both specifications and\nprovide k0 . The result is a type(EquilibriumState) with: Field Meaning kind \"split\" for an accepted split, \"failed\" for a trivial solution or iteration failure x Heavy-phase mole fractions y Light-phase mole fractions Vx Heavy-phase molar volume [L/mol] Vy Light-phase molar volume [L/mol] T Temperature [K] P Pressure [bar] beta Mole fraction of the light phase iters Iterations used PT flash program pt_flash use yaeos , only : pr , ArModel , EquilibriumState , PengRobinson76 , flash use , intrinsic :: ieee_arithmetic , only : ieee_is_finite implicit none class ( ArModel ), allocatable :: model type ( EquilibriumState ) :: result real ( pr ) :: tc ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: pc ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: w ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: z ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: T real ( pr ) :: P real ( pr ) :: balance ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: lnphi_x ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: lnphi_y ( 2 ) integer :: iters tc = [ 19 0.564_pr , 42 5.12_pr ] ! Methane / n-butane pc = [ 4 5.99_pr , 3 7.96_pr ] w = [ 0.0115478_pr , 0.200164_pr ] model = PengRobinson76 ( tc , pc , w ) z = [ 0.4_pr , 0.6_pr ] T = 29 4.0_pr P = 6 0.0_pr ! Supply iters explicitly; see the implementation note below. result = flash ( model , z , T , p_spec = P , iters = iters ) if ( result % kind /= \"split\" ) then print * , \"No accepted split; check stability and initialization.\" stop end if if (. not . all ( ieee_is_finite ( result % x ))) error stop \"Nonfinite x\" if (. not . all ( ieee_is_finite ( result % y ))) error stop \"Nonfinite y\" if (. not . ieee_is_finite ( result % beta )) error stop \"Nonfinite beta\" if ( any ( result % x <= 0.0_pr )) error stop \"Nonpositive x\" if ( any ( result % y <= 0.0_pr )) error stop \"Nonpositive y\" if ( result % beta < 0.0_pr . or . result % beta > 1.0_pr ) error stop \"Invalid beta\" print * , \"beta = \" , result % beta print * , \"x = \" , result % x print * , \"y = \" , result % y print * , \"Composition sums: \" , sum ( result % x ), sum ( result % y ) balance = ( 1.0_pr - result % beta ) * result % x + result % beta * result % y - z print * , \"Maximum component-balance error: \" , maxval ( abs ( balance )) call model % lnphi_pt ( result % x , P , T , root_type = \"liquid\" , lnPhi = lnphi_x ) call model % lnphi_pt ( result % y , P , T , root_type = \"stable\" , lnPhi = lnphi_y ) print * , \"Maximum log-fugacity mismatch: \" , & maxval ( abs ( log ( result % x ) + lnphi_x - log ( result % y ) - lnphi_y )) end program pt_flash The last two diagnostics should be small relative to your required numerical\naccuracy. They test balance and phase equilibrium, not global stability. The\nparameters here illustrate the API; they are not a fitted mixture data set. Current implementation note: flash declares iters optional but uses it\nas its working iteration counter without a presence check. Pass a declared\ninteger with iters=iters in calls to this routine. Invalid combinations of\nspecifications can return early without a usable result; validate inputs before\ncalling rather than relying on kind to catch API misuse. VT flash Fragment replacing the call in the PT program above: result = flash ( model , z , T , v_spec = 1.0_pr , iters = iters ) The specified volume is feed molar volume , not vessel volume. Divide vessel\nvolume by total feed moles first. The calculated pressure also satisfies v_spec = (1-beta)*Vx + beta*Vy . As in the PT case, the routine seeks a\ntwo-phase split and is not a general single-phase state classifier. Initial K-factors By default, the flash initializes the K-factors with the Wilson correlation for ArModel models. A better initial estimate can be supplied with k0 = y/x : Fragment (add the import and declaration before executable statements): use yaeos , only : k_wilson real ( pr ) :: k0 ( 2 ) k0 = k_wilson ( model , T = T , P = P ) result = flash ( model , z , T , p_spec = P , k0 = k0 , iters = iters ) K-factor initialization is especially important near phase boundaries, for\nliquid-liquid splits, and for strongly non-ideal systems. Note that the flash does not use k0 unchanged: before the iteration starts it\nadjusts the K-factors (scaling them by 1.1 or 0.9) until the Rachford-Rice\nequation has a solution with . This modifies an internal copy,\nnot the caller’s k0 array. It helps initialize the split equations but does\nnot establish that the feed is actually two-phase. A genuinely single-phase\nfeed can be pushed toward a trial split; use stability analysis rather than\ntreating this adjustment as a phase-classification test. flash_no_beta_limits flash_no_beta_limits is an advanced variant intended for unconstrained\nphase fractions. Two caveats apply to the current implementation: the required beta0 argument is accepted but never used to initialize the working phase\nfraction, so the first Rachford-Rice solve starts from an undefined value; and\nthe routine prints its iteration history to standard output. Do not rely on\nrecovery from an uninitialized starting value; the behavior is not defined by\nthe interface. Signature illustration only (not a\nrecommended runnable example): result = flash_no_beta_limits ( & model , z , T , beta0 = 0.5_pr , p_spec = P , k0 = k0 , iters = iters & ) Even in an unconstrained formulation, a fraction outside [0,1] is not a\nphysical phase amount for the specified feed. Second-order flash flash_second_order calculates a two-phase split at fixed temperature and\npressure by directly minimizing the reduced Gibbs energy with a second-order\nNewton method: type ( EquilibriumState ) function flash_second_order ( & model , z , T , P_spec , k0 , light_root , iters , tol , max_iters & ) result ( flash ) The main arguments are: Argument Meaning model ArModel or GeModel z Global mole fractions T Temperature [K] P_spec Pressure [bar] k0 Optional initial K-factors light_root \"stable\" (default) or \"liquid\" for ArModel iters Iterations used tol Gradient-norm tolerance, default 1e-8 max_iters Maximum iterations, default 200 use yaeos , only : flash_second_order result = flash_second_order ( model , z , T , P ) For an ArModel , the heavy phase is always evaluated with the liquid root and\nthe light phase with the stable root. Use light_root=\"liquid\" for a\nliquid-liquid split. For a GeModel , both phases are described by activity\ncoefficients. The second-order method is an alternative when successive substitution stalls,\nnot a guarantee of convergence or of the globally stable split. Check kind ,\nbalances, and stability again. It rejects an excessive final gradient or\nidentical compositions; failure is reported with kind=\"failed\" and P=-1 .\nUse strictly positive normalized feeds: the scaled variables and logarithms\nin this implementation divide by each z(i) . Flash with activity-coefficient models flash accepts GeModel objects such as NRTL , UNIQUAC , or UNIFAC . In that case the pressure and volume cannot be specified, and an\ninitial guess for the K-factors is mandatory: Complete liquid-liquid example: program activity_flash use yaeos , only : pr , UNIFAC , Groups , setup_unifac , EquilibriumState use yaeos , only : flash , min_tpd implicit none integer , parameter :: nc = 3 type ( UNIFAC ) :: model type ( Groups ) :: molecules ( nc ) type ( EquilibriumState ) :: result real ( pr ) :: n ( nc ) real ( pr ) :: w ( nc ) real ( pr ) :: T real ( pr ) :: P real ( pr ) :: mintpd integer :: iters ! Define the groups of each molecule molecules ( 1 )% groups_ids = [ 1 , 42 ] molecules ( 1 )% number_of_groups = [ 1 , 1 ] molecules ( 2 )% groups_ids = [ 1 , 2 ] molecules ( 2 )% number_of_groups = [ 2 , 4 ] molecules ( 3 )% groups_ids = [ 16 ] molecules ( 3 )% number_of_groups = [ 1 ] model = setup_unifac ( molecules ) n = [ 0.2_pr , 0.7_pr , 0.1_pr ] T = 25 0.0_pr P = 1.0_pr ! Find the composition with the minimum tangent-plane distance call min_tpd ( model , n , P = P , T = T , mintpd = mintpd , w = w ) ! Use the incipient-phase composition as the initial K-factors if ( mintpd < - 1.0e-6_pr ) then result = flash ( model , n , T , k0 = w / n , iters = iters ) if ( result % kind == \"split\" ) print * , result % x , result % y , result % beta else print * , \"No negative TPD found in this search.\" end if end program activity_flash For GeModel , flash uses the activity coefficients directly and solves only\nthe isofugacity and mass-balance equations. A stability analysis with min_tpd is the recommended initialization strategy. The composition and\nphase fraction of the result are meaningful; the pressure and the two phase\nvolumes are not set by the routine and contain undefined values, so do not\nread them. Omitting k0 for a GeModel stops the program with an error\nmessage rather than returning a failed result. Multiphase flash pt_mp_flash is a limited automatic wrapper for ArModel systems. Its np counts main phases excluding a reference phase; np=1 represents\ntwo phases, not one. Read Multiphase flash before using it:\nthe current implementation has incomplete single-phase handling and does not\nrepeat phase addition until global stability is established. Practical notes A failed flash means no accepted two-phase result was obtained. It may reflect\n  a single-phase feed, poor initialization, a trivial solution, or numerical\n  failure. It does not establish that the feed is stable. Always check result%kind before using the compositions. Use k0 from a previous, nearby calculation when building tables or phase\n  envelopes. This reduces iterations and avoids convergence to a trivial\n  solution. For liquid-liquid equilibria, initialize with a stability analysis and\n  consider flash_second_order(..., light_root=\"liquid\") . Never use compositions or volumes from a failed state; fields may be unset\n  or contain placeholders. For a GeModel the pressure and phase volumes are\n  undefined even in a successful result. See the runnable examples in example/basics/3_phase_split.f90 and example/extra/flash.f90 .","tags":"","url":"page/usage/phase_equilibrium/flash.html"},{"title":"Saturation Points – yaeos","text":"Saturation points Theory saturation_pressure saturation_temperature Choosing the kind Results Pure-component saturation lines Relation with phase envelopes Critical points Practical notes Saturation points A saturation point is a state where a feed of fixed composition is in\nequilibrium with an incipient phase. The two most common cases are: Bubble point : the incipient phase is vapor and the feed is liquid. Dew point : the incipient phase is liquid and the feed is vapor. Liquid-liquid point : both phases are liquids. In yaeos , saturation points are calculated with saturation_pressure and saturation_temperature . Both return an EquilibriumState . “Incipient” means the limit as the amount of the new phase approaches zero.\nThe new phase can have a very different composition despite its negligible\namount. Saturation fixes the bulk-phase composition and either temperature\nor pressure, then solves the other condition and the incipient composition.\nTo find finite amounts of both phases at specified T and P, use a flash . These saturation interfaces require an ArModel . Theory A saturation point is the limit of a two-phase region at fixed composition. For\na bubble point, the liquid phase has the feed composition and the vapor phase is\nincipient: For a dew point, the vapor phase has the feed composition and the liquid phase\nis incipient: Here and denote liquid and vapor mole fractions and . The bubble equation normalizes the emerging vapor, y=x*K ; the dew equation normalizes the emerging liquid, x=y/K . In both cases the K-factors are updated from the fugacity coefficients, and the\npressure or temperature is adjusted until the corresponding sum condition is\nsatisfied. A liquid-liquid point follows the same structure with both phases\nevaluated on the liquid root, so the sum condition normalizes the incipient\nliquid composition. saturation_pressure type ( EquilibriumState ) function saturation_pressure ( & model , n , T , kind , p0 , y0 , max_iters & ) class ( ArModel ), target , intent ( in ) :: model real ( pr ), intent ( in ) :: n (:) !! Composition [moles or mole fractions] real ( pr ), intent ( in ) :: T !! Temperature [K] character ( len =* ), intent ( in ) :: kind !! \"bubble\", \"dew\", or \"liquid-liquid\" real ( pr ), optional , intent ( in ) :: p0 !! Initial pressure [bar] real ( pr ), optional , intent ( in ) :: y0 (:) !! Initial incipient composition integer , optional , intent ( in ) :: max_iters end function saturation_pressure The calculation first solves a scalar normalization equation. Internally its\nupdated ratio is incipient/bulk , so the common equation is: For bubbles , but for dews . Do not transfer this internal\nratio convention into flash k0 , which is always y/x . The scalar loop\nupdates this ratio from bulk/incipient fugacity coefficients. A y0 argument\nis always an incipient composition , not a K-factor vector. A one-dimensional Newton step updates pressure. If the scalar iteration\nreaches its limit, the\nroutine switches to a full Newton method that solves for the K-factors and simultaneously. program bubble_pressure use yaeos , only : pr , ArModel , EquilibriumState , PengRobinson76 use yaeos , only : saturation_pressure use , intrinsic :: ieee_arithmetic , only : ieee_is_finite implicit none class ( ArModel ), allocatable :: model type ( EquilibriumState ) :: sat real ( pr ) :: tc ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: pc ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: w ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: n ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: T real ( pr ) :: K ( 2 ) tc = [ 19 0.564_pr , 42 5.12_pr ] pc = [ 4 5.99_pr , 3 7.96_pr ] w = [ 0.0115478_pr , 0.200164_pr ] model = PengRobinson76 ( tc , pc , w ) n = [ 2.5_pr , 6.7_pr ] T = 15 0.0_pr sat = saturation_pressure ( model , n , T , kind = \"bubble\" ) if ( sat % kind /= \"bubble\" ) error stop \"No accepted bubble point\" if (. not . ieee_is_finite ( sat % P )) error stop \"Nonfinite saturation pressure\" if ( sat % P <= 0.0_pr ) error stop \"Invalid saturation pressure\" print * , sat % kind , sat % T , sat % P print * , \"x = \" , sat % x print * , \"y = \" , sat % y print * , \"Composition sums: \" , sum ( sat % x ), sum ( sat % y ) K = sat % y / sat % x print * , \"Bubble sum x*K (should approach one): \" , sum ( sat % x * K ) end program bubble_pressure This sum verifies the convention but is not an independent equilibrium test.\nAlso evaluate the log-fugacity mismatch as in the flash example .\nCheck finite, positive conditions, distinct volumes, and normalized\ncompositions before accepting the result. saturation_temperature type ( EquilibriumState ) function saturation_temperature ( & model , n , P , kind , t0 , y0 , max_iters & ) class ( ArModel ), target , intent ( in ) :: model real ( pr ), intent ( in ) :: n (:) !! Composition [moles or mole fractions] real ( pr ), intent ( in ) :: P !! Pressure [bar] character ( len =* ), intent ( in ) :: kind !! \"bubble\", \"dew\", or \"liquid-liquid\" real ( pr ), optional , intent ( in ) :: t0 !! Initial temperature [K] real ( pr ), optional , intent ( in ) :: y0 (:) !! Initial incipient composition integer , optional , intent ( in ) :: max_iters end function saturation_temperature The same two-stage strategy is used, but now with respect to temperature.\nCall fragments using a declared model and result: sat = saturation_temperature ( model , n , P = 1 5.0_pr , kind = \"bubble\" ) sat = saturation_temperature ( model , n , P = 1 5.0_pr , kind = \"dew\" , t0 = 33 0.0_pr ) The optional initial guesses p0 , t0 , and y0 are useful for a sequence of\npoints. For bubbles use y0=previous%y ; for dews use y0=previous%x , because\nthe argument means incipient composition in either case. Reuse only checked\npoints. Initialization can help branch tracking but does not guarantee it. Choosing the kind kind Feed phase Incipient phase Typical use \"bubble\" Liquid Vapor Boiling point at fixed composition \"dew\" Vapor Liquid Condensation point at fixed composition \"liquid-liquid\" Liquid Liquid Immiscible liquid splits The incipient phase root is selected internally. For \"bubble\" the main phase\nuses the liquid root and the incipient phase the vapor root; for \"dew\" the\nroles are reversed. Results The returned EquilibriumState contains the same fields used by flash\ncalculations: Field Meaning kind The requested kind, or \"failed\" x Heavy-phase composition: the feed for a bubble point, the incipient liquid for a dew point y Light-phase composition: the incipient vapor for a bubble point, the feed for a dew point Vx , Vy Phase molar volumes [L/mol] T , P Saturation temperature [K] and pressure [bar] beta Phase fraction (0 for bubble, 1 for dew) iters Iterations used A detected failure has kind=\"failed\" . Current checks include NaN values,\nan iteration-limit test, and a phase-volume difference below 1e-8 . They are\nnot exhaustive residual or global-stability tests. Although max_iters is\naccepted, the current fallback and final limit test use the module’s max_iterations rather than that argument. Do not rely on it to bound work. Pure-component saturation lines For a pure component, Psat_pure calculates the saturation pressure at a\ngiven temperature: The following are fragments for an initialized model. Add imports and\ndeclarations before executable statements, and choose a subcritical temperature\nfor the selected component (300 K is not subcritical for methane): real ( pr ) :: Psat real ( pr ) :: Vl real ( pr ) :: Vv logical :: converged Psat = model % Psat_pure ( 1 , T = 15 0.0_pr , Vl = Vl , Vv = Vv , converged = converged ) if (. not . converged ) error stop \"Pure saturation did not converge\" The method uses a secant/Newton formulation on the isofugacity condition and\nreturns the liquid and vapor volumes. It has no critical-temperature guard: for\nmethane at 300 K, above its critical temperature, it returned a pressure of\nabout 34.8 bar with identical liquid and vapor volumes and still reported converged=.true. . Always compare the temperature with the component’s\ncritical temperature and check that the two volumes differ. To calculate a complete pure-component saturation line, use pure_saturation_line : use yaeos , only : pure_saturation_line , PurePsat type ( PurePsat ) :: psat psat = pure_saturation_line ( model , component = 1 , minP = 0.1_pr , minT = 10 0.0_pr ) ! Interpolate the saturation temperature at a given pressure print * , psat % get_T ( P = 1.0_pr ) ! Interpolate the saturation pressure at a given temperature print * , psat % get_P ( T = 15 0.0_pr ) The PurePsat type stores the saturation temperatures, pressures, and\nliquid/vapor volumes, together with interpolators for get_T and get_P .\nThe line is traced from the pure-component critical point down to minP or minT with the continuation method. Both lower limits are required, and the\ntrace stops at whichever limit is reached first. For methane with minP=0.1 bar and minT=100 K the line covered 99.8 K to 190.5 K, that is,\nup to the critical point. Inspect the stored P and T ranges before\ninterpolating; extrapolation is not evidence that liquid-vapor coexistence\nexists. Relation with phase envelopes A saturation point is the starting point for a phase-envelope calculation. The\nenvelope routines in Phase envelopes take a converged EquilibriumState and continue it along the phase boundary: Fragment (add imports and declarations before executable statements): use yaeos , only : pt_envelope_2ph , PTEnvel2 type ( PTEnvel2 ) :: envelope sat = saturation_temperature ( model , n , P = 1.0_pr , kind = \"dew\" , t0 = 15 0.0_pr ) if ( sat % kind == \"dew\" ) envelope = pt_envelope_2ph ( model , n / sum ( n ), sat ) This is usually more robust than calculating every saturation point\nindependently, especially near critical points. Critical points Critical points and critical lines are calculated with critical_point and critical_line . They are described in the critical points page. The spec_CP object provides the\nallowed specifications for a critical-point calculation. Practical notes Use z = n/sum(n) when working with mole fractions; the routines normalize\n  the feed internally, and the compositions returned are normalized. Prefer a reduced model containing only present components for feeds with\n  exact zeros. Some paths divide by feed fractions or take logarithms before\n  masking zeros; blanket zero-component support is not assured. Nearly identical volumes can trigger failure near criticality. Do not treat\n  an identical-phase solution as a physical bubble/dew point; investigate with\n  the critical solver . When scanning a phase boundary, reuse y0 and the previous pressure or\n  temperature as initial guesses. See example/basics/2_saturation_points.f90 and example/basics/5_pure_psat.f90 for complete runnable examples.","tags":"","url":"page/usage/phase_equilibrium/saturation_points.html"},{"title":"Phase envelopes – yaeos","text":"Phase envelopes Theory How a point is solved pt_envelope_2ph Example Output Related routines Practical notes Phase envelopes A phase envelope is the set of conditions at which a feed of fixed\ncomposition first splits into two phases. Below the boundary the feed is a\nsingle phase; on the boundary a second phase appears with an infinitesimal\namount; across it, two phases coexist with finite amounts. Think of a fixed-composition mixture of methane and n-hexane. Lowering the\npressure at constant temperature eventually makes the mixture split into a\nvapor and a liquid. The temperature and pressure at which that happens form\nthe dew branch of the envelope. Raising the pressure instead makes the mixture\nsplit at the bubble branch. The two branches meet at the critical point, and\ntogether they form the closed curve that separates the single-phase region\nfrom the two-phase region. Theory Along the envelope, the incipient phase is in equilibrium with the bulk phase,\nso the isofugacity condition and the saturation sum condition hold at every\npoint. The envelope is traced by a continuation method : a predictor\nestimates the next point from the local tangent, and a corrector solves the\nsaturation equations at the new condition. Reusing the previous point as the\ninitial guess is what makes tracing a branch cheap and robust compared with\nsolving each point independently. Isopleths are the same boundary viewed at constant global composition; they\nconnect the bubble and dew branches through the critical point. The name\ncomes from the Greek isopleth : a line of constant quantity. In yaeos it is possible to calculate two-phase envelopes of different kinds. Isopleths, at constant composition (this page). P-x and T-x envelopes, at constant pressure or temperature while the\n  composition changes, through px_envelope_2ph and tx_envelope_2ph . Three-phase envelopes, through pt_envelope_3ph . How a point is solved The solver works with the logarithm of the composition ratios between the two\nphases and with and . Working in logarithms keeps the\nvariables positive and keeps the Newton steps well scaled over many orders of\nmagnitude. At each point it solves the isofugacity equations together with the normalization of the incipient phase and the specification of one variable. The phase\nroots are selected from first_point%kind , which also decides which branch of\nthe envelope is followed. pt_envelope_2ph function pt_envelope_2ph ( & model , z , first_point , & points , iterations , delta_0 , specified_variable_0 , & solver , stop_conditions , maximum_pressure & ) result ( envelopes ) Argument Meaning model ArModel used for the fugacity coefficients z Overall composition of the isopleth (mole fractions) first_point Converged saturation point that starts the trace points Maximum number of points, default 500 iterations Maximum Newton iterations per point, default 100 delta_0 Initial continuation step, default 0.01 specified_variable_0 Index of the initially specified variable iterations , solver , stop_conditions , maximum_pressure Accepted for compatibility but not used by the current implementation The current pt_envelope_2ph delegates the trace to the multiphase routine pt_envelope with one main phase and forwards only points , delta_0 ,\nand specified_variable_0 . In particular, maximum_pressure does not limit\nthe trace; the internal limit of 2000 bar applies. The first_point%kind field selects the branches to use: kind Bulk phase root Incipient phase root Resulting branch \"bubble\" liquid vapor Bubble branch \"dew\" vapor liquid Dew branch \"liquid-liquid\" liquid liquid Liquid-liquid branch The trace continues from first_point and, when it reaches a critical point,\nswitches to the opposite branch. The kind of each saved point records which\nbranch it belongs to. The continuation variable is adaptive: the solver picks the variable with the\nlargest sensitivity at each step, and switches away from pressure or\ntemperature near a critical point. specified_variable_0 therefore sets the\nstarting specification rather than fixing the path. Example program phase_envelope_example use yaeos , only : pr , ArModel , EquilibriumState , PTEnvel2 , PengRobinson76 use yaeos , only : saturation_temperature , pt_envelope_2ph implicit none class ( ArModel ), allocatable :: model type ( EquilibriumState ) :: sat_point type ( PTEnvel2 ) :: envelope real ( pr ) :: tc ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: pc ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: w ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: n ( 2 ) integer :: i tc = [ 19 0.564_pr , 50 7.6_pr ] pc = [ 4 5.99_pr , 3 0.25_pr ] w = [ 0.0115478_pr , 0.301261_pr ] model = PengRobinson76 ( tc , pc , w ) ! Overall composition of the isopleth n = [ 0.9_pr , 0.1_pr ] ! Start from a dew point at low pressure sat_point = saturation_temperature ( model , n , P = 1.0_pr , kind = \"dew\" , t0 = 15 0.0_pr ) if ( sat_point % kind /= \"dew\" ) error stop \"The seed point did not converge\" ! Continue the two-phase boundary envelope = pt_envelope_2ph ( model , n , sat_point ) print * , \"Number of envelope points: \" , size ( envelope % points ) if ( allocated ( envelope % cps )) print * , \"Critical points found: \" , size ( envelope % cps ) do i = 2 , size ( envelope % points ) if ( envelope % points ( i )% kind /= envelope % points ( i - 1 )% kind ) & print * , \"Branch change at point \" , i end do end program phase_envelope_example With these parameters the calculation produces 122 points and one critical\npoint. The first points are on the dew branch, the last ones on the bubble\nbranch, and the critical point sits between them at about 253.8 K and\n177.3 bar. The figure below shows the resulting pressure-temperature boundary: Note the parameters: they illustrate the API, they are not a validated\ndescription of the methane/n-hexane system. Output The result is a PTEnvel2 with two arrays: Field Meaning points Converged saturation states, in continuation order cps Critical points detected along the branch ( T , P ) Each entry of points is an EquilibriumState with kind ( \"bubble\" , \"dew\" , or \"liquid-liquid\" ), T , P , x , y , and beta . In the\ncurrent implementation x is always the bulk phase (it equals the feed z )\nand y is always the incipient phase. For a dew point this is the opposite\nof the saturation_temperature / saturation_pressure convention, where x holds the incipient liquid. beta holds the fraction of the bulk phase\n(1 on the boundary), and Vx , Vy , and iters are not filled in, so do not\nread them. The write command produces a pre-formatted, sectioned file: # PTEnvel2\n\n# kind of sat point\nkind T P beta [phase-x composition vector] [phase-y composition vector]\n\n# other kind of sat point\nkind T P beta [phase-x composition vector] [phase-y composition vector]\n\n# Critical\nT P The current writer emits every point except the last one , so the file\ncontains one row fewer than size(envelope%points) . The beta column is\npresent in the data rows and precedes the composition vectors. Which when plotted with gnuplot with: plot \"outfile\" \\ index \"dew\" u 2 : 3 w l title \"Dew\" , \\ \"\" index \"bubble\" u 2 : 3 w l t \"Bubble\" , \\ \"\" index \"Critical\" u 1 : 2 w p pt 7 lc rgb \"black\" t \"CP\" Gives the following plot: Related routines Routine Boundary traced Fixed quantity pt_envelope_2ph P-T at constant composition Composition px_envelope_2ph P-x at constant temperature Temperature and a feed/injection pair tx_envelope_2ph T-x at constant pressure Pressure and a feed/injection pair pt_envelope_3ph P-T for a three-phase line Composition and initial phase compositions The P-x and T-x routines take two compositions, z0 (the main phase) and z_injection (the injected fluid), plus a mixing parameter alpha0 . They are\nuseful for injection and leaching processes, where the composition of one\nphase changes along the path. Practical notes Start from a converged saturation point of the same kind as the branch you\n  want to follow. A bad seed is the most common cause of a short or empty\n  envelope. Reuse the previous point when you need a single point rather than a whole\n  curve: a saturation calculation with the previous y0 and pressure or\n  temperature as the initial guess is usually cheaper and more stable. The critical point is detected when the logarithm of the composition ratios\n  changes sign between two consecutive points; the crossing is interpolated and\n  the two phase kinds are swapped, which is what produces the branch change.\n  The interpolated point is reported in cps . If the trace crosses a critical\n  point without the sign change being detected, the branch change is missed.\n  Compare size(cps) with the number of branch changes you expect. Near a critical point the two phases become nearly identical and the solver\n  reduces its step size and jumps over the region. Points close to the critical\n  point are the least reliable ones in the set; treat them as indicative. The trace stops when the internal 2000 bar limit is reached, when the step size\n  collapses, when a point fails to converge, or when the requested number of\n  points is reached. A short envelope is not necessarily a wrong one. Check the branch labels and the phase fractions along the envelope. A\n  negative or inconsistent fraction means the trace left the physical branch. A liquid-liquid branch uses different root and step settings, and its\n  points are the least well tested part of the implementation. Verify each\n  point independently before using a liquid-liquid envelope. See example/basics/4_phase_envelope.f90 for a complete runnable program,\n  including how to write the envelope to a file.","tags":"","url":"page/usage/phase_equilibrium/envelopes.html"},{"title":"Stability analysis – yaeos","text":"Stability analysis Tangent-plane distance Finding the minimum Using stability analysis to initialize a flash Multiphase calculations Practical notes Next steps and reference Stability analysis A homogeneous mixture can satisfy the equation of state and still lower its\nGibbs energy by separating into phases of different compositions. Stability\nanalysis asks whether creating a small amount of a trial phase would help.\nIt is the appropriate first question when you do not know whether to run a\nflash, and can supply a useful initial composition for that flash. This is not the same as selecting a \"stable\" volume root at a fixed\ncomposition. Nor does a negative result tell you the final amount of each\nphase: a flash solves that material balance. yaeos provides two routines: tm : the reduced tangent-plane distance of a trial phase with a given\n  composition. min_tpd : search from several starting compositions and return the lowest\n  trial-phase distance found. Tangent-plane distance The tangent plane describes the chemical potentials of the homogeneous feed.\nA trial phase below that plane can reduce the total Gibbs energy. Let be normalized feed mole fractions, pressure in bar, temperature in\nK, and positive trial-phase mole amounts. tm evaluates the modified,\ndimensionless distance: where Here is a fugacity coefficient and is the feed’s\nreduced chemical-potential term. When , this reduces to\nthe ordinary normalized tangent-plane distance . A significantly negative value establishes instability of the homogeneous\nreference within the model. A nonnegative numerical search result means no destabilizing phase was found , not proof of a global minimum. The\ntrivial trial w=z has zero distance even far from a phase boundary. Fragment for an initialized model (place declarations before executable code): use yaeos , only : pr , tm real ( pr ) :: z ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: w ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: P real ( pr ) :: T real ( pr ) :: tpd z = [ 0.4_pr , 0.6_pr ] w = [ 0.8_pr , 0.2_pr ] P = 5 0.0_pr T = 30 0.0_pr tpd = tm ( model , z , w , P , T ) if ( tpd < 0.0_pr ) then print * , \"The feed is unstable\" end if tm accepts GeModel objects as well as ArModel objects. For a GeModel the\nactivity coefficients replace the fugacity coefficients, and pressure has no\neffect on the model. The optional d argument allows the caller to provide precomputed values of . This avoids recalculating the feed fugacity coefficients: Fragment for an ArModel ; also declare Vz , lnphi_z(size(z)) , and d(size(z)) : call model % lnphi_pt ( z , T = T , P = P , V = Vz , root_type = \"stable\" , lnPhi = lnphi_z ) d = log ( z ) + lnphi_z tpd = tm ( model , z , w , P , T , d = d ) Finding the minimum min_tpd minimizes with respect to the trial-phase mole numbers: subroutine min_tpd ( model , z , P , T , mintpd , w , all_minima ) class ( BaseModel ), target :: model real ( pr ), intent ( in ) :: z (:) real ( pr ), intent ( in ) :: P real ( pr ), intent ( in ) :: T real ( pr ), intent ( out ) :: w (:) real ( pr ), intent ( out ) :: mintpd real ( pr ), optional , intent ( out ) :: all_minima (:, :) end subroutine min_tpd The routine returns: Output Meaning mintpd Minimum tangent-plane distance w Trial-phase composition at the minimum all_minima Array of shape (nc,nc+1) : each row holds nc composition entries followed by its distance min_tpd starts once per component with that component enriched in trial\nmole amounts, then uses successive substitution. It normalizes each resulting\ntrial and reports the lowest normalized distance. all_minima exposes those\ntrials, including possible duplicates; they are not guaranteed distinct or\nfully converged minima. There is no returned convergence flag. An (nc,nc) array is too small: the implementation writes the distance in column nc+1 . program stability_example use yaeos , only : pr , CubicEoS , PengRobinson76 , min_tpd use , intrinsic :: ieee_arithmetic , only : ieee_is_finite implicit none type ( CubicEoS ) :: model real ( pr ) :: tc ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: pc ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: w ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: z ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: P real ( pr ) :: T real ( pr ) :: mintpd real ( pr ) :: wtrial ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: minima ( 2 , 3 ) tc = [ 19 0.564_pr , 42 5.12_pr ] pc = [ 4 5.99_pr , 3 7.96_pr ] w = [ 0.0115478_pr , 0.200164_pr ] model = PengRobinson76 ( tc , pc , w ) z = [ 0.4_pr , 0.6_pr ] P = 5 0.0_pr T = 30 0.0_pr call min_tpd ( model , z , P = P , T = T , mintpd = mintpd , w = wtrial , all_minima = minima ) if (. not . ieee_is_finite ( mintpd )) error stop \"Nonfinite stability result\" if (. not . all ( ieee_is_finite ( wtrial ))) error stop \"Nonfinite trial phase\" print * , \"Minimum TPD: \" , mintpd print * , \"Most unstable trial phase: \" , wtrial if ( mintpd < - 1.0e-6_pr ) then print * , \"The feed is unstable: a flash should be performed\" else print * , \"No significant negative distance found in this search\" end if end program stability_example Using stability analysis to initialize a flash The normalized w is a trial phase, not necessarily a final equilibrium\ncomposition. The following fragment uses it as the initial y estimate relative\nto feed z as x . It requires strictly positive z ; reverse the phase roles\n( k0=z/w ) when necessary for the desired heavy/light assignment. Add imports\nfor flash and EquilibriumState , declare result and integer iters first: call min_tpd ( model , z , P = P , T = T , mintpd = mintpd , w = w ) if ( mintpd < - 1.0e-6_pr ) then result = flash ( model , z , T , p_spec = P , k0 = w / z , iters = iters ) end if This is especially useful for: Liquid-liquid equilibria, where the Wilson correlation may be a poor initial\n  guess. Systems near a critical point, where the two phases have similar\n  compositions. Activity-coefficient models, where the initial K-factors are mandatory. Multiphase calculations pt_mp_flash uses stability analysis internally: it tests the feed, solves\na two-phase flash, and then tests the resulting phase compositions, adding one\nmore phase if the reference phase is still unstable. It does not iterate until\nglobal stability is proved, and its no-split path is not usable. The process,\nand its limitations, are described on the multiphase flash page. Practical notes A truly globally stable feed is not metastable. A feed reported as stable\n  by a finite search may be metastable if a lower-energy split was missed.\n  Inspect the trials and compare independent initializations/model data when\n  phase behavior is complicated. For an ArModel , the trial phase is evaluated with the stable volume root.\n  For liquid-liquid calculations, a separate check with the liquid root may be\n  necessary. The distance is dimensionless. The example’s 1e-6 threshold is an\n  illustrative numerical screen, not an adjustable solver tolerance or a\n  universal physical threshold. The routine’s own stopping tests can limit\n  resolution near zero. Near-zero values may mean a boundary, criticality, convergence to the\n  trivial feed trial, or numerical noise. Check trial/feed differences and\n  neighboring states before interpreting them. Feed and trial logarithms require positive fractions. Remove absent\n  components consistently from the model rather than passing exact zeros to\n  this implementation. min_tpd is also available for GeModel objects. In that case the pressure\n  is only used for the calling convention and does not enter the activity\n  coefficient model. Next steps and reference Use an unstable trial to initialize a flash , then verify the split’s\nfugacity and balance residuals. A negative stability test on either returned\nphase can signal a missing phase; see the limitations on the multiphase page . Michelsen and Mollerup, Thermodynamic Models: Fundamental and Computational\nAspects , Tie-Line Publications (2004), is the formulation referenced by the\nimplementation ( review DOI ).","tags":"","url":"page/usage/phase_equilibrium/stability.html"},{"title":"Critical points – yaeos","text":"Critical points Theory Critical-point specification critical_point Example critical_line get_critical_constants Practical notes Critical points A critical point is the state where two coexisting phases become\nindistinguishable: their compositions, molar volumes, and densities converge,\nand the boundary between them disappears. For a pure substance it is a single\npoint in pressure-temperature space. For a mixture it also depends on\ncomposition, so the critical states form a critical line in\npressure-temperature-composition space. Near a critical point the usual “which phase is this?” questions stop having\nsharp answers. That is why critical calculations are useful: they mark the end\nof a phase envelope, and they explain why a flash or a saturation solver becomes\nill-conditioned in that region. yaeos provides: critical_point : a single critical point at a specified composition. critical_line : a critical line between two limiting compositions. get_critical_constants : pure-component critical constants of a model. Theory For a pure fluid, the critical point is the point where the pressure-volume\ncurve develops an inflection: For a mixture, composition fluctuations matter as well as changes in density;\nthe pure-fluid pressure-inflection test is not a general mixture criticality\ncriterion. Write the total Helmholtz energy as an ideal-gas contribution plus\na residual (real-fluid) contribution, The mixture loses stability when the matrix of second derivatives of the total Helmholtz energy with respect to the component mole numbers becomes\nsingular: The ideal contribution is not optional. In the Hessian divided by , it\nsupplies the positive diagonal elements . An ideal gas with positive\ncomponent amounts is stable against these fluctuations: this term does not\nmake it unstable at low temperature. In a real fluid, the residual contribution\ncan offset that stabilizing curvature. Using the residual Hessian alone would\ntherefore test a different, generally incorrect condition. This is how the implementation proceeds. The fugacity derivative returned by lnfug_vt satisfies that is, the ideal-mixing term is already included. The stability matrix built\nfrom it is and the implementation selects the eigenvalue closest to zero in absolute\nvalue, denoted here. This selection is an implementation choice;\nit is not by itself a test that all other eigenvalues are nonnegative.\nThe implementation evaluates at\nthe state and along a small composition perturbation , where is\nthe eigenvector associated with the selected eigenvalue. The solver imposes plus the pressure identity and the requested\nspecification. The second condition is evaluated by a central difference with , and the whole system is solved by Newton iteration. Critical-point specification Critical-point calculations solve a system of equations involving the\nstability limit and the criticality conditions. One variable must be\nspecified. The spec_CP object provides the allowed specifications: Field Meaning spec_CP%a Composition parameter spec_CP%V Volume spec_CP%T Temperature spec_CP%P Pressure The composition of the mixture is defined as: where and are the two limiting compositions. With and , is simply the mole fraction of the\nsecond component. With more general limiting compositions, is a\nmixing parameter and the actual mole fractions are the linear combination above. critical_point type ( EquilibriumState ) function critical_point ( & model , z0 , zi , spec , S , max_iters , V0 , T0 , a0 , P0 & ) class ( ArModel ), intent ( in ) :: model real ( pr ), intent ( in ) :: z0 (:) !! First limiting composition real ( pr ), intent ( in ) :: zi (:) !! Second limiting composition integer , intent ( in ) :: spec !! spec_CP field real ( pr ), intent ( in ) :: S !! Specification value integer , intent ( in ) :: max_iters real ( pr ), optional , intent ( in ) :: V0 real ( pr ), optional , intent ( in ) :: T0 real ( pr ), optional , intent ( in ) :: a0 real ( pr ), optional , intent ( in ) :: P0 end function critical_point The specification value S follows the natural variables of the solver, : For spec_CP%a , S is the composition parameter itself, in . For spec_CP%T , S is , with in kelvin. For spec_CP%P , S is , with in bar. For spec_CP%V , S is , with in L/mol. The V0 , T0 , P0 , and a0 arguments are initial guesses, given in the\nordinary units (K, bar, L/mol, and respectively), not as logarithms.\nOnly the specified variable S uses the logarithmic convention. Passing a\ntemperature in kelvin as S for spec_CP%T therefore produces a nonsense\nstate rather than an error. Example program critical_example use yaeos , only : pr , CubicEoS , PengRobinson76 , EquilibriumState use yaeos , only : critical_point , spec_CP implicit none type ( CubicEoS ) :: model type ( EquilibriumState ) :: cp real ( pr ) :: tc ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: pc ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: w ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: z0 ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: zi ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: a ! Carbon dioxide + n-hexane tc = [ 30 4.21_pr , 50 7.6_pr ] pc = [ 7 3.83_pr , 3 0.25_pr ] w = [ 0.223621_pr , 0.301261_pr ] model = PengRobinson76 ( tc , pc , w ) z0 = [ 1.0_pr , 0.0_pr ] ! Pure carbon dioxide zi = [ 0.0_pr , 1.0_pr ] ! Pure n-hexane a = 0.5_pr ! Equimolar mixture cp = critical_point ( & model , z0 , zi , spec = spec_CP % a , S = a , max_iters = 1000 , a0 = a & ) print * , \"Critical temperature: \" , cp % T , \" K\" print * , \"Critical pressure: \" , cp % P , \" bar\" print * , \"Critical volume: \" , cp % Vx , \" L/mol\" print * , \"Critical composition: \" , cp % x print * , \"Iterations: \" , cp % iters end program critical_example The result is an EquilibriumState with kind=\"critical\" , x == y == z ,\nand Vx == Vy . beta is zero. With the parameters above the calculation\nconverges in a few tens of Newton iterations to about 461.2 K and 76.4 bar. critical_point has no separate failure flag: kind is set to \"critical\" when the iteration ends, whether or not the residuals are small. Check the\nreturned state instead: the pressure must be positive and finite, the\ncomposition must be normalized and nonnegative, and the result must be\nconsistent with an independent calculation. critical_line critical_line continues a critical point along the composition parameter . It uses the same continuation method as the phase envelopes. type ( CriticalLine ) function critical_line ( & model , a0 , z0 , zi , ns0 , S0 , dS0 , & v0 , t0 , p0 , max_points , maxP , first_point , stability_analysis & ) class ( ArModel ), intent ( in ) :: model real ( pr ), intent ( in ) :: a0 !! Initial alpha real ( pr ), intent ( in ) :: z0 (:) real ( pr ), intent ( in ) :: zi (:) integer , intent ( in ) :: ns0 !! Specification variable real ( pr ), intent ( in ) :: S0 !! Initial specification value real ( pr ), intent ( in ) :: dS0 !! Initial step real ( pr ), optional , intent ( in ) :: v0 real ( pr ), optional , intent ( in ) :: t0 real ( pr ), optional , intent ( in ) :: p0 integer , optional , intent ( in ) :: max_points real ( pr ), optional , intent ( in ) :: maxP type ( EquilibriumState ), optional , intent ( in ) :: first_point logical , optional :: stability_analysis end function critical_line ns0 is usually spec_CP%a . In that case S0 is the initial and dS0 is the step in . A negative dS0 scans in the opposite\ndirection. The optional first_point allows the continuation to start from an\nalready converged critical point; without it the routine starts from an\ninitial guess built from the component critical constants and a volume\nestimate. When ns0 is not spec_CP%a , S0 follows the same logarithmic convention as critical_point : log(T) , log(P) , or log(V) . The optional first_point is used for two things: the volume, temperature and\npressure guesses, and the composition parameter, which is taken from first_point%x(2) . That second element is the mole fraction of the second\ncomponent only for a binary mixture whose limiting compositions are the pure\ncomponents. For other mixtures, set the starting point with a0 and S0 instead. use yaeos , only : CriticalLine , critical_line , critical_point , spec_CP type ( CriticalLine ) :: cl type ( EquilibriumState ) :: cp real ( pr ) :: a a = 0.5_pr cp = critical_point ( model , z0 , zi , spec = spec_CP % a , S = a , max_iters = 1000 , a0 = a ) cl = critical_line ( & model , a0 = a , z0 = z0 , zi = zi , & ns0 = spec_CP % a , S0 = a , dS0 = 0.01_pr , & first_point = cp , max_points = 1000 & ) print * , \"Number of points: \" , size ( cl % a ) print * , \"Alpha range: \" , cl % a ( 1 ), cl % a ( size ( cl % a )) The CriticalLine type contains: Field Meaning a Composition parameter at each point z0 , zi Limiting compositions P Pressure [bar] V Molar volume [L/mol] T Temperature [K] ns Specification variable used at each point iters Iterations used at each point CEP Critical end point, when one is found The number of points is decided by the continuation itself: it stops when the\nline turns, when a point fails to converge, when the pressure exceeds maxP (default 2500 bar), or when max_points is reached. An empty line is a\nlegitimate outcome and means the starting point was not usable, not that the\nmixture has no critical behaviour. Starting from a converged critical point\nmatters: with the example above, a continuation started from a poor point\nreturned an empty line. CEP is only filled when stability_analysis is requested and a critical end\npoint is actually detected. The detection routine is limited to binary\nmixtures and scans a fixed grid of trial compositions, so a null CEP is not\nevidence that no critical end point exists. get_critical_constants get_critical_constants calculates the pure-component critical constants of\nan ArModel and updates the model’s components object in place: use yaeos__equilibria_critical , only : get_critical_constants call get_critical_constants ( model ) ! The critical constants are now available in the model print * , model % components % Tc print * , model % components % Pc print * , model % components % w This routine is not re-exported by the top-level yaeos module; it must be\nimported from the implementation module yaeos__equilibria_critical as shown\nabove. Use it only with a model that has already allocated Tc , Pc , and w for its components. The routine solves a critical point for each pure component, then estimates\nthe acentric factor from the saturation pressure at : It is used internally by models such as PC-SAFT, where the critical constants\nare not known analytically and must be obtained from the equation of state\nitself. Practical notes Critical-point calculations are sensitive to the initial guesses. Use a0 , T0 , P0 , and V0 when they are available. Specify temperature or pressure in preference to volume. A run with spec_CP%V for the example mixture returned a state with a pressure of\n  order bar, which is a converged iteration of the wrong problem. The specification value is logarithmic for spec_CP%T , spec_CP%P , and spec_CP%V ; the initial guesses are not. critical_point does not report convergence failure. Validate the returned\n  temperature, pressure, and composition, and compare with a second\n  specification when possible. A critical line can cross a critical end point or a liquid-liquid region.\n  Check the CEP field and the stability of the calculated points. The critical line and the phase envelope should meet at the critical point.\n  Use this as a consistency check. Near a critical point the two phases are nearly identical, so the\n  saturation and flash routines lose their conditioning: phase fractions,\n  volumes, and compositions all become uncertain at the same time. Treat\n  results close to a critical point as qualitative. Critical calculations are expensive because they require second and third\n  derivatives of the Helmholtz energy through numerical differentiation of the\n  criticality conditions.","tags":"","url":"page/usage/phase_equilibrium/critical.html"},{"title":"Multiphase flash – yaeos","text":"Multiphase flash Theory pt_mp_flash Current wrapper behavior and limitations What the labels mean Example: gated exploratory call The MPEquilibriumState type Differences from two-phase flash Practical notes Multiphase flash Some mixtures split into more than two equilibrium phases. Common examples are\nwater-hydrocarbon systems, gas-condensate systems with a liquid-liquid split,\nand systems near a three-phase line. The routine pt_mp_flash calculates\nthe equilibrium phase distribution at fixed composition, pressure, and\ntemperature using stability-based initialization. The current automatic wrapper\nhas important limitations, described below; it is not an unrestricted global\nmultiphase-equilibrium solver. A ternary mixture has three components , but need not have three phases.\nConversely, two liquids can each contain all three components. Phase fractions\nare amounts of phases on a mole basis, not the fractions of individual\ncomponents. A water/hydrocarbon calculation also needs a model and interaction\nparameters appropriate for that mixture; a numerical split alone does not\nvalidate cubic-EoS predictions for associating liquids. Theory A multiphase equilibrium satisfies the same isofugacity condition as a\ntwo-phase flash, but for phases: together with the overall mass balance Here is the total number of phases, the number of\ncomponents, the mole fraction of component in phase ,\nand the fraction of total feed moles in that phase. Each\ncomposition sums to one. is a fugacity coefficient; equality\nof composition*fugacity coefficient at common P and T gives equal component\nfugacities. These equations define a candidate split, but do not establish\nthat no other split has lower Gibbs energy. The implementation stores one phase separately as the reference phase w . This is a mathematical reference, not necessarily a zero-amount phase:\nits fraction is generally finite after the flash. Internal ratios are K(l,i)=x_l(l,i)/w(i) , not necessarily the two-phase convention vapor/liquid . pt_mp_flash type ( MPEquilibriumState ) function pt_mp_flash ( model , z , P , T ) class ( ArModel ), intent ( in ) :: model real ( pr ), intent ( in ) :: z (:) !! Global composition real ( pr ), intent ( in ) :: P !! Pressure [bar] real ( pr ), intent ( in ) :: T !! Temperature [K] end function pt_mp_flash Current wrapper behavior and limitations The executable code currently: Calls min_tpd on the feed. It attempts a two-phase solve only if mintpd < -0.001 . Uses one liquid-root main phase and one vapor-root reference phase, solving\n   for log composition ratios and log phase fractions. Tests both resulting phase compositions. If the reference-phase test is\n   negative beyond the same threshold, it attempts one three-phase solve with\n   two liquid-root main phases and a vapor-root reference phase. It does not loop until all phases are stable, despite the workspace’s\nfour-row internal storage. It attempts at most three total phases. The\nthree-phase initialization also reuses the trial overwritten by the last\nstability call, so it requires independent verification. Do not use the no-split path as a valid single-phase result. When the first\nstability test is not sufficiently negative, the local w and betas arrays\nare not initialized before the code uses them to assemble the result. That\npath therefore has undefined behavior; inspecting np afterwards cannot make\nit safe. Gate the call with your own stability analysis and treat np < 1 as an error. There is no\npublic kind=\"failed\" or convergence status on MPEquilibriumState . The inner\nNewton solve has an iteration limit, but its final residual and phase-removal\nflags are not exposed by this wrapper. What the labels mean kinds_x and kind_w record the volume-root branch requested from the\nequation of state, not a verified physical phase identity. In the example\nbelow, the reference phase is butane-rich even though its requested branch is\nlabelled \"vapor\" . Use the labels to reproduce the calculation, rather than\ninferring phase identity from the label alone. Evaluate the molar volume and\nconsider the mixture’s phase diagram and stability. A pure component’s critical\ntemperature alone does not classify a phase of a mixture. Example: gated exploratory call This complete program checks feed instability first to avoid the known\nno-split path. It may deliberately skip the flash. It does not certify that a\nreturned split is globally stable; use the diagnostics and checks below. program multiphase_example use yaeos , only : pr , CubicEoS , PengRobinson76 , MPEquilibriumState , pt_mp_flash use yaeos , only : min_tpd use , intrinsic :: ieee_arithmetic , only : ieee_is_finite implicit none type ( CubicEoS ) :: model type ( MPEquilibriumState ) :: result real ( pr ) :: tc ( 3 ) real ( pr ) :: pc ( 3 ) real ( pr ) :: w ( 3 ) real ( pr ) :: z ( 3 ) real ( pr ) :: P real ( pr ) :: T real ( pr ) :: mintpd real ( pr ) :: trial ( 3 ) real ( pr ) :: reconstructed ( 3 ) real ( pr ) :: lnphi_ref ( 3 ) real ( pr ) :: lnphi_phase ( 3 ) integer :: i ! Water, methane, and n-butane tc = [ 64 7.14_pr , 19 0.564_pr , 42 5.12_pr ] pc = [ 22 0.64_pr , 4 5.99_pr , 3 7.96_pr ] w = [ 0.3443_pr , 0.0115478_pr , 0.200164_pr ] model = PengRobinson76 ( tc , pc , w ) z = [ 0.1_pr , 0.6_pr , 0.3_pr ] P = 5 0.0_pr T = 30 0.0_pr call min_tpd ( model , z , P , T , mintpd , trial ) if (. not . ieee_is_finite ( mintpd )) error stop \"Invalid stability result\" if ( mintpd >= - 0.001_pr ) then print * , \"No sufficiently negative TPD; skipping this wrapper.\" stop end if result = pt_mp_flash ( model , z , P , T ) if ( result % np < 1 ) error stop \"No usable split\" if (. not . allocated ( result % betas )) error stop \"Missing phase fractions\" if ( size ( result % betas ) /= result % np + 1 ) error stop \"Unexpected fraction count\" if (. not . all ( ieee_is_finite ( result % betas ))) error stop \"Invalid fractions\" if (. not . all ( ieee_is_finite ( result % w ))) error stop \"Invalid reference phase\" if (. not . all ( ieee_is_finite ( result % x_l ))) error stop \"Invalid main phases\" print * , \"Total phase count: \" , result % np + 1 print * , \"Reference phase: \" , result % kind_w , result % w print * , \"Reference fraction: \" , result % betas ( result % np + 1 ) reconstructed = result % betas ( result % np + 1 ) * result % w call model % lnphi_pt ( result % w , P , T , root_type = result % kind_w , lnPhi = lnphi_ref ) do i = 1 , result % np print * , \"Phase \" , i , \" kind: \" , result % kinds_x ( i ) print * , \"Phase \" , i , \" composition: \" , result % x_l ( i , :) print * , \"Phase \" , i , \" fraction: \" , result % betas ( i ) reconstructed = reconstructed + result % betas ( i ) * result % x_l ( i , :) call model % lnphi_pt ( result % x_l ( i , :), P , T , root_type = result % kinds_x ( i ), & lnPhi = lnphi_phase ) print * , \"Phase \" , i , \" max log-fugacity mismatch: \" , & maxval ( abs ( log ( result % x_l ( i , :)) + lnphi_phase - log ( result % w ) - lnphi_ref )) end do print * , \"Fraction sum: \" , sum ( result % betas ) print * , \"Maximum component-balance error: \" , maxval ( abs ( reconstructed - z )) end program multiphase_example With the parameters above this run reports np=2 , that is three phases in\ntotal: two liquid-root main phases and one \"vapor\" -root reference phase.\nThe phase fractions sum to one and the reconstructed feed matches the input\nto about ; the log-fugacity mismatches against the reference phase\nare of order . Treat those numbers as evidence that the solver\nconverged to a consistent split, not that it found the globally stable one. The MPEquilibriumState type The result is a MPEquilibriumState with: Field Meaning z Global composition P , T Pressure [bar] and temperature [K] np Number of main phases, excluding the reference; a valid split has np+1 total phases x_l Mole fractions of the main phases, x_l(phase, component) w Mole fractions of the reference phase betas Mole fractions of the main phases, followed by the reference-phase fraction at index np+1 kinds_x Kind of each main phase ( \"liquid\" or \"vapor\" ) kind_w Kind of the reference phase The reference phase starts from a stability trial but is a full phase in the\nflash result. The main phases have compositions stored in x_l . The phase\nfractions in betas(1:np) refer to the main phases, and betas(np+1) is the\nreference-phase fraction. The sum of all np+1 fractions is one. Differences from two-phase flash flash is restricted to two phases. A kind=\"failed\" flash is not proof\n  that the feed is single-phase; it can also mean poor initialization or a\n  numerical failure. np=1 means two total phases and np=2 means three. np=0 marks the\n  no-split branch, which is not a usable single-phase result in the current\n  implementation. pt_mp_flash only supports ArModel models. Activity-coefficient models are\n  not supported. The multiphase flash is more expensive because it performs repeated stability\n  analyses and solves a larger system of equations. Practical notes Check array sizes, finite nonnegative compositions and fractions, composition\n  sums, sum(betas)=1 , and the reconstructed feed. A small balance error alone\n  does not show fugacity equality: the reconstruction formula builds balance\n  into the solver variables. For a two-phase system, pt_mp_flash should agree with flash within the\n  convergence tolerance. Use this as a consistency check. Evaluate log fugacities for every phase against the reference using the\n  returned root labels. Check that each component’s mismatch is small, and\n  test the returned phases for additional destabilizing trials. Near a three-phase boundary, fractions can approach zero and small changes\n  in P or T can change phase count. Scan with small steps and compare against\n  independent calculations. This wrapper accepts no previous-state or k0 argument, so it cannot directly reuse a previous split as initialization. Stability uses stable volume roots for its trials, but the subsequent flash\n  uses the fixed liquid/liquid/vapor root choices described above. It is not\n  a general liquid-liquid-only or four-phase automatic solver. For phase diagrams with three or more phases, combine pt_mp_flash with the\n  critical-line and envelope routines to locate the boundaries between regions.","tags":"","url":"page/usage/phase_equilibrium/multiphase.html"},{"title":"Adding your own models – yaeos","text":"Most thermodynamic properties calculated in yaeos depend heavily on different\nkinds of models and their derivatives. Obtaining the derivatives of complex\nmodels can be tedious and error-prone, so yaeos provides three ways to add a\nnew model: Analytical derivatives, implemented by hand. Automatic differentiation with hyperdual numbers. Source-to-source differentiation with Tapenade. These are implementation choices, not different thermodynamic models. Begin\nwith the same energy equation and choose how to obtain its derivatives. Configure first, extend only when necessary Why start from an energy and its derivatives? Residual Helmholtz models A validation ladder Guides Where this fits in the codebase Example models Configure first, extend only when necessary You do not need a new derived type to change critical constants, interaction\nparameters, or parameters of an existing activity model. Start with the equations of state and excess Gibbs models . For a cubic EOS, an existing\nalpha function or mixing rule may already express your intended change.\nExtending just that component is usually smaller and easier to test than\nrewriting the whole EOS. Implement a new model when the energy equation itself is missing, or when you\nneed a genuinely new functional form. Before doing so, you should be able to: Construct an existing model and calculate pressure at specified n, V, T . Distinguish total volume from molar volume and residual from ideal-gas energy. Read a Fortran module, a derived type, a type-bound procedure, and an optional\n   dummy argument. The style guide explains\n   the conventions used here. State the new equation’s parameters, units, validity range, and limiting cases. A useful beginner path is to reproduce the analytic van der Waals example , test its derivatives, then\ntranslate the primal energy into hyperdual arithmetic. Only then tackle a more\ncomplex model or a Tapenade generation workflow. Keep the first tests at a\nsingle, well-behaved state; phase-equilibrium solvers are not derivative debuggers. Why start from an energy and its derivatives? An EOS provides an extensive residual Helmholtz energy : the departure from the ideal gas at the same n, V, T . For example, Mole derivatives supply residual chemical potentials; second derivatives supply\nproperty sensitivities and the Jacobians used by nonlinear solvers. Deriving\nthese quantities from a common energy helps preserve thermodynamic identities.\nDo not add the ideal-gas pressure inside residual_helmholtz : the inherited pressure method already adds it. An activity model instead supplies extensive excess Gibbs energy , relative to an ideal solution, with . It is not an EOS and does not independently\nprovide pressure or a liquid density. Its abstract base is GeModel ;\nimplement its excess_gibbs subroutine with the optional energy, temperature,\nand mole derivatives in src/models/excess_gibbs/ge_models.f90 .\nThere is no get_v0 obligation for a GeModel . Both energies are returned in bar L , not joules, molar energy, or energy\ndivided by RT . Use R supplied by yaeos ; 1 bar L is 100 J. Inputs are\ncomponent mole amounts [mol], total volume [L] for an EOS, and temperature [K].\nUsing mole fractions as n is a one-mole basis, not permission to normalize n internally. A composition derivative changes one mole amount while holding\nthe other mole amounts fixed, not while forcing their sum to one. Residual Helmholtz models Residual Helmholtz models are the basis to obtain the residual properties. The main basis in yaeos is the abstract type ArModel , which enforces the\nexpected functionality of this kind of model. Abstract means that it\ndescribes a contract, not a model you can instantiate. A concrete type stores\nparameters and supplies all deferred procedures in that contract. An abstract\ninterface specifies the procedure kind (function or subroutine), argument\nnames, types, ranks, and attributes. The compiler checks your bindings against\nit; matching just the procedure name is not enough. This deliberately incomplete sketch illustrates inheritance, not a compilable\nconcrete model: use yaeos , only : ArModel type , abstract , extends ( ArModel ) :: MyNewModel end type The definition of an ArModel requires two procedures: abs_residual_helmholtz : calculates the residual Helmholtz\n  energy and its derivatives. abs_volume_initializer : provides an initial volume to find a\n  liquid root at a given pressure and temperature. use yaeos , only : ArModel type , extends ( ArModel ) :: MyNewModel contains procedure :: residual_helmholtz => an_Ar_implementation procedure :: get_v0 => an_v0_implementation end type The binding sketch above needs module procedures with the exact interfaces.\nOnce those procedures and a parameter constructor are defined, the model can\nuse inherited methods (the following is a usage fragment): use my_model , only : MyNewModel type ( MyNewModel ) :: model ! Assuming the model parameters are set up call model % pressure ( n , V , T , P ) type(MyNewModel) declares that specific implementation; class(ArModel), allocatable can hold any concrete extension of ArModel .\nThat polymorphism lets the same flash or property routine work with different\nEOS implementations without knowing their parameter layout. A validation ladder Before trusting a new model, check in this order: Primal equation: units, parameter dimensions, admissible logarithms and\n   denominators, pure-component and low-density limits. Derivatives: finite differences over several step sizes, Hessian symmetry,\n   and each optional output requested alone as well as together. Extensivity: at fixed T , scaling both n and V by a positive factor\n   must scale Ar by that factor and leave pressure unchanged. For Ge , scale n only: Ge scales and activity coefficients stay unchanged. Thermodynamic identities: collect the consistency residuals rather than\n   assuming that a call without outputs prints a pass/fail report. Solvers: test volume roots before saturation, flash, or envelopes; a\n   converged result does not establish that the physical equation is correct. See the analytic guide for executable checks and how to interpret failures. Guides Analytical derivatives shows how to implement the residual_helmholtz and get_v0 procedures by hand, with a complete\n  van der Waals example. Automatic differentiation shows how to obtain the derivatives\n  with hyperdual numbers and with Tapenade. Excess Gibbs models describes how to add an\n  activity coefficient model. Where this fits in the codebase These pages teach the model author’s side of the work. The developer handbook covers the surrounding\ncodebase: the repository map, the Fortran contracts, the numerical algorithms,\nthe build and test workflow, and the language-interface layers. Read it when\nyou need to know where something lives or how to build and test the\nproject, not only how to write the energy equation. A few handbook pages pair directly with this guide: Need Handbook page Where a model, solver, or interface lives Architecture Exact interface rules and optional-output handling Fortran core Turning a derivative check into a regression test Testing Build, test, and documentation commands Workflow Debugging a solver that will not converge Numerical algorithms Example models The source tree contains complete implementations that can be used as\nreferences: src/models/residual_helmholtz/cubic/ for cubic equations of state. src/models/residual_helmholtz/multifluid/gerg2008.f90 for a multifluid\n  model with hyperdual automatic differentiation. src/models/residual_helmholtz/saft/pc_saft.f90 for a Tapenade-generated\n  model. src/models/excess_gibbs/ for activity-coefficient models. The architecture page of the developer handbook places these in the type hierarchy and explains how a user call reaches them.\nThe testing handbook shows how the shipped\nmodels are checked, which is the pattern to copy for a new one.","tags":"","url":"page/usage/newmodels/index.html"},{"title":"Analytical derivatives – yaeos","text":"Adding a model with analytical derivatives The ArModel contract Units and differentiation variables Implementation sequence Example: van der Waals equation Validating a new model Diagnosing failures Notes and recommendations Adding a model with analytical derivatives The thermodynamic properties in yaeos are calculated from the residual\nHelmholtz energy and its derivatives. The most\ndirect way to add a new residual Helmholtz model is to implement those\nderivatives analytically. This gives control over shared intermediates and\nperformance, but requires checking every derivative, including mixed ones. The other available options are: Automatic differentiation with hyperdual numbers, described in Automatic differentiation . Source-to-source differentiation with Tapenade, also described in Automatic differentiation . The ArModel contract A residual Helmholtz model extends the abstract type ArModel and must\nimplement two deferred procedures: Procedure Interface Purpose residual_helmholtz abs_residual_helmholtz Calculate and its derivatives get_v0 abs_volume_initializer Provide an initial liquid volume for the volume solver use yaeos , only : ArModel , pr type , extends ( ArModel ) :: MyModel contains procedure :: residual_helmholtz => my_ar procedure :: get_v0 => my_v0 end type MyModel The residual_helmholtz procedure receives the mole numbers n , the total\nvolume V , and the temperature T . All the outputs are optional. The following\nderivatives can be requested: Output Meaning Ar ArV ArT ArV2 ArT2 ArTV Arn(i) ArVn(i) ArTn(i) Arn2(i,j) The implementation should calculate only the requested outputs. A common\npattern is: if ( present ( Ar )) Ar = ... if ( present ( ArV )) ArV = ... ! ... The get_v0 function supplies a volume scale that the library’s solvers use\nto find the liquid root. Read src/models/solvers/volume.f90 and src/models/residual_helmholtz/ar_models.f90 before choosing a value,\nbecause the two solver families use it differently: The generic ArModel%volume routine uses get_v0 as the initial guess for a Newton solve on volume, multiplied by a small factor. A value near the\n  dense-liquid volume works well. volume_michelsen uses the returned value as a covolume B in the\n  reduced coordinate zeta = B/V , requires 0 < B < V , and uses B to bound\n  the search interval. In both cases the value must be an extensive volume in liters: it has to\ngrow with the amount of material, because the solver’s volume is a total\nvolume. The cubic models return the mixture covolume computed by their mixing\nrule, and PC-SAFT returns the close-packed segment volume\n( src/models/residual_helmholtz/saft/pc_saft.f90 ). A model whose parameters\nare defined per mole must convert them to a total quantity before returning. For a model with a per-component covolume b , the total covolume is the\nnatural choice: function my_v0 ( self , n , P , T ) result ( v0 ) class ( MyModel ), intent ( in ) :: self real ( pr ), intent ( in ) :: n (:) real ( pr ), intent ( in ) :: P real ( pr ), intent ( in ) :: T real ( pr ) :: v0 v0 = sum ( n * self % b ) end function my_v0 Returning zero, a negative value, or a quantity that does not scale with n does not fulfill the contract. A molar value is the classic mistake: it looks\nright on a one-mole basis and then misleads the solver for any other amount.\nA wrong get_v0 shows up as a solver that fails to converge or that converges\nto the wrong branch, not as a wrong pressure, so test it directly: the volume\nfound for a doubled amount at the same density should also double, and both a\nliquid and a vapor root should be reachable at the same state. Units and differentiation variables n contains mole amounts [mol], V is total volume [L], and T is in K. Ar is in bar L; its derivatives carry those units divided by the units of\nthe differentiated variables. Thus ArV is in bar, ArT in bar L/K, Arn in bar L/mol, ArV2 in bar/L, and Arn2 in bar L/mol².\nThe array extents are size(n) or size(n), size(n) as appropriate. All mole derivatives hold the other mole amounts fixed. If you write the\nequation using mole fractions, differentiate x = n/sum(n) too. Likewise,\nusing molar volume internally requires differentiating v = V/sum(n) .\nNever normalize the input amounts and silently change the meaning of the\nderivatives. Implementation sequence Derive the extensive energy from the intended EOS and confirm the ideal-gas\n   reference. List all composition and temperature dependencies of parameters. Create the concrete type and constructor. Allocate parameter arrays, populate\n   the inherited components object, and check input sizes and physical ranges. Implement Ar first, then first derivatives, then second/mixed derivatives.\n   Use local intermediates rather than optional outputs as scratch storage. Implement the independent get_v0 function and test its scaling with n . Run the checks below before testing a phase-equilibrium calculation. Example: van der Waals equation The van der Waals equation can be written as: with the residual Helmholtz energy: where: and the pure-component parameters are: The cross parameter is .\nThis example assumes symmetric kij , temperature-independent aij and b ,\npositive critical constants, and matching array sizes. The constructor is kept\nshort for teaching; validate those assumptions in production code. In\nparticular, Di = 2*matmul(aij,n) uses symmetry. The parameters a and aij have units bar L²/mol², b has units L/mol, B is in L, and D is in bar L². The following module implements this model analytically: module vdw_model use yaeos , only : pr , R , ArModel , Substances implicit none type , extends ( ArModel ) :: VdW real ( pr ), allocatable :: a (:) !! Pure attractive parameters real ( pr ), allocatable :: b (:) !! Pure covolumes real ( pr ), allocatable :: aij (:, :) !! Cross attractive parameters contains procedure :: residual_helmholtz => vdw_ar procedure :: get_v0 => vdw_v0 end type VdW contains type ( VdW ) function setup_vdw ( tc , pc , kij ) result ( model ) real ( pr ), intent ( in ) :: tc (:) !! Critical temperatures [K] real ( pr ), intent ( in ) :: pc (:) !! Critical pressures [bar] real ( pr ), optional , intent ( in ) :: kij (:, :) !! Binary interaction parameters integer :: i integer :: j integer :: nc nc = size ( tc ) model % components = Substances ( tc = tc , pc = pc ) model % a = 2 7.0_pr / 6 4.0_pr * R ** 2 * tc ** 2 / pc model % b = R * tc / ( 8.0_pr * pc ) allocate ( model % aij ( nc , nc )) do i = 1 , nc do j = 1 , nc model % aij ( i , j ) = sqrt ( model % a ( i ) * model % a ( j )) if ( present ( kij )) then model % aij ( i , j ) = model % aij ( i , j ) * ( 1.0_pr - kij ( i , j )) end if end do end do end function setup_vdw subroutine vdw_ar (& self , n , V , T , Ar , ArV , ArT , ArTV , ArV2 , ArT2 , & Arn , ArVn , ArTn , Arn2 & ) class ( VdW ), intent ( in ) :: self real ( pr ), intent ( in ) :: n (:) real ( pr ), intent ( in ) :: V real ( pr ), intent ( in ) :: T real ( pr ), intent ( out ), optional :: Ar real ( pr ), intent ( out ), optional :: ArV real ( pr ), intent ( out ), optional :: ArT real ( pr ), intent ( out ), optional :: ArTV real ( pr ), intent ( out ), optional :: ArV2 real ( pr ), intent ( out ), optional :: ArT2 real ( pr ), intent ( out ), optional :: Arn ( size ( n )) real ( pr ), intent ( out ), optional :: ArVn ( size ( n )) real ( pr ), intent ( out ), optional :: ArTn ( size ( n )) real ( pr ), intent ( out ), optional :: Arn2 ( size ( n ), size ( n )) real ( pr ) :: nt real ( pr ) :: B real ( pr ) :: D real ( pr ) :: L real ( pr ) :: Di ( size ( n )) nt = sum ( n ) B = sum ( n * self % b ) D = dot_product ( n , matmul ( self % aij , n )) L = log ( 1.0_pr - B / V ) Di = 2.0_pr * matmul ( self % aij , n ) if ( present ( Ar )) Ar = - nt * R * T * L - D / V if ( present ( ArV )) ArV = - nt * R * T * B / ( V * ( V - B )) + D / V ** 2 if ( present ( ArT )) ArT = - nt * R * L if ( present ( ArTV )) ArTV = - nt * R * B / ( V * ( V - B )) if ( present ( ArV2 )) then ArV2 = nt * R * T * B * ( 2 * V - B ) / ( V ** 2 * ( V - B ) ** 2 ) - 2 * D / V ** 3 end if if ( present ( ArT2 )) ArT2 = 0.0_pr if ( present ( Arn )) then Arn = - R * T * L + nt * R * T * self % b / ( V - B ) - Di / V end if if ( present ( ArVn )) then ArVn = - R * T * B / ( V * ( V - B )) & - nt * R * T * self % b / ( V - B ) ** 2 + Di / V ** 2 end if if ( present ( ArTn )) ArTn = - R * L + nt * R * self % b / ( V - B ) if ( present ( Arn2 )) then Arn2 = R * T * ( spread ( self % b , 1 , size ( n )) & + spread ( self % b , 2 , size ( n ))) / ( V - B ) & + nt * R * T * spread ( self % b , 1 , size ( n )) & * spread ( self % b , 2 , size ( n )) / ( V - B ) ** 2 & - 2.0_pr * self % aij / V end if end subroutine vdw_ar function vdw_v0 ( self , n , P , T ) result ( v0 ) class ( VdW ), intent ( in ) :: self real ( pr ), intent ( in ) :: n (:) real ( pr ), intent ( in ) :: P real ( pr ), intent ( in ) :: T real ( pr ) :: v0 v0 = sum ( n * self % b ) end function vdw_v0 end module vdw_model With the model defined, all the inherited ArModel methods are available: program main use yaeos , only : pr use vdw_model , only : VdW , setup_vdw implicit none type ( VdW ) :: model real ( pr ) :: tc ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: pc ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: n ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: V real ( pr ) :: T real ( pr ) :: P tc = [ 19 0.0_pr , 42 5.0_pr ] pc = [ 4 6.0_pr , 3 8.0_pr ] model = setup_vdw ( tc , pc ) n = [ 0.4_pr , 0.6_pr ] V = 1.0_pr T = 30 0.0_pr call model % pressure ( n , V , T , P ) end program main Validating a new model Analytical derivatives are easy to get wrong. The consistency module contains different tools for numerical comparisons, independent optional-output\ncalls, and thermodynamic identities. Add these imports and declarations to the\nexample program above, before its executable statements: use yaeos__consistency_armodel , only : ar_consistency , & numeric_ar_derivatives , individual_ar_calls ! Declarations (after implicit none and the other declarations): real ( pr ) :: Ar_num real ( pr ) :: ArV_num real ( pr ) :: ArV_exact real ( pr ) :: eq31 real ( pr ) :: eq33 ( 2 , 2 ) real ( pr ) :: eq34 ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: eq36 real ( pr ) :: eq37 logical :: passed Then, after initializing model, n, V, T , run: call model % residual_helmholtz ( n , V , T , ArV = ArV_exact ) call numeric_ar_derivatives ( model , n , V , T , & d_n = 1.e-5_pr , d_v = 1.e-5_pr , d_t = 1.e-2_pr , & Ar = Ar_num , ArV = ArV_num ) print * , \"ArV: implemented, numerical\" , ArV_exact , ArV_num call individual_ar_calls ( model , n , V , T , passed ) print * , \"Independent optional outputs agree:\" , passed call ar_consistency ( model , n , V , T , & eq31 = eq31 , eq33 = eq33 , eq34 = eq34 , eq36 = eq36 , eq37 = eq37 ) print * , \"Identity residuals:\" , eq31 , maxval ( abs ( eq33 )), & maxval ( abs ( eq34 )), eq36 , eq37 numeric_ar_derivatives uses central differences of the primal energy. Ar is a required output, even when only a derivative is of interest. Request the\nother derivatives by keyword and compare each in turn. The example step sizes\nare starting choices in mol, L, and K, not universal tolerances. Try several\nsteps: truncation error dominates for large steps and cancellation for very\nsmall ones. All perturbed states must remain in the equation’s domain; do not\nlet a central mole perturbation produce negative amounts. individual_ar_calls checks whether each output agrees with the same output\nrequested alongside all the others. It does not check correctness against an\nindependent equation, and its comparisons use a strict absolute machine-epsilon\nthreshold. If it fails, inspect actual differences and optional-output handling\nbefore deciding whether this is roundoff or a defect. ar_consistency returns identity residuals that should approach zero; it does not print diagnostics or compare against finite differences. Calling it\nwithout eqXX outputs produces no visible report. The outputs test the\nfugacity/Gibbs relation ( eq31 ), composition-derivative symmetry ( eq33 ), a\nGibbs-Duhem sum ( eq34 ), and pressure ( eq36 ) and temperature ( eq37 )\nidentities. Their units and scales differ, so use scale-aware tolerances rather\nthan a single raw threshold. Start at positive pressure away from spinodals:\nthe helper evaluates fugacity expressions involving log(Z) and pressure-based\nsensitivities, which can be undefined or ill-conditioned elsewhere. Finally, verify Ar(lambda*n, lambda*V, T) = lambda*Ar(n,V,T) and unchanged\npressure for several positive lambda . A one-mole test alone cannot reveal\nall missing total-amount factors. Run across compositions and densities; only\nthen test liquid/vapor volume roots and equilibrium calculations. Diagnosing failures Symptom First things to check Pressure differs by a constant factor bar versus Pa, L versus m³, and molar versus total energy Pressure is correct but composition derivatives fail Differentiation of sum(n) , mole fractions, and mixing rules Temperature derivatives fail Missing temperature dependence of parameters, or differentiating a reduced energy without its RT factor Hessian is asymmetric Index order, asymmetric interaction inputs, and missing mixed terms Outputs fail only when requested alone Reading absent optional arguments or relying on another output being present NaN near high density V <= B , invalid logarithms, or an unsuitable volume initializer Flash fails after local checks pass Root selection, initial states, model validity, and conditioning near critical points Consistency is necessary but not sufficient: a consistently differentiated\nwrong equation can pass identities. Retain independent equation/parameter\nchecks and comparisons to justified reference data. Notes and recommendations Follow the derivative naming convention from the style guide : ArV , ArT , ArV2 , ArTV , Arn , ArVn , ArTn , and Arn2 . Use the R constant from yaeos__constants ; it is consistent with the\n  library units (bar, L, K, mol). Every model must carry a Substances object in components . This object\n  stores the critical constants, acentric factors, and critical volumes of the\n  components. If the model has an analytical covolume, implementing get_v0 as the total\n  covolume usually improves the liquid-root volume calculation. The optional outputs are independent. Avoid computing derivatives that were\n  not requested when the calculation is expensive. If the model is intended for phase-equilibrium calculations, all the second\n  derivatives listed above are required.","tags":"","url":"page/usage/newmodels/analtical.html"},{"title":"Automatic differentiation – yaeos","text":"Hyperdual autodiff ArModelAdiff Step-by-step hyperdual workflow Example Tapenade source-to-source AD What the yaeos wrapper needs Write and prepare the primal routine Generate the derivative source Review and integrate the result Seeds, adjoints, and repeated calls Validate the derivatives Choosing between the two approaches Implementing a new model and all its required derivatives can be tedious and\nerror-prone. Automatic differentiation (AD) avoids most of that work. Its\nruntime cost depends on the equation, derivative requests, and implementation;\nmeasure it before choosing an approach solely for speed. Automatic differentiation applies the chain rule to the operations in a program,\nso it computes derivatives of the implemented equations rather than\napproximating them with finite differences. Forward mode propagates directional\nderivatives from inputs to outputs; reverse mode propagates sensitivities back\nfrom outputs to inputs. Combining modes gives mixed and second derivatives.\nThis is useful here because thermodynamic properties and phase-equilibrium\ncalculations require several derivatives of the residual Helmholtz energy. In yaeos there are two ways to add a model with automatic differentiation:\noperator overloading with hyperdual numbers and source-to-source\ndifferentiation with Tapenade. @warn\nRemember to use the R constant from yaeos__constants . The base model already\nprovides a type(Substances) attribute named components ; initialize it rather\nthan redeclaring it. Return extensive energy in bar L, not Ar/(RT) or molar\nenergy. Inputs are mole amounts [mol], total volume [L], and temperature [K].\n@endwarn Hyperdual autodiff ArModelAdiff Automatic differentiation with hyperdual numbers is available through the ArModelAdiff derived type. A new model extends it and provides: Ar : a function returning the residual Helmholtz energy as a hyperdual value. get_v0 : a volume initializer. The interface of Ar is: type ( hyperdual ) function Ar ( self , n , v , t ) class ( ArModelAdiff ) :: self type ( hyperdual ), intent ( in ) :: n (:) !! Number of moles type ( hyperdual ), intent ( in ) :: v !! Volume [L] type ( hyperdual ), intent ( in ) :: t !! Temperature [K] end function Ar This is an interface sketch. In a concrete implementation, replace class(ArModelAdiff) by your concrete model type. Match the actual deferred\ninterface: its passed-object dummy has no intent attribute. In particular,\ndo not add intent(in) here just because the analytic API uses it. The ArModelAdiff type implements residual_helmholtz by evaluating Ar with hyperdual perturbations and extracting the first and second derivatives.\nThe model therefore gets all the derivatives required by the rest of the\nlibrary automatically. Step-by-step hyperdual workflow Write down the extensive primal equation and test its units and domain with\n   real arithmetic. AD differentiates what you wrote, not what you intended. Extend ArModelAdiff , store real-valued parameters, and bind its Ar function and the inherited real-valued get_v0 function . Import hyperdual_mod , including its overloaded operators and intrinsics.\n   Keep every intermediate depending on n , v , or t hyperdual-valued.\n   Converting it to a real discards the derivative information. Model constants\n   and inactive parameters may remain real. Express the energy with supported operations such as log , sqrt , sum ,\n   and arithmetic. An external real-only helper will not automatically become\n   differentiable: overload it or express its calculation in hyperdual form. Let the adapter manage seeds; request properties through the ordinary ArModel API. Test each derivative and optional-output combination using\n   the validation ladder . Branches and clipping deserve particular care. A branch selects the derivative\nof the executed path; a discontinuity does not acquire a meaningful smooth\nderivative simply because AD is used. Test both sides of regime changes. Example The following module implements a Peng-Robinson 1976 model with quadratic\nmixing rules: module hyperdual_pr76 use yaeos__constants , only : pr , R use yaeos__adiff_hyperdual_ar_api , only : ArModelAdiff use yaeos__substance , only : Substances use hyperdual_mod implicit none type , extends ( ArModelAdiff ) :: PR76 !! Peng-Robinson 1976 model with hyperdual derivatives. real ( pr ), allocatable :: kij (:, :) real ( pr ), allocatable :: lij (:, :) real ( pr ), allocatable :: ac (:) real ( pr ), allocatable :: b (:) real ( pr ), allocatable :: k (:) contains procedure :: Ar => arfun procedure :: get_v0 => v0 end type PR76 real ( pr ), parameter :: del1 = 1._pr + sqrt ( 2._pr ) real ( pr ), parameter :: del2 = 1._pr - sqrt ( 2._pr ) contains type ( PR76 ) function setup ( tc , pc , w , kij , lij ) result ( self ) !! Set up the model from critical constants and interaction matrices. real ( pr ), intent ( in ) :: tc (:) !! Critical temperatures [K] real ( pr ), intent ( in ) :: pc (:) !! Critical pressures [bar] real ( pr ), intent ( in ) :: w (:) !! Acentric factors real ( pr ), intent ( in ) :: kij (:, :) !! k_{ij} matrix real ( pr ), intent ( in ) :: lij (:, :) !! l_{ij} matrix self % components % tc = tc self % components % pc = pc self % components % w = w self % ac = 0.45723553_pr * R ** 2 * tc ** 2 / pc self % b = 0.07779607_pr * R * tc / pc self % k = 0.37464_pr + 1.54226_pr * w - 0.26993_pr * w ** 2 self % kij = kij self % lij = lij end function setup function arfun ( self , n , v , t ) result ( ar ) class ( PR76 ) :: self type ( hyperdual ), intent ( in ) :: n (:) type ( hyperdual ), intent ( in ) :: v type ( hyperdual ), intent ( in ) :: t type ( hyperdual ) :: ar type ( hyperdual ) :: amix type ( hyperdual ) :: a ( size ( n )) type ( hyperdual ) :: ai ( size ( n )) type ( hyperdual ) :: nij type ( hyperdual ) :: bmix type ( hyperdual ) :: b_v integer :: i integer :: j associate ( & ac => self % ac , b => self % b , k => self % k , & kij => self % kij , lij => self % lij , & tc => self % components % tc & ) ! Soave alpha function a = 1.0_pr + k * ( 1.0_pr - sqrt ( t / tc )) a = ac * a ** 2 ai = sqrt ( a ) ! Quadratic mixing rule amix = 0.0_pr bmix = 0.0_pr do j = 1 , size ( n ) - 1 do i = j + 1 , size ( n ) nij = n ( i ) * n ( j ) amix = amix + 2 * nij * ai ( i ) * ai ( j ) * ( 1 - kij ( i , j )) bmix = bmix + nij * ( b ( i ) + b ( j )) * ( 1 - lij ( i , j )) end do end do amix = amix + sum ( n ** 2 * a ) bmix = bmix + sum ( n ** 2 * b ) bmix = bmix / sum ( n ) b_v = bmix / v ar = ( & - sum ( n ) * log ( 1.0_pr - b_v ) & - amix / ( R * t * bmix ) * 1.0_pr / ( del1 - del2 ) & * log (( 1.0_pr + del1 * b_v ) / ( 1.0_pr + del2 * b_v )) & ) * ( R * t ) end associate end function arfun function v0 ( self , n , p , t ) result ( v0out ) !! Covolume initializer for the liquid root. class ( PR76 ), intent ( in ) :: self real ( pr ), intent ( in ) :: n (:) real ( pr ), intent ( in ) :: p real ( pr ), intent ( in ) :: t real ( pr ) :: v0out integer :: i integer :: j ! Same extensive covolume as in arfun, including lij. v0out = sum ( n ** 2 * self % b ) do j = 1 , size ( n ) - 1 do i = j + 1 , size ( n ) v0out = v0out + n ( i ) * n ( j ) * ( self % b ( i ) + self % b ( j )) & * ( 1.0_pr - self % lij ( i , j )) end do end do v0out = v0out / sum ( n ) end function v0 end module hyperdual_pr76 Use symmetric interaction matrices with zero diagonals for this example and\ncheck that the mixed covolume remains positive. The compact constructor assumes\nmatching array sizes and positive critical constants. get_v0 returns the\nsame total covolume used by arfun , including lij . Dividing the linear\nsum sum(n*b) by sum(n) would return a molar volume and only appear correct\non a one-mole basis. See the analytic guide for the default solver’s B/V transformation and domain obligations. Once the model is defined, it can be used like any other ArModel : program main use yaeos , only : pr , ArModel use hyperdual_pr76 , only : PR76 , setup implicit none class ( ArModel ), allocatable :: model real ( pr ) :: tc ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: pc ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: w ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: kij ( 2 , 2 ) real ( pr ) :: lij ( 2 , 2 ) real ( pr ) :: n ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: V real ( pr ) :: T real ( pr ) :: P tc = [ 19 0.0_pr , 31 0.0_pr ] pc = [ 1 4.0_pr , 3 0.0_pr ] w = [ 0.001_pr , 0.03_pr ] kij = reshape ([ 0.0_pr , 0.1_pr , 0.1_pr , 0.0_pr ], [ 2 , 2 ]) lij = kij / 2.0_pr model = setup ( tc , pc , w , kij , lij ) n = [ 0.3_pr , 0.7_pr ] V = 1.0_pr T = 15 0.0_pr call model % pressure ( n , V , T , P ) end program main A related complete implementation is available in example/extra/adiff_pr76.f90 . The example here makes the total-volume\ninitializer explicit; compare conventions rather than copying a molar\ninitializer unchanged into a new extensive model. Note The Ar function must be a function returning type(hyperdual) , not a\nsubroutine with an output argument. The return value is the residual Helmholtz\nenergy itself, not a derivative array. Tapenade source-to-source AD Tapenade reads a conventional Fortran routine and writes new Fortran routines\nthat evaluate derivatives. Unlike hyperdual numbers, the derivative operations\nare explicit in the generated source. This can improve runtime performance,\nbut requires a separate generation step and review of the generated code.\nTapenade must be installed and available as tapenade in PATH ; see the Tapenade download and user documentation . What the yaeos wrapper needs The abstract type ArModelTapenade adapts generated routines to the standard ArModel derivative interface. A concrete model supplies the primal routine ar and four derivative routines: Binding Role in the wrapper ar Evaluate the residual Helmholtz energy . ar_d Forward directional derivatives. ar_b Reverse-mode derivatives with respect to the input variables. ar_d_b Mixed forward/reverse derivatives, used for composition Hessians and mixed derivatives. ar_d_d Second forward directional derivatives. The routines have strict interfaces; use the library API and the working PC-SAFT\nimplementation as references rather than guessing argument order: src/adiff/autodiff_api/tapenade_ar_api.f90 and src/models/residual_helmholtz/saft/pc_saft.f90 .\nThe derived type must also implement the inherited get_v0 initializer, which\nprovides a liquid-volume starting estimate to the volume solver. Write and prepare the primal routine Start from tools/tapenade_diff/template.f90 in the repository.\nThe template is a scaffold, not a compilable model: replace its placeholders,\nimplement the model parameters and constructor, and write the primal routine\nwith the expected argument names and order: subroutine ar ( model , n , v , t , arval ) type ( ModelName ), intent ( in ) :: model real ( 8 ), intent ( in ) :: n (:) real ( 8 ), intent ( in ) :: v real ( 8 ), intent ( in ) :: t real ( 8 ), intent ( out ) :: arval ! Calculate A&#94;r(n, v, t) here. end subroutine ar The example uses real(8) because the generator workflow expects that kind in\nits input. Its post-processing script converts generated declarations to real(pr) . Keep the interface names unchanged: the script and Tapenade\ndirectives refer to them. The residual Helmholtz routine should compute the\nprimal value only; do not hand-code derivatives in ar . For derived-type parameters, the template recommends copying values to local\nvariables with explicit shapes based on size(n) . This gives Tapenade clear\nshape information during differentiation. Keep the routine deterministic and\navoid I/O, hidden state changes, and unsupported external calls in the\ndifferentiated calculation. Generate the derivative source Inspect the script before running it , in a disposable working copy. Its\npaths are relative to the current directory. The intended invocation from the\ngenerator directory is: cd tools/tapenade_diff\nbash gen_tapemodel.sh /absolute/path/to/your_model.f90 There is a path caveat in the checked-in script: ADFirstAidKitDIR is ../src/adiff/autodiff_api/tapenade , but from tools/tapenade_diff/ the source\ntree is at ../../src/adiff/autodiff_api/tapenade . Correct the helper path in\nyour working copy (or otherwise supply the expected layout) before invoking\nthe generator. The command above is not a promise of an unattended build. The script performs a sequence of tangent and reverse Tapenade passes and\npost-processes the result into tapeout/your_model_diff.f90 . It clears files\nin its tapeout/ directory before generating output. Save any work there\nelsewhere before running it. Check the script and the installed Tapenade version\nbefore use; this is a developer workflow, not a command to run on an existing\nproject output directory. The script also removes intermediate sources and\nTapenade message files; preserve diagnostics separately when investigating\ngeneration problems. It does not use fail-fast shell handling, so check every\npass for errors rather than treating the final file’s existence as success. The current pass sequence is tangent, nested tangent, third tangent,\nreverse-of-tangent, then reverse of the primal. The final file is renamed and\npost-processed: module flags are stripped, REAL*8 /kind suffixes are adjusted,\nderived-type dummy declarations are changed toward polymorphic class , and\nexternal declarations are removed. These textual substitutions are not a\nFortran interface checker; inspect the result, including use statements and\nthe availability of the Tapenade support interfaces. Review and integrate the result Generation is not the end of the process. Review the output against the wrapper\ninterfaces, then make the necessary project-specific fixes: Put the generated routines in a module that can access the model type and pr kind. Bind ar , ar_d , ar_b , ar_d_b , and ar_d_d to the derived type. Check that model is the passed-object argument and that all other dummy\n   arguments match the deferred interfaces exactly. Check array extents, kinds, and intent declarations; ensure the generated\n   code uses the correct component count and does not retain temporary\n   Tapenade-specific names or flags. Add the required get_v0 implementation and compile the model with the\n   library. tools/tapenade_diff/tapeout/pr_diff.f90 is a post-processed Peng-Robinson\nexample. The production PC-SAFT implementation\nis another reference, but its generated derivatives are integrated into a\nlarger module and should not be treated as raw Tapenade output. Seeds, adjoints, and repeated calls Normally call residual_helmholtz and let the adapter manage these details.\nWhen testing generated callbacks directly, follow src/adiff/autodiff_api/tapenade_ar_api.f90 exactly. The generated reference\nimplementation in tools/tapenade_diff/tapeout/pr_diff.f90 shows the\nconventions concretely. A tangent seed selects a direction. Zero all tangent inputs, then set, for\nexample, vd = 1 to obtain the volume derivative in arvald . Reverse sweeps consume their output seed. In the generated ar_b , the\nincoming energy seed arvalb is set to zero before the routine returns, and\nin ar_d_b both arvalb and arvaldb are zeroed. A seed value read after\nthe call is therefore not the value you passed in. Do not rely on how a particular generated routine initializes its input\nadjoints. In the checked-in ar_b routines, nb and tb are first assigned\nand then incremented within the routine (for example tb = tb + SUM(...) ),\nbut other generated code or a hand-edited copy may accumulate onto the incoming\nvalue instead. The safe rule is the one the wrapper follows: clear nb , vb , and tb before each independent reverse sweep,\nand set arvalb = 1 again before every ar_b call. For ar_d_b , clear both adjoint families and all unused seeds. Set a tangent\ndirection and arvaldb = 1 , leaving arvalb = 0 . With nd(i) = 1 , the\nwrapper reads a composition-Hessian row from nb ; ndb supplies the mole\ngradient. Volume and temperature adjoints provide the corresponding mixed\nderivatives. arvalb and arvaldb are not interchangeable. Nested forward calls use two seed directions: vd = vd0 = 1 gives ArV2 ; vd = 1 and td0 = 1 gives ArTV , with all other seeds zero. Read the\nsecond derivative from arvaldd , not from a first-tangent output. Do not assume a reverse sweep returns the primal energy. The wrapper calls\nthe primal routine separately when Ar is requested. All five Tapenade\nbindings are subroutines , unlike the hyperdual Ar function. The adapter’s reset_vars clears tangent, adjoint, mixed, and scalar workspace\nbefore independent sweeps. Preserve that behavior in custom wrappers. Nonzero\nstale adjoints can make a first call look correct but repeated calls or requests\nfor several derivatives fail. Test repeated calls and combined requests, not\nonly a single isolated Hessian evaluation. Validate the derivatives Compare generated derivatives with numeric_ar_derivatives at multiple\ncompositions, temperatures, and volumes; see the analytic guide for its required\nenergy output and finite-difference step choices. Thermodynamic identities are\na separate check. In a program with a declared scalar eq31 , for example: use yaeos__consistency_armodel , only : ar_consistency call ar_consistency ( model , n , V , T , eq31 = eq31 ) print * , \"Fugacity/Gibbs identity residual:\" , eq31 Collect all five identity outputs and check independent optional calls as shown\nin the analytic guide. Small identity residuals do not by themselves prove that\nthe derivatives match the intended primal equation or experimental data. Also test limiting cases (pure-component compositions, dilute components, and\nvolumes near the model’s lower limit) and verify that the model returns finite\npressures and properties before using it in flash or envelope calculations. Turn these checks into a permanent regression test once they pass. The testing page of the developer handbook shows how\nthe existing models are tested, which tolerances the suite uses, and how to\nchoose a step size that does not hide a wrong derivative. Choosing between the two approaches Hyperdual AD is usually simpler for prototyping and keeps the derivative\nimplementation close to the primal equations. Tapenade takes more setup and\nmaintenance, but can generate efficient routines for production models. Both\napproaches need validation; neither makes an incorrect primal equation\nthermodynamically correct.","tags":"","url":"page/usage/newmodels/autodiff.html"},{"title":"Python-API – yaeos","text":"Python API Choose the right starting point Conventions that prevent silent mistakes About this page Python API The Python package exposes the same models and phase-equilibrium routines as\nthe Fortran library, so you can build a model, evaluate properties, and\nsolve flashes from a Python script or notebook. Start with the Python tutorial and use the API reference for exact argument and return-value details. Choose the right starting point You want to know Start with The pressure or density of a fluid at given conditions An equation of state How nonideal a liquid mixture is (activity coefficients) An excess Gibbs model Whether a mixture separates into phases, and how much of each A phase-equilibrium calculation The equations, derivatives, and references behind a model The Fortran user documentation How the Python package is built and tested The developer handbook Conventions that prevent silent mistakes The property methods use kelvin , bar , litres , and moles ;\n  energies are reported in bar L. moles is an amount vector, while z in equilibrium calculations is an\n  overall mole-fraction vector that must sum to one. Keep the component order identical in every parameter array, composition\n  vector, and interaction matrix. The order used when the model was built is\n  the order the results come back in. Results named residual or excess are departures from a reference state,\n  not complete enthalpies or Gibbs energies. About this page This page is a placeholder for a FORD-only documentation build. In CI, the\nSphinx-generated Python documentation is copied over the whole page/python-api/ directory, so this page and its index.html are replaced\nby the Python tutorial and API reference listed above. If you are reading\nthis page in a local FORD build, the Python reference has not been copied\ninto that location yet. The sources of the Python documentation live in python/docs/source/ . Build\nthem with: cd python/docs\nsphinx-build -b html source build The result is a self-contained HTML tree whose entry point is build/index.html ; copy it to doc/ford_site/page/python-api/ to reproduce\nwhat CI publishes.","tags":"","url":"page/python-api/index.html"},{"title":"Becoming a yaeos developer – yaeos","text":"How to become a yaeos developer Your first week: a suggested route Pick the smallest useful change A feature can cross several layers What this handbook treats as authoritative Before asking for review How to become a yaeos developer This handbook is for someone who can use a model but now wants to understand\nthe implementation, fix a calculation, add a feature, or expose it to another\nlanguage. It covers the Fortran library and the interface layers around it.\nYou do not need to read every source file before making a useful contribution. If you have never run yaeos , first complete the user tutorial . For terminology such as residual energy,\nphase fraction, or volume root, use thermodynamics essentials . This handbook explains\nwhere those concepts live in the code and how to change them safely. Your first week: a suggested route Step Guide Practical outcome 1 Architecture and repository map Locate the implementation behind a public call 2 Development workflow Build and test a checkout rather than an installed release 3 Working in the Fortran core Understand types, bindings, derivative contracts, and exports 4 Working on numerical algorithms Separate model errors from residual, Jacobian, and solver errors 5 The C-facing interface layer Understand integer model handles and the actual interoperability boundary 6 The Python interface Follow a Python call through the compiled wrapper into Fortran 7 Tests and numerical validation Add a regression and check more than numerical convergence 8 An end-to-end change Plan a feature across implementation, interfaces, tests, and docs Pick the smallest useful change For a first contribution, choose a change with a clear expected result: a\nmissing explanation, an incorrect argument description, an additional\nregression test, or a small wrapper around an existing calculation. An entirely\nnew thermodynamic model is possible, but it adds both mathematical and\nsoftware-design questions at once. Write down three things before editing: The contract: what inputs, units, shapes, and output behavior should be\n   preserved or added? The evidence: what test or calculation will distinguish the old behavior\n   from the corrected behavior? The affected layers: is this a Fortran-only change, or must callers,\n   wrappers, Python classes, exports, and documentation also change? These questions keep a local fix from becoming an accidental API break. A feature can cross several layers Python user code\n    -> Python model method / result conversion\n    -> compiled f2py wrapper\n    -> yaeos_c module: handle lookup and argument adaptation\n    -> Fortran model or equilibrium entry point\n    -> property/derivative kernel and numerical routines A native Fortran program starts at the Fortran entry point and bypasses the\nPython layers. The directory name c_interface and use of iso_c_binding do not, on their own, establish that every routine is an independently callable\nstandard C ABI. The interface guide explains what the\ncurrent build actually exposes and what a new C client would require. What this handbook treats as authoritative The explanation follows the current repository’s code and build configuration.\nThe architecture discussion was informed by the author’s thesis material on\nmodularity, derivative adapters, and language interfaces. The thesis was used\nas read-only background, not copied into the repository or treated as an API\nspecification. Names, signatures, and interface mechanisms can evolve between\na research description and an implementation. Source comments and generated API pages help you navigate, but verify an\nimportant claim against the actual procedure body and its tests. In\nparticular, do not assume that every solver has the same failure convention,\nthat a declared optional argument is handled safely on every path, or that a\nmodel with mutable global support state is thread-safe. Before asking for review Keep implementation, regression tests, and documentation together. Explain\nwhich commands you actually ran and which checks were unavailable. Do not\nreport a Python build as tested merely because the native Fortran build\nsucceeded. The testing guide and the contribution guide provide the next steps.","tags":"","url":"page/developer/index.html"},{"title":"Architecture and repository map – yaeos","text":"Find your way around the codebase The repository in one table Public modules are entry points, not separate model implementations The model family tree Why use an energy-function contract? Trace a native pressure calculation Trace an equilibrium calculation The language boundary is another layer Choose the right place for a change A short source-reading exercise Find your way around the codebase The central design idea in yaeos is to keep a thermodynamic model separate\nfrom the calculations that use it. An equilibrium algorithm needs pressure,\nfugacity, or derivatives; it should not have to know whether they came from\nPeng–Robinson, GERG2008, or another compatible energy model. That separation is implemented through Fortran modules, abstract derived types,\nand type-bound procedures. This page explains those layers and gives a\nconcrete route from a user call to the mathematical kernel. The repository in one table Paths in this handbook are relative to the repository root. Path Responsibility Start here when… src/yaeos.f90 Top-level public imports A name is missing from use yaeos src/constants.f90 Precision, gas constant, selectors, shared settings Units, kinds, or root conventions matter src/models/ Model base types and implementations Adding or diagnosing property calculations src/equilibria/ Stability, saturation, flash, critical and boundary calculations A phase calculation is wrong or fails src/math/ Linear systems, nonlinear solvers, continuation, numerical helpers Diagnosing an algorithm’s numerical machinery src/adiff/ Hyperdual arithmetic and derivative adapters Implementing automatic differentiation src/consistency/ Derivative and thermodynamic consistency checks Validating a new energy function src/extra/ Example/test fluid fixtures Reusing a documented mixture src/legacy/ Older global-state implementations Maintaining an existing legacy caller c_interface/yaeos_c.f90 Flat wrapper procedures and model-handle storage Changing a language-facing call python/yaeos/ Python classes, conversion, fitting and tools Changing Python user behavior test/ and python/tests/ Native and Python regression tests Proving behavior across layers example/ Demonstration programs Learning a call sequence or adding a runnable example tools/ Development/generation utilities Regenerating differentiated code doc/page/ and python/docs/source/ FORD guides and Sphinx documentation Explaining a feature to users The actual compiled source sets differ by build. fpm.toml describes the\nnative package; python/meson.build adds the interface and extension build.\nFinding a file in the repository does not establish that every target builds\nit. See workflow for the separate build paths. Public modules are entry points, not separate model implementations src/yaeos.f90 imports broad API groups. src/models/models.f90 gathers model\ntypes and constructors; src/equilibria/equilibria.f90 gathers equilibrium\nroutines and result types. These modules are often called facades : they\nmake common names convenient to import without duplicating the underlying\ncalculation. For example, these are alternative import styles, not different flash\nalgorithms: use yaeos use yaeos__equilibria , only : flash The directory hierarchy is reflected in names such as yaeos__models_ar_genericcubic . These are ordinary modules. Do not confuse an\norganizational “submodule” in prose with the Fortran language’s specific submodule construct. When adding a public feature, check both its defining module’s visibility and\nthe relevant facade’s use ..., only: list. Implementing a procedure does not\nautomatically make it available through every facade or through Python. The model family tree Substances: component metadata\n    stored in BaseModel%components\n\nBaseModel\n    +-- ArModel: residual Helmholtz energy A&#94;r(n,V,T)\n    |     +-- CubicEoS\n    |     +-- ArModelAdiff -> hyperdual models, including Gerg2008\n    |     +-- ArModelTapenade -> source-differentiated models, including PCSAFT\n    |\n    +-- GeModel: excess Gibbs energy G&#94;E(n,T)\n          +-- analytical activity models, such as NRTL and UNIQUAC\n          +-- hyperdual/source-differentiation adapter families The tree shows implementation families, not a list of every concrete type. Substances is a container of arrays in component order, not a parent class\nof BaseModel . Why use an energy-function contract? An ArModel supplies residual Helmholtz energy and requested derivatives.\nShared methods use them to calculate pressure, fugacity coefficients, residual\nenthalpy, and other properties. A new compatible model can therefore reuse\nproperty relations and equilibrium algorithms instead of independently\nimplementing each property. GeModel follows the same idea for excess Gibbs energy. Its input does not\ninclude volume or pressure in the base energy interface. It is not an EOS\nimplementation with a few arguments omitted: it describes a different\nthermodynamic reference and different capabilities. The detailed Fortran contract guide explains which methods are\ndeferred, how optional derivatives work, and why extensive energy matters. Trace a native pressure calculation Consider a user program that constructs PengRobinson76 and calls model%pressure(n, V, T, P) . Follow this sequence in the source: Construction: src/models/residual_helmholtz/cubic/implementations/implementations.f90 defines PengRobinson76 . It configures a CubicEoS using component data,\n   pure-component parameters, an alpha function, and a mixing rule. Shared property: pressure in src/models/residual_helmholtz/ar_models.f90 requests the volume derivative\n   of residual Helmholtz energy and forms the pressure relation. Dynamic dispatch: the call to the model’s residual_helmholtz binding\n   reaches the cubic implementation in cubic/generic_cubic.f90 . The concrete\n   type, not the declared class(ArModel) variable, determines that binding. Model composition: the cubic implementation evaluates its alpha and\n   mixture parameters, then calls the algebraic energy kernel in cubic/base.f90 for the requested quantities. The same user pressure call on a hyperdual model goes through its AD adapter\ninstead of the cubic energy kernel. The shared pressure identity does not\nchange. This is the practical meaning of polymorphism in this library. Trace an equilibrium calculation For a saturation pressure, begin in src/equilibria/saturations_points.f90 (note the existing filename spelling). The routine organizes initialization,\nphase/root selection, iterations, and construction of EquilibriumState .\nLower-level simultaneous saturation equations live in src/models/solvers/saturation_point.f90 . Those equations call model property methods. They do not contain a separate\nPeng–Robinson thermodynamic implementation. If a residual is wrong, first\nidentify whether the error is in unpacking the solver variables, calling the\nright phase/root, evaluating model derivatives, or assembling the residual.\nChanging a generic Newton tolerance is rarely the first useful diagnosis. Phase boundaries add continuation: predict a nearby state, correct the\nequilibrium equations, choose a specification, and store the point. The src/equilibria/boundaries/ modules contain several specialized formulations;\nnot all of them use the same driver or unknown-vector layout. Read the\nspecific routine rather than assuming a single universal envelope algorithm. The language boundary is another layer Native polymorphic model objects do not pass directly into Python. The yaeos_c module stores model objects behind integer handles and offers flat\nprocedures. f2py generates a Python extension around that module; Python\nclasses hold the handle and present a more idiomatic user API. The native ArModel class and the Python ArModel class have related purposes,\nbut they are not the same object in two syntaxes. Shapes, option flags, result\ncontainers, and memory lifetime need explicit adaptation. Read the C-facing layer and Python layer before changing a wrapper signature. Choose the right place for a change Proposed change Likely starting point Check downstream New cubic alpha correlation cubic/alphas/ Constructor/configuration, derivatives, wrappers New mixture combining rule cubic/mixing_rules/ Composition/state derivatives and pure limits Entirely new EOS energy expression Model implementation and derivative adapter Shared property use, volume initialization, exports Incorrect flash material balance Equilibrium residual/phase reconstruction Result conventions and cross-language wrappers Wrong NumPy output shape Python method and flat wrapper Fortran dimensions and memory ordering Missing argument explanation User guide and defining procedure’s FORD comments Generated API and Python documentation A short source-reading exercise Choose a property you already use. Find its public export, defining procedure,\nthe model method it calls, and a regression test. Write down the units and\nshape at each step. Then repeat through Python and mark where its arguments\nare converted. This small exercise gives a more useful mental model than\nreading the repository alphabetically. Continue with Fortran contracts , or use the end-to-end change walkthrough to turn that map into a\ncontribution plan.","tags":"","url":"page/developer/architecture.html"},{"title":"Developer workflow – yaeos","text":"From a checkout to a reviewed change 1. Start from an identifiable revision 2. Understand the build boundaries 3. Install the tools for the layer you need Native Fortran work Python work Documentation work 4. Build the native package first Debugging and performance builds are different checks A smaller test is a faster feedback loop 5. Build the Python package separately 6. Build both documentation systems 7. Diagnose failures in the right layer 8. Prepare the change for review From a checkout to a reviewed change This page explains how to build the project you are editing, rather than\naccidentally testing a different installed version. Read the architecture map first if you are unsure which layer a\nchange belongs to. There are three distinct outcomes to check: the native Fortran package, the\nPython package with its compiled extension, and the generated documentation.\nA successful build of one does not prove the others work. 1. Start from an identifiable revision For a fresh checkout: git clone https://github.com/ipqa-research/yaeos.git cd yaeos\ngit switch -c feature/my-change\ngit status --short For a fork-based contribution, use your fork’s clone URL and add the project\nrepository as an upstream remote. Agree on the target branch before starting\na larger change. The commands above create a new development branch; they do\nnot reset or discard existing work. Before editing an existing checkout, inspect git status and understand its\nmodified and untracked files. A dirty tree can be perfectly ordinary work in\nprogress. Do not clean, overwrite, or revert it merely to make a command run. Record git rev-parse HEAD , compiler and fpm versions when diagnosing a\nnumerical regression. A package version string alone may not identify the\nsource of an uncommitted development checkout. 2. Understand the build boundaries Outcome Entry point What gets produced Native library, apps, examples, and tests Root fpm.toml Compiler-specific files under build/ Python package python/pyproject.toml and python/meson.build A compiled extension and installed Python modules Fortran API and guide pages Root ford.md doc/ford_site/ Python API and tutorials python/docs/source/conf.py A Sphinx HTML tree in your selected output directory Meson invokes fpm install internally to build a position-independent native\nlibrary for the Python extension. That is a separate build from an ordinary\nroot-level fpm build . Do not copy a random .mod file from one compiler or\nprofile into another build: module files are compiler-specific artifacts. Keep generated outputs out of commits. Check .gitignore for the current\nrules rather than assuming every file generated by a new tool is ignored. 3. Install the tools for the layer you need Native Fortran work You need a Fortran compiler, a C compiler for the Tapenade runtime support, fpm , Git for dependencies, and BLAS/LAPACK libraries. The root manifest links lapack and blas . On Debian/Ubuntu a typical system-library setup is: sudo apt install gfortran gcc git libblas-dev liblapack-dev Install fpm using its supported installation instructions, then check: fpm --version\ngfortran --version\ngcc --version The first build needs network access to fetch the Git dependencies declared in fpm.toml . Later builds normally reuse their downloaded copies under build/ .\nChanging a dependency revision, compiler, profile, or compiler flags can\nrequire a different build or a rebuild. Diagnose fetch failures separately\nfrom compilation failures. Python work In addition to the native tools, the Python build uses meson-python , Meson,\nNinja, NumPy/f2py, and a supported Python interpreter. Build isolation installs\nthe requirements declared in python/pyproject.toml but does not install a\nsystem compiler or BLAS/LAPACK for you. Check the actual compiler requirement. The current non-Windows branch of python/meson.build rejects a Fortran compiler version below 14.1 and uses\nGNU-specific flags, including heap trampoline support. This requirement belongs\nto this Python build, not to a generic Fortran language requirement or an\nequivalent minimum enforced by root fpm.toml . The Windows branch has different\nflags and checks; do not infer platform support solely from the absence of\nthat version gate. Documentation work FORD needs a compatible Fortran preprocessor and Graphviz for the configured\ndependency graphs. Sphinx uses python/docs/requirements.txt ; notebook\nconversion also needs Pandoc. Use a virtual environment for documentation\npackages instead of changing unrelated system Python installations. 4. Build the native package first Run from the repository root: fpm build\nfpm run --example basics\nfpm test mixing_base\nfpm test basics is a registered example in fpm.toml . mixing_base is the test\nprogram test/models/residual_helmholtz/cubic/mixing_rules/test_mixing_base.f90 ,\nregistered in fpm.toml by tools/dev/test_to_toml.py . Every native test is\na standalone program like this one, in a test/ subdirectory that mirrors src/ , and the full fpm test runs all of them.\nDiscover your checkout’s targets\nwith fpm test --list and consult fpm test --help for your installed version. Run build commands sequentially when they share the same build directory.\nTwo concurrent fpm processes can overwrite intermediate files or make a\nfailure harder to reproduce. Debugging and performance builds are different checks A debug build is useful for finding invalid array access or uninitialized\nstate; a release build is useful for checking optimized behavior and timing: fpm test --profile debug\nfpm test --profile release For additional compiler diagnostics, select flags supported by that compiler\nand document what you used. For example, a GNU debugging investigation might\nuse --flag \"-g -fcheck=all -fbacktrace\" . Bounds checks can expose existing\ndefects as well as your new change. Do not turn them off merely to make a\nregression disappear. Some existing test files contain long lines; GNU’s -ffree-line-length-none can be useful when reproducing a build with explicit\nflags. Do not hard-code this compiler-specific flag as if it applied to every\ntoolchain. A smaller test is a faster feedback loop Start with the affected target, then run the larger suite before review. When\nchanging a model kernel, test derivatives and a small property call before\nrunning an expensive phase envelope. A failing one-point correction is easier\nto inspect than the last point of a long curve. The testing guide shows how to write a standalone test program\nwith the existing assertion helper and how to choose numerical assertions. 5. Build the Python package separately From the repository root, in a Python environment intended for development: python -m pip install ./python\npython -c \"import yaeos; print(yaeos.__file__, yaeos.__version__)\" The printed path helps catch importing an old wheel or another environment.\nAfter changing compiled Fortran or wrapper signatures, rebuild/reinstall; a\nPython interpreter cannot pick up a newly changed shared library reliably just\nbecause its source file changed. Restart the interpreter after rebuilding. The current Meson build locates the interface source relative to python/ and invokes fpm without an explicit project-directory argument. Its working\ndirectory and fpm ’s upward manifest search therefore matter. Prefer the\nstandard package build layout; do not assume an arbitrary external Meson build\ndirectory will work unchanged. See Python internals for the full pipeline. From the python/ directory, run: python -m pytest tests/ --import-mode = importlib Some Python tests generate Fortran programs under root app/ and invoke fpm to compare the two language paths. They need a working checkout and native\ntools, not just an installed Python wheel. Do not run them concurrently against\nthe same generated filenames. python/tox.ini documents the project’s test,\nstyle, coverage, and documentation environments; tox runs from python/ . Importing the package directly from source can also require installed package\nmetadata: yaeos.__version__ uses importlib.metadata.version(\"yaeos\") . Adding python/ to PYTHONPATH alone is not a substitute for an installation. 6. Build both documentation systems From the repository root, FORD builds the native API and the Markdown guides: ford ford.md Open doc/ford_site/index.html . Review your rendered page, its equations,\ntables, code, images, and links. A successful process exit does not prove\nthat a formula is correct or an example is runnable. From the repository root, an explicit Sphinx output path avoids directory\nambiguity: sphinx-build -b html python/docs/source python/docs/build Use -W for a warning-as-error check when the environment can import the\ndocumented package and all required extensions. Explain any existing warnings\nseparately from warnings introduced by your changes. The Sphinx configuration\ndisables notebook execution, so building the site does not validate notebook\nnumerical outputs. The documentation deployment workflow builds both systems and replaces FORD’s page/python-api/ output with the Sphinx HTML tree. Consequently a local\nFORD-only build shows the Python fallback page, not the complete Python API.\nPreserve that replacement path and test links in the assembled site when\nchanging navigation. Do not use a path to a personal compiler cache or temporary environment in a\npublished example. Describe reproducible package-level commands, and identify\nany environment-specific command used for local debugging as such. 7. Diagnose failures in the right layer Symptom First investigation Dependency clone fails Network, Git URL/revision, and dependency cache BLAS/LAPACK symbols are unresolved Installed libraries, link names, and search paths A native call succeeds but Python fails Rebuilt extension, handle lookup, shapes, and option conversion Python imports the wrong implementation Interpreter/environment and printed yaeos.__file__ Wrapper compiles but values are transposed Array ordering and native shape conventions A regression occurs only in release mode Uninitialized values, aliasing, bounds, and undefined behavior A Sphinx page omits an object Import diagnostics, installed metadata, and extension availability FORD succeeds but a page is missing Front matter, ordered_subpage , filename, and page-relative links If you need a clean build, inspect what is generated and what is user work\nbefore removing anything. fpm clean --help describes the installed version’s\ncleaning options. Do not put a broad deletion command into a debugging recipe\nwithout explaining exactly which artifacts it removes. 8. Prepare the change for review Run git diff --check and inspect the diff yourself. Include implementation,\ntests, and documentation for one coherent change. If a failure predated your\nwork, retain the evidence rather than silently changing an unrelated algorithm. In the review description, include the affected layers, a minimal reproducer,\nthe expected physical/numerical behavior, and the exact checks run. Distinguish\nnative tests, Python tests, and documentation builds. A check that could not\nrun is a limitation to report, not an implied success. Read the actual .github/workflows/CI.yml , .github/workflows/docs.yml , and ci/ci.sh when comparing with CI. Workflow names, exclusions, or a commented\ncommand are not proof of test coverage. For example, current Python wheel\ntesting and the nominal Python-API job do not execute identical commands. Continue with writing tests , the C-facing boundary , or an end-to-end contribution .","tags":"","url":"page/developer/workflow.html"},{"title":"Working in the Fortran core – yaeos","text":"Understand the Fortran contracts before extending them Read the type hierarchy in ordinary Fortran terms The residual Helmholtz interface Extensive energy is a requirement Optional means every supported combination must work The excess-Gibbs interface Choose a derivative implementation strategy Volume initialization is a second contract Constructor, visibility, and publication Write documentation that explains the contract A review checklist for a core change Understand the Fortran contracts before extending them The most important part of adding a model is not choosing a filename. It is\nsatisfying the contract that shared property routines and solvers already\nexpect. A function that returns a plausible pressure at one state can still\nhave incorrect composition derivatives, an incompatible volume initializer,\nor an energy normalized on the wrong amount basis. Use this page alongside the repository map . For complete\nmodel implementations, continue to the analytical and automatic-differentiation tutorials. Read the type hierarchy in ordinary Fortran terms A derived type groups data and procedures. A type can extend a parent and\ninherit its data and methods. A deferred binding specifies a procedure\ninterface but leaves its implementation to a concrete extension. A variable\ndeclared with class(ArModel) can refer to different concrete EOS types. ! Fragment inside a program that imports these names from yaeos. class ( ArModel ), allocatable :: model model = PengRobinson76 ( tc , pc , omega ) call model % pressure ( n , V , T , P ) The assignment constructs a concrete model. The pressure routine then calls\nthe residual_helmholtz binding associated with that concrete type. You do not\nneed a select type branch for every EOS in a generic property calculation.\nAdd one only when the operation genuinely requires model-specific information. BaseModel contains components , a Substances value whose arrays hold\ncritical properties and other metadata in component order. A constructor must\ninitialize the fields its algorithms actually use; leaving arrays unallocated\nand hoping every inherited method tolerates that is not a valid contract. The residual Helmholtz interface The defining interface is abs_residual_helmholtz in src/models/residual_helmholtz/ar_models.f90 . Its inputs are component mole\namounts n(:) , total volume V , and temperature T . Outputs are optional: Output Meaning Shape Ar Residual Helmholtz energy Scalar ArV , ArT First volume and temperature derivatives Scalar ArV2 , ArT2 , ArTV Second state derivatives Scalar Arn Mole-number gradient (nc) ArVn , ArTn State/mole-number mixed derivatives (nc) Arn2 Mole-number Hessian (nc,nc) Here nc = size(n) . The derivatives are with respect to amounts , not\nindependently varied mole fractions. Holding total volume fixed while varying\none amount changes both density and composition. Normalizing n before\ndifferentiating can silently implement a different derivative. Extensive energy is a requirement The energy is in bar·L, not automatically J/mol or the reduced molar quantity . If a published correlation supplies a\ndimensionless molar energy , convert it consistently: Differentiate the full extensive expression, including its amount and\ntemperature factors. Adding those factors only to the final energy output\ndoes not fix derivatives that were taken on a different function. For fixed temperature, the extensive scaling identity is It implies, for example, . These identities make useful tests\nalongside independent derivative checks. Optional means every supported combination must work Guard assignment to an optional output with present . Do not reference an\nabsent output as a temporary. This is a schematic implementation fragment ,\nnot a complete energy kernel: ! Compute local values independently of whether corresponding outputs exist. if ( present ( ArV )) ArV = local_ArV if ( present ( Arn )) Arn = local_Arn Also consider what a caller requests internally. The current shared pressure method requests several state derivatives even when the user asks\nonly for pressure. Conversely, direct calls can request just a Hessian or a\nmixed derivative without requesting Ar . A model must not work only for the\ncombination used in its first example. Tests should include energy only, individual derivatives, mixed combinations,\nand all outputs together. Verify that repeated calls do not depend on\nuninitialized work arrays or results left by a previous call. The excess-Gibbs interface GeModel in src/models/excess_gibbs/ge_models.f90 requires excess_gibbs(self, n, T, Ge, GeT, GeT2, Gen, GeTn, Gen2) . The pattern is\nsimilar, but there is no volume input and no EOS volume root in this contract. Gen and GeTn are vectors; Gen2 is a matrix. The shared activity-coefficient method uses As with Helmholtz energy, an excess-Gibbs implementation must return the\nextensive energy on the library’s unit basis. A molar correlation needs its\ntotal-amount factor. Do not route a liquid activity model through an EOS\nproperty calculation merely because both types inherit BaseModel . Choose a derivative implementation strategy Route Implement Good reason to choose it Main risk to test Analytical The real-valued energy and derivative interface Existing reliable formulas or optimized kernel Algebra, index orientation, optional branches Hyperdual The adapter’s hyperdual energy function and required model support A clear primal expression with overloaded operations Unsupported operations, dropped derivative lanes, nonsmooth branches Tapenade Primal routines plus generated derivative bindings Source-to-source differentiation of a real-valued kernel Seed/adjoint contracts, generated code integration and reproducibility Automatic differentiation applies chain rules to the program you wrote. It\ndoes not know whether you implemented the intended thermodynamic equation or\nused the right units. Keep analytical identities and independent numerical\nchecks in all three routes. For an existing cubic model, first ask whether a new AlphaFunction or CubicMixRule is enough. Replacing the entire CubicEoS energy implementation\nto change one temperature correlation duplicates more logic than necessary. Volume initialization is a second contract An ArModel also implements get_v0(self, n, P, T) . The default volume solver\nuses it to initialize the dense branch. The specialized Michelsen volume\nalgorithm uses a covolume or lower-volume scale in a reduced-coordinate solve.\nThese roles are not identical. Choose an initializer compatible with the actual volume method your model\nuses. An ideal-gas volume returned for every branch is not automatically an\nadequate liquid initializer. If a model overrides volume , check that the\noverride has the inherited signature and consistent root/failure behavior. Read src/models/solvers/volume.f90 and a comparable model before reusing that\nsolver. Test pressure recovered at the solved volume, extensive volume\nscaling, and both requested root branches at appropriate states. Constructor, visibility, and publication A new model module normally has three parts: a type that stores its parameters,\na constructor that initializes them, and the bound procedures. Keep parameter\nvalidation near construction where possible. Document dimensions, units,\ninteraction matrix orientation, and whether inputs are copied. To publish a model, trace the relevant imports through src/models/models.f90 and src/yaeos.f90 . To publish an equilibrium routine,\nalso check src/equilibria/equilibria.f90 . Use narrow imports inside\nimplementation modules to reduce unnecessary dependencies and name conflicts. Adding a use line in the facade is not sufficient for Python support. The\nflat wrapper, constructor handle management, Python class, and package exports\nare separate changes. Follow the end-to-end checklist . Write documentation that explains the contract Use one variable declaration per line so each argument or work value can have\nan unambiguous description: real ( pr ), intent ( in ) :: T !! Temperature [K]. real ( pr ), intent ( out ) :: P !! Pressure [bar]. For modules, types, and procedures, write a title and short summary, then # Description , # Examples , and # References when references are needed.\nExplain why the calculation exists and what a caller must guarantee, not just\nthe English expansion of its name. Mark schematic fragments as such; avoid\nexamples with undeclared variables presented as complete programs. Source comments are part of the implementation’s interface. If a change alters\nunits, failure behavior, shape, default settings, or derivative conventions,\nupdate the comments, user guide, and relevant wrapper documentation together. A review checklist for a core change Before review, be able to answer: Which binding or routine changed, and what callers depend on its signature? Does it preserve the amount basis, units, and derivative shapes? Are optional outputs and invalid inputs handled deliberately? Is the volume initializer compatible with the selected volume method? Are mathematical identities, individual derivatives, and regression cases tested? Are public exports and downstream interfaces updated if needed? Continue with numerical algorithms if you are changing a\nsolver, or testing to turn these contracts into regression\nchecks.","tags":"","url":"page/developer/fortran.html"},{"title":"Working on numerical algorithms – yaeos","text":"From thermodynamic equations to a reliable solver 1. Write the problem down before editing the loop Log coordinates change the Jacobian 2. Know which numerical implementation you are calling 3. Verify the Jacobian independently 4. Understand continuation as a sequence of local solves 5. Separate three meanings of “good result” 6. Build a minimal failure case 7. Review numerical changes as behavioral changes From thermodynamic equations to a reliable solver A phase calculation combines several independently fallible pieces: a physical\nmodel, a choice of unknowns, residual equations, derivatives, a nonlinear\nalgorithm, and result interpretation. Treat these as separate layers when\ndebugging. Otherwise, a unit error can look like a convergence problem, and a\nsmall residual can hide convergence to the wrong branch. This page is a source-reading and modification guide. The user equilibrium pages explain which\ncalculation to choose; the testing guide explains how to verify\nan implementation. 1. Write the problem down before editing the loop For the routine you are changing, identify: Fixed inputs: feed, pressure, temperature, or other constraints. Unknown vector: exactly which physical quantity each entry represents. Transformations: for example log pressure instead of pressure, or a\n  phase-composition ratio instead of a mole fraction. Residuals: one equation per unknown, including normalization and\n  continuation constraints. Acceptance rules: residual tolerance, valid domain, iteration limit,\n  branch tests, and stability checks. Returned state: which arrays and flags are meaningful on failure. Search for both the routine that packs X and the routine that unpacks it.\nIndex positions are part of the internal contract. PT, PX, TX, three-phase,\nand general multiphase equations do not all use the same layout. Log coordinates change the Jacobian Suppose . A residual derivative with respect to the packed unknown\nis not simply its pressure derivative: Log coordinates keep pressure positive, but they do not by themselves make the\niteration well scaled or prevent overflow. A finite-difference check must\nperturb u , not physical pressure, if the Jacobian is defined with respect to\nthe packed vector. 2. Know which numerical implementation you are calling Several solver families coexist. Similar routine names do not imply identical\ninterfaces or guarantees. Source Role to inspect src/math/newton.f90 Configurable Newton solver with diagnostics and safeguarding src/math/nonlineal.f90 Another multivariate Newton implementation and homotopy helpers src/math/math.f90 Scalar Newton, numerical helpers, and MINPACK adapters src/math/linalg.f90 Dense linear-system and eigenproblem wrappers src/math/continuation.f90 Predictor/corrector driver with callback interfaces src/models/solvers/saturation_point.f90 Saturation equations and local point correctors src/equilibria/ Problem-specific loops and phase logic When reading an unqualified call newton(...) , follow its use association\nto the actual module. Before replacing a solver, compare callback signatures,\noutput status conventions, scaling, maximum-iteration behavior, and which\nquantities the caller assumes were filled on return. Do not infer that a low-level linear solve propagates all LAPACK errors simply\nbecause it calls LAPACK. Check whether the wrapper exposes or handles the\nstatus. If you need stronger error reporting, plan that as an explicit\ninterface change with tests rather than quietly changing a caller’s assumed\nsemantics. 3. Verify the Jacobian independently For , Newton’s method solves , where . A useful\ncentral-difference check of column is Use a relative and absolute scale for so a zero coordinate still gets\na nonzero perturbation. Try more than one step size: an extremely small step\ncan amplify roundoff, while a large step measures curvature rather than the\nlocal derivative. Avoid crossing invalid domains or switching volume branches\nwhen comparing derivatives intended for one smooth branch. If the Jacobian is wrong, inspect unpacking, chain-rule factors, component\nordering, and phase conventions before changing damping. In particular, the\nuser convention may be represented internally as an incipient\nphase divided by the feed phase; dew calculations can therefore use inverted\nratios. Check the actual reconstruction equations. 4. Understand continuation as a sequence of local solves A phase boundary is a curve, not a single root. A continuation method fixes\none specification to select a point, corrects the equilibrium equations,\nthen predicts a nearby point using a tangent. For equations , The generic driver in src/math/continuation.f90 exposes callbacks for\nresiduals/derivatives, optional specification updates, postprocessing, point\ncorrection, and stopping. Read the call site to see which callbacks a given\nenvelope actually supplies. The current generic driver’s default specification update selects the largest\nabsolute tangent component and rescales the tangent and step. It does not apply the 0.05 step cap shown in an example comment. If a branch needs a\nphysical step limit, identify the active callback that supplies it or add a\ntested policy deliberately. The generic result has a fixed row count, with unfilled rows initialized to\nzero. That is a storage convention, not proof that every requested point was\naccepted. Specialized envelope routines can use other storage conventions.\nDo not interpret padding as a zero-temperature equilibrium point. 5. Separate three meanings of “good result” Numerical convergence: the algorithm met its stated termination test. Physical admissibility: compositions, phase fractions, volumes, and\n   temperatures lie in the intended domain and balances close. Physical relevance: the branch is stable or otherwise appropriate for\n   the task, and the model/parameters are valid for the system. A converged negative phase fraction can be useful in a mathematical\ncontinuation formulation but is not an ordinary physical phase split.\nLikewise, forcing a liquid root may intentionally follow a metastable branch.\nPreserve the distinction in names, documentation, tests, and wrappers. Near a critical point, phases become indistinguishable and the equations may\nbe nearly singular. Tolerances and step control matter, but singularity can\nalso reflect the formulation itself. A full mixture criticality condition\nincludes the ideal contribution to the stability matrix; a residual-energy\nHessian alone is not the general mixture condition. 6. Build a minimal failure case When an envelope fails at point 137, save the last accepted state, the\npredictor, selected specification, residual norms, and relevant parameters.\nTry to reproduce the single point correction before rerunning a long\ncurve. This makes it easier to tell whether the problem began with a bad\npredictor, an invalid phase reconstruction, or a broken residual/Jacobian. For a flash regression, check the feed balance in addition to a solver flag: For multiple phases, use the representation and reference-phase convention of\nthat routine rather than extrapolating the two-phase array layout. Never\ncompare two implementations’ raw packed vectors until you have checked that\nthey pack the same variables in the same order. 7. Review numerical changes as behavioral changes Changing a tolerance, line search, initial guess, or root choice can change\nwhich solution is found. Record why the change is needed and test more than\nthe failing example: one ordinary case, the regression, a relevant limiting\ncase, and a failure path. For performance changes, compare both outputs and\ntimings under the same compiler/profile. Avoid broadly increasing iteration limits to hide unexplained regressions.\nPreserve a minimal test of the failure and document any remaining limitation.\nThe end-to-end walkthrough shows how to carry that evidence\nthrough a contribution.","tags":"","url":"page/developer/numerics.html"},{"title":"C interface layer – yaeos","text":"The yaeos_c interface module What the layer contains ISO_C_BINDING kinds are not a C ABI Handles: the model registry Lifetime and release Argument conventions a caller must respect Column-major layout Optional outputs and NaN sentinels Trace of one call: pressure Adding a routine to this layer Debugging boundaries Known sharp edges Related pages The yaeos_c interface module c_interface/yaeos_c.f90 is the flat, array-based façade over the\npolymorphic Fortran core. It exists so that a caller which cannot speak\nFortran — today that is f2py , used by the Python package — can build a\nthermodynamic model and evaluate properties by passing plain numbers and\nflat arrays. This page is for developers changing that layer. If you are calling yaeos from Python, read the Python interface page .\nIf you are adding a model, read adding your own models first. A model used only\nby native Fortran callers need not change this layer, but exposing a new model\nto Python normally requires constructor/registration support here. Read the file itself before editing\nit — the file is the contract, this page only describes it. What the layer contains One module, yaeos_c , with three groups of routines: Instantiation — pr76 , pr78 , srk , rkpr , psrk , pcsaft , multifluid_gerg2008 , nrtl , uniquac , unifac_vle , unifac_psrk , unifac_dortmund . Builds the model, stores it in the registry, returns an\n  integer handle. Configuration — set_qmr , set_qmrtd , set_mhv , set_hv , set_hvnrtl , set_sddlc , set_cmr , set_cmrtd , set_alpha_* , pcsaft_set_kij . Attaches a mixing rule, an alpha function, or interaction\n  parameters to an existing handle. Evaluation — pressure , volume , lnphi_vt , helmholtz_residual_pt , flash , saturation_pressure , pt_mp_phase_envelope . Computes one property or one equilibrium for a\n  given handle. The module is private by default and re-exports its surface through an\nexplicit public :: list near the top of the file. That list is not\ndecoration: f2py only generates wrappers for names that are public, so a\nnew routine that is not listed is unreachable from Python. Units are the\nlibrary defaults, stated in the module header: pressure in bar, volume in\nlitre, temperature in kelvin, amount in mole. ISO_C_BINDING kinds are not a C ABI This is the most important thing to understand before editing this file. The module header contains one import: use iso_c_binding , only : c_double , c_int , c_int64_t These kinds correspond to the associated C types: real(c_double) for\ndouble-precision reals, integer(c_int) for handles and\ncounts. It does not export a symbol name, does not define a C\ncalling convention, and does not make scalar arguments pass by value. bind(c) is absent from this interface module. The hyperdual type in src/adiff/hyperdual.f90 does use bind(c) , but a type declaration does not\ncreate C-callable wrapper procedures. No procedure in yaeos_c carries bind(c) . The\nconsequences are concrete: compilers mangle Fortran names, so\nthe module procedure pr76 is not thereby a C symbol named pr76 . Ordinary\nFortran assumed-shape arrays can require compiler-specific descriptors, and\ncharacter arguments can involve hidden length arguments. C-compatible kinds\ndo not specify these calling conventions. The current repository does not\nprovide a portable public C header for these procedures. Do not assume that\ndeclaring matching-looking C prototypes makes direct calls portable. Some background material about this project describes the layer as a BIND(C) interface. Treat that text as design intent; the shipped file is\nthe contract, and it is an f2py -oriented façade with C-compatible kinds ,\nnot a stable C ABI. If you genuinely need a C entry point, do not retrofit bind(c) onto yaeos_c.f90 without designing and testing the ABI. Decide how\narrays, strings, optional values, handles, and errors cross the boundary.\nFor a simple explicit-shape ABI, pass extents explicitly and use interoperable\ncharacter buffers or lengths. Scalars may be passed by reference, or use value to match a by-value C prototype. Modern Fortran also supports additional\ninteroperability mechanisms, including C descriptors and some optional\narguments; these require matching headers and compiler support, not guesses.\nA small separate adapter with documented prototypes and C-side tests can\npreserve the existing f2py layer while establishing a deliberate C API. Handles: the model registry The core models are polymorphic objects ( class(ArModel) , class(GeModel) ), and Fortran cannot expose polymorphic objects through a\nflat interface. The layer therefore keeps a registry and hands out integer\nhandles. type :: ArModelContainer class ( ArModel ), allocatable :: model end type ArModelContainer type :: GeModelContainer class ( GeModel ), allocatable :: model end type GeModelContainer integer , parameter :: max_models = 1000000 !! Maximum number of models logical :: free_ar_model ( max_models ) = . true . logical :: free_ge_model ( max_models ) = . true . class ( ArModelContainer ), allocatable :: ar_models (:) class ( GeModelContainer ), allocatable :: ge_models (:) ar_models and ge_models are allocated lazily, on the first model\ncreation, with max_models entries each. The handle is a one-based index\ninto that table: id = 1 is the first slot. Instantiation follows a fixed recipe — build into a module-level singleton,\nthen move the singleton into the first free slot: subroutine pr76 ( tc , pc , w , id ) use yaeos , only : PengRobinson76 real ( c_double ), intent ( in ) :: tc (:) real ( c_double ), intent ( in ) :: pc (:) real ( c_double ), intent ( in ) :: w (:) integer ( c_int ), intent ( out ) :: id ar_model = PengRobinson76 ( tc , pc , w ) call extend_ar_models_list ( id ) end subroutine pr76 extend_ar_models_list scans free_ar_model from index 1, marks the first\nfree slot as taken, and move_alloc s the singleton into it. The GeModel path is identical through extend_ge_models_list . Lifetime and release make_available_ar_models_list(id) sets free_ar_model(id) = .true. and\nnothing else. It does not deallocate the model; the object stays in the\ncontainer until the slot is reused by a later move_alloc . Consequently,\nreleasing a handle returns the slot , not the memory, and a released\nhandle is not invalidated — using it afterwards is undefined behaviour,\nbecause the slot may already hold a different model. Treat handles as\nopaque and never cache them beyond the object that owns them. Because the registry and the singleton buffers are module state, the module\nis not re-entrant. Do not create models concurrently from several threads.\nDo not infer that evaluation is thread-safe either: inspect the called model,\nsolver, AD runtime, and any shared flags or caches. Read-only handle lookup\nalone is insufficient to establish reentrancy of the entire calculation. The exhaustion guard deserves a note: if ( id == max_models ) error stop 1 id is intent(out) and is only assigned inside the scan loop, so when\nthe table is full this comparison inspects a value the procedure never set.\nDo not rely on this branch to produce a clean error. The check also rejects\nthe final slot after allocation. Avoid registry exhaustion and test any future\ncapacity/error-handling change explicitly. Argument conventions a caller must respect Order, names and returns. Dummy arguments are positional. f2py also\nexposes them as keyword arguments, lowercased ( --lower is passed to f2py in the Meson build), so the Fortran name dPdn becomes the Python\nkeyword dpdn . An intent(out) scalar becomes the value of the call: in pressure the pressure P is intent(out) , so f2py returns it and the\nremaining optional outputs become keyword arguments. optional, intent(inout) arguments are optional keywords. Omit the\nkeyword and the generated binding can represent its absence; wrapper code\nuses presence checks to decide which optional outputs to pass onward. Verify\nthis behavior in the generated binding when changing an argument. Shared\nproperty kernels may still compute derivative intermediates even when no\nderivative is returned to the user. Shapes. Several dummy arguments take their extent from another\nargument: real ( c_double ), optional , intent ( in out ) :: dPdV real ( c_double ), optional , intent ( in out ) :: dPdT real ( c_double ), optional , intent ( in out ) :: dPdn ( size ( n )) real ( c_double ), intent ( out ) :: x ( size ( z0 )) real ( c_double ), intent ( out ) :: x_ls ( max_points , np , size ( z )) A direct caller must supply matching extents. f2py emits parser\ndiagnostics for some dependent dimensions; those diagnostics alone do not\nestablish what shape a generated wrapper accepts or returns. Check the\ngenerated signature and exercise the relevant call when changing a shape. Some outputs have a fixed size chosen by this file rather than by the\ncaller: pure_saturation_line fills arrays of 10000 elements, and find_self_intersections fills arrays of 10. Character arguments are fixed-width and padded. Several root_type and kind arguments use character(len=15) . Root selectors use \"stable\" , \"liquid\" , and \"vapor\" ; saturation-point kinds instead use values such as \"bubble\" , \"dew\" , and \"liquid-liquid\" . Follow the specific procedure’s\ncontract. convert_kind maps the numeric root selectors used by multiphase\nenvelopes back to root strings. Integer kinds are mixed. Most arguments use integer(c_int) , but\nseveral use the plain default integer : nc , ngs , g_ids and g_v in psrk , ids in multifluid_gerg2008 , and spec , max_iters and max_points in the critical-point routines. f2py maps both to a C int in the generated configurations used by common builds, but do not assume\ndefault integer always matches c_int . Check the generated type mapping when\nchanging compiler kinds or portability settings; normalizing kinds is a\nseparate compatibility change that needs tests. Column-major layout Fortran arrays are column-major. For a two-dimensional array the element\nat Fortran position (i, j) sits at zero-based C offset (i - 1) + (j - 1) * nrows . This matters whenever a matrix crosses the boundary — the NRTL aij , bij , cij matrices, the mixing-rule kij / lij matrices, the group\nmatrices of the UNIFAC constructors, or a Hessian of mole derivatives. A\nfuture explicit-buffer C adapter would need to document this layout. f2py is more\nforgiving, but only partly, and the difference decides whether a wrong\nanswer is loud or silent: An intent(in) two-dimensional argument is converted to Fortran order\n  by f2py when the caller’s array is not already Fortran-contiguous.\n  The logical values are preserved; only a copy is added. An intent(inout) two-dimensional argument is not converted. The\n  caller must supply a Fortran-contiguous array, and f2py raises ValueError: failed to initialize intent(inout) array -- input not\n  fortran contiguous otherwise. This is why every Hessian and\n  derivative matrix in python/yaeos/core.py is allocated with order=\"F\" . A dummy argument without an intent attribute is not equivalent to\n  explicitly declaring intent(inout) . Fortran imposes no intent restriction,\n  and f2py may infer or default to its own treatment. set_qmr ( kij , lij )\n  and set_cmr ( kijk , lijk ) include such arguments. Check the generated\n  binding rather than assuming that writes propagate back to the caller;\n  a temporary input copy need not provide write-back behavior. The Python layer avoids the last case by always passing an np.array(matrix, order=\"F\") copy, as in python/yaeos/models/excess_gibbs/nrtl.py and in python/yaeos/models/residual_helmholtz/cubic_eos/mixing_rules.py . Optional outputs and NaN sentinels Two different conventions express “no value”, and they are not\ninterchangeable. NaN marks missing, invalid, or unfilled output, depending on the routine. A helper function computes 0.0 / 0.0 and is used to pre-fill output arrays before the points that\nwere actually computed are written in. A reader must therefore filter on\nNaN rather than assume that a returned array is dense. critical_line fills as , Ts , Ps and Vs with NaN and sets every CEP output to NaN\nwhen the critical line has no critical end point; binary_llv_from_cep fills its eight output arrays with NaN; the three multi-phase envelope\nroutines pre-fill x_ls , ws , betas , Ps , Ts , alphas , Pcs and Tcs , so trailing points of a short envelope read as NaN rather than as\nzeros; pure_saturation_line pre-fills its four 10000-element arrays and\nwrites only the computed prefix; find_self_intersections pre-fills its\noutputs. Failure sentinels are routine-specific. When a saturation\ncalculation returns a state whose kind is \"failed\" , the wrapper sets\nthe pressure or the temperature to NaN instead of returning a number. The\nflash wrappers use a different signal: beta = -1 , with x and y set\nto the feed composition and Vx and Vy set to 1 . flash_second_order additionally checks trim(result%kind) == \"failed\" , which plain flash does not. Check for failure before using a result: a NaN propagates\nthrough NumPy arithmetic without raising, and beta == -1 is easy to miss\nin a dictionary. Trace of one call: pressure Step 1 — the flat wrapper in c_interface/yaeos_c.f90 takes the\nhandle, the amounts, the volume and the temperature, returns the pressure\nthrough an intent(out) argument, and offers the three derivatives as optional, intent(inout) arguments: subroutine pressure ( id , n , V , T , P , dPdV , dPdT , dPdn ) integer ( c_int ), intent ( in ) :: id real ( c_double ), intent ( in ) :: n (:) real ( c_double ), intent ( in ) :: V real ( c_double ), intent ( in ) :: T real ( c_double ), intent ( out ) :: P real ( c_double ), optional , intent ( in out ) :: dPdV real ( c_double ), optional , intent ( in out ) :: dPdT real ( c_double ), optional , intent ( in out ) :: dPdn ( size ( n )) call ar_models ( id )% model % pressure ( n , V , T , P , dPdV , dPdT , dPdn ) end subroutine pressure Step 2 — dynamic dispatch. ar_models(id)%model is the polymorphic\nobject stored in the registry slot. The select type blocks elsewhere in\nthe file, for instance in the mixing-rule setters, show the same pattern:\nthe concrete type is resolved at run time. Step 3 — the model method in src/models/residual_helmholtz/ar_models.f90 calls residual_helmholtz for the energy and its derivatives and then adds the ideal-gas term\nitself: P = totn * R * T/V - ArV , with dPdV = -ArV2 - R*T*totn/V**2 , dPdT = -ArTV + totn*R/V and dPdn(:) = R*T/V - ArVn(:) . The ideal-gas\nterm is added by the inherited pressure method, not by the residual\nHelmholtz energy; do not add it again in a wrapper. Note also the kind\nchange: the model works in real(pr) , and pr is real64 ( src/constants.f90 ). Verify that the chosen compiler’s pr and c_double are compatible when porting the build; do not rely only on similar kind names. Adding a routine to this layer Write the subroutine in c_interface/yaeos_c.f90 , inside the module,\n   with iso_c_binding kinds on every dummy argument. Add its name to the public :: list. Without this, f2py will not\n   generate a wrapper and Python will not see it. Use intent(in) for inputs and intent(out) for results. Mark\n   derivative outputs optional, intent(inout) so callers can request\n   only what they need. Take the model from the registry: ar_models(id)%model or ge_models(id)%model . Never cache a model in a local variable across\n   calls. Decide the failure convention explicitly: return NaN for the property\n   or the documented sentinel, and pre-fill array outputs with NaN when\n   the number of meaningful entries can be smaller than the array extent.\n   Say both in a comment. Add the Python wrapper that allocates the derivative buffers and shapes\n   the result. See the Python interface page . Add a test. The Python-side test suite lives in python/tests/ , and python/tests/models/residual_helmholtz/test_ar_same_as_fortran.py compares the Python result against a Fortran program it generates and\n   runs. This checks the interface against the native implementation; add\n   independent reference or mathematical checks for the property itself. Keep the doc comment style of the file. The !! comments follow the\n   FORD convention, but ford.md sets src_dir: src , so this directory is not part of the generated API documentation. The comments are still\n   worth writing for IDE users and maintainers of these routines. Debugging boundaries Symptom Look first at Then at AttributeError on yaeos_c The public :: list in yaeos_c.f90 Whether the extension was rebuilt Wrong numbers for a matrix argument The intent of the dummy argument Whether the routine writes into the matrix Wrong numbers for a vector argument Component ordering against the model parameters Units: bar, litre, kelvin, mole A derivative is garbage but the value is fine The optional buffer was not allocated F-ordered Whether the model supports that derivative NaN where a number was expected The failure convention of that routine The underlying equilibrium solver beta == -1 from a flash The feed composition and the specification Whether an initial guess was supplied A handle refers to another model A handle was released and reused The lifetime rules above The process aborts during model creation The registry exhaustion guard The number of live models Known sharp edges No bind(c) , so this is not a C ABI and there is no header. make_available_*_models_list releases the slot, not the memory. The registry exhaustion guard reads an unassigned intent(out) argument. c_int64_t is imported and never used, and integer kinds are mixed\n  between integer(c_int) and the default integer . character(len=15) is padded and must be trimmed by the caller. flash_grid prints progress to standard output when its parallel argument is false. The registry tables hold a million entries and are allocated on first use. The !! doc comments here are not rendered by FORD. Related pages Developer handbook — the rest of the developer guide. Architecture — how the Fortran layers fit together. Fortran internals — the core library this layer wraps. Numerics — the solvers behind the equilibrium routines. Development workflow — how to build and test a change. Testing — the test suites and how to extend them. Python interface layer — the wrapper above this file. Adding your own models — the user-facing guide for extending the library.","tags":"","url":"page/developer/c_interface.html"},{"title":"Python interface layer – yaeos","text":"The yaeos Python package Package map The build pipeline: fpm, then f2py, then Meson Compiler requirements What needs a rebuild The yaeos.lib boundary Trace: PengRobinson76 to a pressure Shapes, memory order and dtypes Return conventions The package mirrors the Fortran API Adding a feature: wrapper, Python, tests Testing Debugging boundaries Known sharp edges Related pages The yaeos Python package The Python package is a thin, typed layer over the Fortran library. It\ngives a Python user an object-oriented API — models with methods, results\nas dictionaries, derivatives as named entries — instead of a flat list of\nfunctions that take and return bare arrays. This page is for developers changing that layer. For using the package,\nread the Python API guide . For the layer that\nsits underneath, read the C interface page . For\nadding a thermodynamic model, read adding your own models first. Native Fortran\nextension does not automatically publish a Python class: a new model exposed\nto Python normally needs wrapper construction, a handle-owning class, and\npackage/build exports, even though shared property methods can be inherited. Package map Everything lives under python/ . python/pyproject.toml — package metadata and the meson-python backend. python/meson.build — the real build: fpm, f2py, and the extension module. python/yaeos/__init__.py — public exports; __version__ from the metadata. python/yaeos/core.py — the ArModel and GeModel abstract classes and\n  every property method. python/yaeos/constants.py — R and the root_kinds mapping. python/yaeos/envelopes.py — PTEnvelope , PXEnvelope , TXEnvelope . python/yaeos/gpec.py — the generalised phase-envelope calculator. python/yaeos/models/ — concrete models: excess_gibbs/ , residual_helmholtz/ , groups.py . python/yaeos/fitting/ — parameter-fitting helpers and their solvers. python/yaeos/tools/writers.py — writers that render model parameters as\n  Fortran source. python/yaeos/lib/__init__.py — the boundary to the compiled extension. python/tests/ — the Python test suite; python/docs/source/ — Sphinx. python/yaeos/core.py is large because it holds every thermodynamic\nproperty method for both abstract classes; the concrete model classes\nmostly hold parameters and call yaeos_c . The build pipeline: fpm, then f2py, then Meson The full build instructions, the toolchain list and the working-directory\npitfalls are in the developer workflow . What matters for\nthe Python layer is the order of the three stages, because it decides what\nhas to be rebuilt after a change. Stage 1 — the Fortran core. python/meson.build shells out to fpm install --profile release --prefix <build>/fpm_build , which produces\nthe static library libyaeos.a and the module files. The include subdirectory matters: Meson adds fpm_build/include to the include path\nso that use yaeos resolves when the extension is compiled. Stage 2 — the f2py wrapper. A custom_target runs f2py over the flat\ninterface module: python - m numpy . f2py @INPUT @ - m yaeos_python --lower @INPUT@ is c_interface/yaeos_c.f90 , resolved as meson.global_source_root() / '..' / 'c_interface' / 'yaeos_c.f90' . Two\nfiles come out: yaeos_pythonmodule.c and yaeos_python-f2pywrappers2.f90 . The --lower flag lowercases the\nFortran names, which is why the Python side calls yaeos_c.cv_residual_vt and yaeos_c.set_alpha_rkpr for Fortran routines named Cv_residual_vt and set_alpha_RKPR . f2py prints a long list of messages like analyzevars : could not parse dimension for variable 'size(n)' These messages indicate that f2py could not determine the dimension while\nparsing the source. They do not, by themselves, establish whether the\ngenerated wrapper handles that argument correctly. Inspect the generated\nsignature and test the actual accepted and returned shapes when changing\ndependent-dimension arguments. Stage 3 — the extension module. Meson compiles yaeos_c.f90 together with the generated files into a single extension module named yaeos_python and installs it under the yaeos/lib subdirectory of the\npackage. The pure-Python modules are installed separately with py.install_sources . Compiler requirements python/meson.build enforces them, and the gate is worth knowing before\nyou install the package: Meson >= 0.64.0 , and a Fortran compiler added with add_languages('fortran', native: false) so that a cross build is\n  possible. On every platform except Windows, the Fortran compiler must be version\n  14.1 or newer ; otherwise the build stops with Fortran compiler version 14.1 or newer is required! . The comparison is >=14.1 , so a GCC 14.0 toolchain fails. The flags added for that path\n  — -ftrampoline-impl=heap , -ffree-line-length-none , -fno-strict-aliasing , -fno-common , -fno-inline-small-functions — are GNU-style, not portable. Windows skips the version check and links statically instead. quadmath is required everywhere except Windows, where it is linked\n  explicitly, and OpenMP is linked with -lgomp . The build requires fpm and ninja on non-Windows platforms, declared\n  as build requirements in python/pyproject.toml . The Fortran core has no such requirement: fpm builds the library and its\ntests with whatever compiler you give it. The 14.1 gate lives only in the\nPython package build. One inconsistency to know about: python/meson.build declares version : '3.1.0' , while python/pyproject.toml and fpm.toml both\nsay 4.5.4 . The version the user sees comes from the installed\ndistribution metadata, so the Meson project version is stale. What needs a rebuild Change Rebuild needed? A pure-Python file under python/yaeos/ No c_interface/yaeos_c.f90 Yes — the extension is compiled from it A file under src/ Yes — fpm install re-runs python/meson.build or python/pyproject.toml Yes — Meson reconfigures The yaeos.lib boundary python/yaeos/lib/__init__.py is three lines long and defines the whole\nboundary: from yaeos.lib.yaeos_python import yaeos_c __all__ = [ \"yaeos_c\" ] The compiled extension is yaeos_python ; the Fortran module yaeos_c inside it appears as the submodule yaeos_c . Every routine of the flat\ninterface is therefore reachable as yaeos_c.<fortran_name_lower_cased> . Application code should use the public classes exported by python/yaeos/__init__.py — PengRobinson76 , NRTL , QMR and so on.\nThe yaeos_c namespace is an implementation detail with no stability\nguarantee. And nothing above python/yaeos/lib/ may hand NumPy arrays to yaeos_c with the wrong memory order or the wrong shape: that is where a\nsilent wrong answer enters the system. Trace: PengRobinson76 to a pressure The whole path of one number, from a Python constructor to a Fortran\nresidual Helmholtz evaluation and back. Step 1 — the constructor in python/yaeos/models/residual_helmholtz/cubic_eos/cubic_eos.py stores the\nparameters and asks the flat layer for a handle: class PengRobinson76 ( CubicEoS ): def __init__ ( self , critical_temperatures , critical_pressures , acentric_factors , mixrule : CubicMixRule = None ) -> None : super ( PengRobinson76 , self ) . __init__ ( critical_temperatures , critical_pressures , acentric_factors ) self . id = yaeos_c . pr76 ( self . tc , self . pc , self . w ) self . mixrule = mixrule if mixrule : mixrule . set_mixrule ( self . id ) self.id is the handle used by this Python object’s calls into the Fortran\nregistry. The model itself remains in module-level Fortran registry state. Step 2 — the handle registry. yaeos_c.pr76 calls the Fortran pr76 , which builds the concrete model, stores it in the first free slot\nof the ArModel registry, and returns the one-based index of that slot.\nThe registry is described in the C interface page ; the\npoint to remember here is that the handle is an opaque integer owned by\nthe Fortran module. Step 3 — the property wrapper in python/yaeos/core.py allocates the\noutput buffers and calls the flat layer: def pressure ( self , moles , volume , temperature , dv = False , dt = False , dn = False ): nc = len ( moles ) dv = np . array ( 0 , dtype = np . float64 ) if dv else None dt = np . array ( 0 , dtype = np . float64 ) if dt else None dn = np . empty ( nc , order = \"F\" ) if dn else None res = yaeos_c . pressure ( self . id , moles , volume , temperature , dpdv = dv , dpdt = dt , dpdn = dn ) Note the pattern: a boolean flag becomes an output buffer, and None means “do not ask for this derivative”. The buffers are allocated with order=\"F\" because the derivative matrices are Fortran-ordered on the\nother side. Step 4 — the f2py marshalling. yaeos_c.pressure reaches the\ngenerated C wrapper, which converts the Python objects to Fortran\narguments, calls the Fortran routine, and converts the results back. The\nrules that matter, each verified against a small f2py probe built from\nthis repository’s interface file: An intent(out) scalar becomes the value of the call. The pressure P is intent(out) , so the call returns a float. Several intent(out) arguments come back as a tuple in declaration order: flash has seven\n  of them, and the Python wrapper unpacks them as x, y, pressure, temperature, volume_x, volume_y, beta . An optional, intent(inout) argument becomes an optional keyword.\n  Omitting it, or passing None , means the argument is not passed at all\n  and the Fortran side sees .not. present(...) . A rank-0 argument wants\n  a zero-dimensional NumPy array; core.py uses np.array(0, dtype=np.float64) and reads the result back with float(...) . A one-dimensional array may be in any order. A two-dimensional array\n  that Fortran writes into must be Fortran-contiguous: passing a\n  C-ordered one raises ValueError: failed to initialize intent(inout)\n  array -- input not fortran contiguous . A two-dimensional array that\n  Fortran only reads is converted by f2py , so its logical values are\n  always right. A character(len=15) argument such as root_type receives a Python\n  string blank-padded to fifteen characters. Step 5 — the Fortran model. The wrapper calls ar_models(id)%model%pressure(...) , which dispatches dynamically to the\nconcrete model, which evaluates the residual Helmholtz energy and adds the\nideal-gas term. This is where the physics happens; everything above it is\nplumbing. Step 6 — the result. Back in Python the wrapper reshapes the outcome\ninto a consistent shape: a bare value when no derivative was requested,\nand a (value, derivatives) pair otherwise, with lowercase derivative\nkeys. Step 7 — the lifetime. ArModel.__del__ releases the Fortran slot\nwith yaeos_c.make_available_ar_models_list(self.id) ; GeModel.__del__ does the same with make_available_ge_models_list . Releasing a slot\nreturns it to the registry; the model object itself is only released when\nthe slot is reused. __del__ is best-effort, so a session that creates and\ndrops many models in a loop should not assume the memory comes back\nimmediately. Shapes, memory order and dtypes Fortran dummy argument Python buffer Rule n(:) amounts 1-D float64 Order irrelevant dPdn(size(n)) 1-D float64 Order irrelevant; order=\"F\" is used for consistency Arn2(size(n), size(n)) 2-D float64 Must be Fortran-contiguous aij(:,:) and friends 2-D float64 Read-only; f2py converts kij(:,:) in set_qmr 2-D float64 The Fortran dummy has no intent , so f2py may not convert it root_type str One of \"stable\" , \"liquid\" , \"vapor\" The last two rows are the sharpest edges in this layer. In c_interface/yaeos_c.f90 , set_qmr declares kij and lij and set_cmr declares kijk and lijk without an intent attribute .\nA dummy argument without intent carries no intent restriction (which is\nnot the same as declaring intent(inout) ), and f2py does not enforce the\nmemory order for it: in a probe built from\nthis interface file, a value written by Fortran into kij(1, 2) of a\nC-ordered array was not visible at kij[0, 1] in NumPy. The Python\nclasses avoid the problem by always passing an order=\"F\" copy: class QMR ( CubicMixRule ): def __init__ ( self , kij , lij ) -> None : self . kij = np . array ( kij , order = \"F\" ) self . lij = np . array ( lij , order = \"F\" ) def set_mixrule ( self , ar_model_id : int ) -> None : yaeos_c . set_qmr ( ar_model_id , self . kij , self . lij ) Follow that convention in any new model class: a C-ordered matrix handed\nto one of those routines may be misinterpreted without an error being\nraised. Note also that NRTL.__init__ stores the Fortran-ordered copies\nin self.a , self.b and self.c but passes the original arguments to yaeos_c.nrtl — harmless there because the nrtl dummies are intent(in) , so f2py converts them, but not the pattern to copy. Return conventions Kind of result Shape Scalar property float Property with derivatives (value, {\"dt\": ..., \"dv\": ..., \"dn\": ...}) Flash dict with x , y , Vx , Vy , P , T , beta Phase envelope PTEnvelope , PXEnvelope or TXEnvelope object Stability analysis ({\"w\": ..., \"tm\": ...}, {\"tm\": [...], \"w\": [...]}) Two failure conventions cross the boundary and must be checked. beta == -1 means the flash did not converge: the compositions are then the feed\ncomposition and the volumes are placeholders. NaN means a value does not\nexist — a saturation pressure that failed, or an envelope point that was\nnever computed. NumPy propagates NaN silently, so filter on it\nexplicitly. The envelope classes in python/yaeos/envelopes.py already\ndrop the NaN rows; a raw call to yaeos_c does not. The package mirrors the Fortran API python/yaeos/core.py defines GeModel and ArModel as abstract\nclasses that mirror the Fortran class(GeModel) and class(ArModel) .\nEach concrete model inherits one of them and implements size() , plus _model_params_as_str() and _model_params_declaration_as_str() , which\nrender Fortran source that constructs the model. Those last two are not\ndecoration: the equivalence tests use them to generate a Fortran program,\nrun it with fpm , and compare its output with the Python result, so a new\nmodel class that omits them cannot take part in that check. Adding a feature: wrapper, Python, tests Work through these in order; each step depends on the previous one. Add the flat routine in c_interface/yaeos_c.f90 , and add its name\n   to the module’s public :: list so that f2py generates a wrapper. Add the Python wrapper in python/yaeos/core.py , on the abstract\n   class that owns the property. Allocate the output buffers, pass the\n   handle, and reshape the result into the house style: a bare value, or a (value, derivatives) pair with lowercase derivative keys. Expose it publicly if it belongs to a concrete model: add the\n   method to the class in python/yaeos/models/ , and export the class\n   from python/yaeos/__init__.py if it is new. Add a test under python/tests/ . For a property, extend python/tests/models/residual_helmholtz/test_ar_same_as_fortran.py so\n   that the Fortran and the Python values are compared. For a model,\n   follow the neighbouring tests in the same directory. The testing guide covers what a good regression looks like. Update the Sphinx sources under python/docs/source/ if the new\n   method is part of the public API. Keep the styles : black with a line length of 79, flake8 with N803 , W503 and E203 ignored, and pydocstyle with the NumPy\n   convention. Testing The Python suite lives in python/tests/ and mirrors the source tree: models/residual_helmholtz/ for the equations of state, models/gibbs_excess/ for the activity models, models/algorithm/ for\nthe stability analysis, models/equilibria/ for flash calculations, and fitting/ for the parameter fitting. python/tox.ini defines the environments. The default one runs, in order, fpm run ge_python_test_values , pip install . , and pytest tests/ --import-mode=importlib . The first command runs the\nFortran helper in app/ge_python_test_values.f90 , which writes ge_test_vals.txt in the working directory; that file is gitignored. No\ntest in the current tree reads it, so treat it as a generated fixture\nrather than a required step. The strongest test in the suite is the Fortran–Python equivalence check.\nIt builds a Fortran program from the model’s own parameter source, writes\nit into the repository’s app/ directory, runs it with fpm run , and\ncompares the printed values with the Python results: fortran_code += \"program main \\n \" fortran_code += \"use yaeos \\n\\n \" fortran_code += \"implicit none \\n\\n \" fortran_code += ar_model . _model_params_declaration_as_str () fortran_code += ar_model . _model_params_as_str () fortran_code += thermoprops_code fortran_code += \"end program main\" Note that this test writes app/test_ar_program.f90 into the working\ntree. Do not commit it. Other environments: tox -e style runs flake8 , tox -e docstyle runs pydocstyle , tox -e docs builds the\nSphinx pages, and tox -e coverage runs the suite with coverage and\nfails below 50 %. Debugging boundaries Symptom Look first at Then at ImportError on yaeos.lib.yaeos_python The extension build in python/meson.build The fpm install step and the Fortran compiler version AttributeError on a yaeos_c function The public :: list in c_interface/yaeos_c.f90 Whether the extension was rebuilt ValueError: ... not fortran contiguous The order=\"F\" on the allocated buffer The Fortran dummy’s intent A matrix argument gives wrong numbers Whether the routine writes into the matrix Whether the dummy declares an intent A derivative is wrong but the value is right The buffer allocated for that derivative Whether the model supports that derivative at all A returned array has NaN in it The failure convention of that routine The equilibrium solver The Python and Fortran results disagree The _model_params_as_str output The component ordering of the model The build stops at the Fortran version check The compiler version in python/meson.build The flags, which are GNU-specific Known sharp edges The yaeos_c namespace is generated from the Fortran module and has no\n  stability guarantee. Use the public classes. The extension module is built from c_interface/yaeos_c.f90 , so a\n  change there needs a rebuild, not just a reinstall. The Meson project version ( 3.1.0 ) disagrees with the package version\n  ( 4.5.4 ). set_qmr and set_cmr declare their matrix arguments without an intent , which makes them sensitive to the caller’s memory order. NRTL.__init__ passes the original arrays rather than the\n  Fortran-ordered copies it stores. __del__ releases the Fortran slot, not the model memory. The equivalence tests write generated Fortran programs into app/ . Related pages Developer handbook — the rest of the developer guide. Architecture — how the Fortran layers fit together. Developer workflow — the build, the toolchain, and the review loop. Fortran internals — the core library this layer wraps. Numerics — the solvers behind the equilibrium routines. Testing — what a good regression test looks like. End-to-end change — planning a feature across all the layers. C interface layer — the flat layer underneath this one. Python API guide — the user-facing guide. Adding your own models — the user-facing guide for extending the library.","tags":"","url":"page/developer/python_interface.html"},{"title":"Tests and numerical validation – yaeos","text":"Write a test that catches the bug you fixed Where tests live and how they run Add a small native regression 1. Write a standalone program 2. Register and run the program Decide what kind of evidence a test provides Derivatives: test the variables the interface actually uses Thermodynamic checks that expose amount or reference errors Scaling and homogeneity Heat-capacity identity Symmetry, Gibbs–Duhem, and limits Phase calculations: check physics as well as termination Cross-language checks: shapes before values Choose tolerances to match the claim A minimal pre-review test plan Measure coverage Write a test that catches the bug you fixed A useful test distinguishes correct behavior from a plausible wrong answer.\nFor yaeos , that means testing mathematical contracts, units, array shapes,\nphase balances, and failure handling—not only checking that an iteration\nfinished or a value is finite. This guide starts with a small native test, then adds the independent checks\nneeded for thermodynamic and cross-language work. Use workflow for prerequisites and build commands. Where tests live and how they run The native tests are standalone Fortran programs under test/ , one file per\nprogram ( test_<feature>.f90 ). The project no longer uses test-drive suites or\na collector program. Every test is an independent executable, so a failure can\nbe run and debugged on its own. The directories under test/ mirror src/ . A test goes in the directory\nthat corresponds to the source it exercises. For example, tests of src/equilibria/flash.f90 are in test/equilibria/ , and tests of the cubic\nmixing rules in src/models/residual_helmholtz/cubic/mixing_rules/ are in test/models/residual_helmholtz/cubic/mixing_rules/ . A test that exercises\nseveral files goes in the directory of the routine it is mainly about. Shared helpers are in the local package test/support/ , which fpm.toml lists as a dev-dependency. It holds testing_aux ( assert , test_title , in test/support/src/yaeos_testing.f90 ) and the model fixtures. It is a separate\npackage because fpm only lets a test program use modules from its own\ndirectory or below. A helper module used by tests in a single directory can\nstay next to them. auto-tests is off: every test program has a [[test]] entry in fpm.toml ,\ngenerated by tools/dev/test_to_toml.py . Each test is named after its file\nwithout the test_ prefix ( test_flash.f90 is the target flash ), so file\nnames must be unique across all directories. CI runs the script with --check and fails if fpm.toml is out of date. Python tests are under python/tests/ . Some compare directly with native\nFortran calls by generating small programs in app/ . Those tests need a\ncheckout, fpm , and a working native build as well as the Python package. Add a small native regression 1. Write a standalone program Save this complete program as test_my_feature.f90 , in the test/ subdirectory that mirrors the source it tests (here a residual-model\ncalculation, so test/models/residual_helmholtz/ ), in a working development\nbranch. It follows the program-based pattern used by test/models/residual_helmholtz/cubic/mixing_rules/test_huron_vidal.f90 , test/models/residual_helmholtz/cubic/mixing_rules/test_sddlc.f90 , and test/equilibria/test_flash_optional_iters.f90 : program test_my_feature use yaeos , only : pr , ArModel , PengRobinson76 use testing_aux , only : assert , test_title use , intrinsic :: ieee_arithmetic , only : ieee_is_finite implicit none class ( ArModel ), allocatable :: model real ( pr ) :: n ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: specified_pressure real ( pr ) :: calculated_pressure real ( pr ) :: volume real ( pr ) :: temperature write ( * , * ) test_title ( \"Volume/pressure regression\" ) model = PengRobinson76 ([ 19 0.564_pr , 30 5.32_pr ], & [ 4 5.99_pr , 4 8.72_pr ], [ 0.01142_pr , 0.0995_pr ]) n = [ 0.3_pr , 0.7_pr ] specified_pressure = 1 0.0_pr temperature = 32 0.0_pr call model % volume ( n , specified_pressure , temperature , volume , & root_type = \"stable\" ) call assert ( ieee_is_finite ( volume ), \"Volume must be finite\" ) call assert ( volume > 0.0_pr , \"Volume must be positive\" ) call model % pressure ( n , volume , temperature , calculated_pressure ) call assert ( ieee_is_finite ( calculated_pressure ), \"Pressure must be finite\" ) call assert ( abs ( calculated_pressure - specified_pressure ) < 1.0e-7_pr , & \"Volume reproduces the specified pressure\" ) end program test_my_feature testing_aux is the repository’s test helper module, defined in test/support/src/yaeos_testing.f90 , not part of the installed public yaeos API. test_title formats a heading; assert(condition, message) reports a passing\ncheck or terminates the program with error stop on failure. A failing\nprerequisite therefore stops before later calculations use an invalid result.\nPrinting a warning without a nonzero exit status is not a failing test. For a group of related checks, put private test subroutines after contains and call them explicitly from the program body. There is no collector or\nsuite registry to update. 2. Register and run the program From the repository root, after saving the file in the directory that mirrors\nthe source it tests: python tools/dev/test_to_toml.py # adds the [[test]] entry to fpm.toml fpm test --list\nfpm test my_feature The target name is the filename without test_ , so use a descriptive and\nunique test_*.f90 name. The script only registers files that contain a program unit and its end program , and it refuses duplicate names. Commit\nthe fpm.toml change with the test. Run the focused target first, then fpm test before review. Decide what kind of evidence a test provides Check What it establishes What it does not establish Compare with independent data Agreement for specified states and parameters Accuracy everywhere Numerical derivative check Local agreement of derivatives and energy evaluation That the energy equation is the intended physical model Thermodynamic identity Internal consistency of related quantities Independence of shared implementation errors Material balance and isofugacity A candidate phase split satisfies its equations Global stability against all other splits Native/Python comparison Agreement across wrapper paths Independent verification of their shared native kernel Regression reproducer A particular failure remains fixed Coverage of unrelated edge cases Use more than one kind of evidence for a new model or solver. Two routines\nthat share the same incorrect derivative can agree perfectly. Derivatives: test the variables the interface actually uses For a scalar property , a central-difference estimate is Choose steps with both an absolute and relative scale so that a zero input\nstill gets a nonzero perturbation. Repeat at several step sizes. Small steps\namplify cancellation; large steps introduce truncation error. Neither choosing\nthe smallest possible number nor copying another test’s step blindly is a\nreliable validation strategy. Test the amount derivatives required by ArModel and GeModel , not\nderivatives of normalized mole fractions. For an equilibrium Jacobian in log\ncoordinates, perturb the packed log variable and include its chain-rule\nfactor. Avoid changing volume-root branches during a derivative check intended\nfor a single smooth branch. Useful native helpers include numeric_ar_derivatives , ar_consistency , numeric_ge_derivatives , ge_consistency , and individual_ar_calls under src/consistency/ . Read their interfaces and the cases that call them:\ndifferent outputs have different scalar, vector, and matrix shapes. individual_ar_calls helps check optional-output combinations; it does not\nreplace a mathematical accuracy check. For a derivative implementation, cover energy only, each derivative separately,\nall outputs together, mixed-output requests, and repeated calls at different\nstates. AD implementations need these checks too: differentiation propagates\nthe program’s operations, including mistakes in its primal expression. Thermodynamic checks that expose amount or reference errors Scaling and homogeneity For residual Helmholtz energy at fixed temperature, Pressure and fugacity coefficients should stay unchanged under that same\njoint scaling. Scaling amounts without volume changes the density, so it is\nnot the same test. For an excess-Gibbs model, , whereas activity\ncoefficients are unchanged. These checks help catch a missing total-amount\nfactor or an inappropriate normalization. Heat-capacity identity At fixed component amounts, define and . The total heat capacities\nsatisfy . Subtracting the ideal-gas difference gives Here and . First derivatives of residual\nHelmholtz energy cannot simply be multiplied to obtain this identity. This insertable test fragment assumes an initialized model and declared n , V , and T ; import pr and R from yaeos and assert from testing_aux . Place the declarations before executable statements.\nChoose a regular state where dPdV is nonzero: real ( pr ) :: P real ( pr ) :: dPdT real ( pr ) :: dPdV real ( pr ) :: Cp real ( pr ) :: Cv real ( pr ) :: expected_difference call model % pressure ( n , V , T , P , dPdT = dPdT , dPdV = dPdV ) call model % Cp_residual_vt ( n , V , T , Cp = Cp ) call model % Cv_residual_vt ( n , V , T , Cv = Cv ) expected_difference = - T * dPdT ** 2 / dPdV - sum ( n ) * R call assert ( abs ( Cp - Cv - expected_difference ) < 1.0e-8_pr , & \"Residual heat capacities satisfy the pressure-derivative identity\" ) This tests consistency of property relations and derivative paths. An\nindependent derivative check is still needed to diagnose a shared model error.\nNear singular states, conditioning and cancellation can make this a poor\nchoice of numerical test even when the identity is valid. Symmetry, Gibbs–Duhem, and limits For a smooth excess-Gibbs model, the Hessian of is symmetric and . ge_consistency checks\nthese relations and the link between energy and activity coefficients. The\nmatrix of first mole derivatives of is a Jacobian , not a\nHessian of . Check dilute-gas limits where applicable: , ,\nand residual energy tends to zero as density tends to zero at fixed amount\nand temperature. For activity models using pure-component reference states,\ncheck appropriate pure limits and parameter conventions. Use a sufficiently\ndilute finite state, not a singular zero-volume or zero-amount calculation. Phase calculations: check physics as well as termination For an accepted two-phase result, check: Finite, nonnegative phase compositions, each summing to one. Finite phase fractions in their intended domain. Component balance: z = (1-beta)*x + beta*y . Volume balance for a VT flash, on the routine’s specified molar basis. Log-fugacity agreement on the same roots used by the solver. Distinct phases when the requested calculation should not be trivial. For multiphase output, reconstruct the feed using all phase fractions,\nincluding the reference phase. MPEquilibriumState%np counts main phases in\nthe current representation; read the corresponding routine’s contract rather\nthan assuming it is the total count. Test ordinary cases, relevant boundaries, and a failure path. Keep\nnear-critical conditioning tests distinct from ordinary accuracy tests. A\nconverged root can be metastable, and a finite search that finds no destabilizing\ntrial phase is not a proof of global stability. Failure conventions differ. Some routines return a sentinel, some return an\nearly result or status field, and some terminate with error stop . Test the\nspecific contract; do not read uninitialized result arrays after a failed\ncall. To test an expected terminating failure, use a subprocess and check\nits exit status from a harness. Do not add a program that always exits nonzero\nas an ordinary passing fpm test target: fpm correctly treats that exit as\na test failure. Cross-language checks: shapes before values Python tests under python/tests/ include native-reference comparisons. Some\ngenerate Fortran programs in app/ , execute them with fpm , and parse their\noutput with helpers such as parse_vec and parse_mat . Inspect an existing\ntest for the exact protocol instead of inventing another incompatible one. For an interface change, check: The same model parameters, state, units, and component ordering on both sides. Scalar/vector/matrix shape before comparing values. Matrix orientation with a nonsymmetric test case where appropriate. Dtype, contiguous layout, and Fortran-order buffers where the wrapper requires them. Each optional derivative alone and in combination. The return container and unrequested-output convention, not only its numbers. Model creation/deletion and invalid-handle behavior where safely defined. A Python test that agrees with native Fortran validates the adaptation layer,\nnot the physical model independently. Test an installed build after changes to\nthe compiled wrapper; a stale extension can conceal or mimic a regression. Choose tolerances to match the claim A practical floating-point comparison uses both scales: Choose atol in the quantity’s units and rtol for its expected scale.\nNear-zero consistency residuals need an absolute criterion. Properties spanning\norders of magnitude often need a relative criterion too. Do not compare\nlog-fugacity mismatch, temperature error, and material balance with a single\nunexplained tolerance. State the reason for a reference value and its tolerance in the test. Consider\nrounding in published parameters, conditioning, compiler differences, and\nfinite-difference error. Do not loosen a tolerance until a failure disappears\nwithout understanding why. CI runs on both an older and the newest Ubuntu image. The newest images use\nx86-64-v3 (AVX2/FMA) builds of gfortran and BLAS/LAPACK, and fused\nmultiply-add changes the last bits of results. A limit of a few units in the\nlast place, such as 1e-15 on a thermodynamic identity, can then fail on one\nmachine and pass on another. Two measured examples set the scale: second derivatives compared with central finite differences (step about 1e-4 ) agree to about 1e-6 relative in every build; the Michelsen-Mollerup identities hold to about 1e-15 . The cubic EoS tests therefore use 1e-5 and 1e-13 . Those limits sit well\nabove the rounding noise, and real derivative bugs give errors of order one.\nTo check a test against FMA rounding locally: fpm test --profile release --flag \"-g -ffree-line-length-0 -march=x86-64-v3\" my_feature Adding -ffp-contract=off to the same command disables FMA contraction. If\nthe failure then disappears, rounding is the cause and the tolerance is too\ntight. A minimal pre-review test plan Run the affected target first, then the native suite. Rebuild the Python\nextension and run relevant Python tests for an interface change. Build the\ndocumentation and run or compile its complete examples separately: successful\nsite generation does not execute the tutorial. In the review summary, report which layers were tested, the revision and\nenvironment, and anything unavailable. Coverage is useful for finding unvisited\nbranches, but it cannot establish that the equations, reference data, or\nassertions are correct. Measure coverage CI measures line coverage from the release-profile test run and uploads it to\nCodecov. The project target is 95%, and generated code such as the Tapenade\nPC-SAFT derivatives counts toward it. To reproduce the measurement locally\n(it needs gcovr ): fpm test --profile release --flag \"-g -coverage -ffree-line-length-0\" bash ci/ci.sh coverage ci/ci.sh coverage applies the same exclusions as CI: tests, examples, app/ , tools/ , the legacy API and the vendored hyperdual module. Use gcovr --html-details with those exclusions to see the uncovered lines. A new\ntest should check a result, not just execute lines. Continue with workflow , numerical implementation ,\nor the end-to-end change walkthrough .","tags":"","url":"page/developer/testing.html"},{"title":"An end-to-end change – yaeos","text":"Plan a small feature from calculation to public API 1. Define a contract in a few sentences 2. Make the calculation work outside the library first 3. Choose tests that can catch your own mistakes 4. Decide where the core feature belongs 5. Add language exposure only when it is needed 6. Review compatibility before changing an existing signature 7. Submit evidence, not just a passing screenshot Plan a small feature from calculation to public API This walkthrough uses a small property helper to show how a contribution\ngrows across the codebase. The mathematical task is deliberately simple:\ncalculate the compressibility factor is dimensionless and equals one for an ideal gas. It is not the same\nquantity as the isothermal compressibility, which is a derivative of volume\nwith respect to pressure. Naming the desired property precisely is the first\npart of a good feature proposal. The helper below is application code , not a new method already supplied by ArModel . No library modification is necessary to run it. The later sections\ndescribe a possible contribution plan, not commands that silently add that\nmethod to your checkout. 1. Define a contract in a few sentences Given an EOS model, component mole amounts n , total volume V in L, and\ntemperature T in K, evaluate model pressure in bar and return . Use the\ntotal amount, not the length of the composition array or an implicit one-mole\nnormalization. The physical state is homogeneous: the helper does not perform\na flash or decide whether the mixture is stable. Before adding a method to the library, search for equivalent functionality and\ndiscuss whether a helper is useful enough to publish. A one-line application\ncalculation can be preferable to expanding a maintained public interface. 2. Make the calculation work outside the library first The following is a complete Fortran source file. Put it in an application’s app/main.f90 after configuring the yaeos dependency as in getting started : module application_properties use yaeos , only : pr , R , ArModel implicit none private public :: z_factor contains function z_factor ( model , n , V , T ) result ( Z ) class ( ArModel ), intent ( in ) :: model real ( pr ), intent ( in ) :: n (:) real ( pr ), intent ( in ) :: V real ( pr ), intent ( in ) :: T real ( pr ) :: Z real ( pr ) :: P if ( any ( n < 0.0_pr ) . or . sum ( n ) <= 0.0_pr ) & error stop \"Invalid mole amounts\" if ( V <= 0.0_pr . or . T <= 0.0_pr ) error stop \"Invalid V or T\" call model % pressure ( n , V , T , P ) Z = P * V / ( sum ( n ) * R * T ) end function z_factor end module application_properties program check_property_helper use yaeos , only : pr , ArModel , PengRobinson76 use application_properties , only : z_factor implicit none class ( ArModel ), allocatable :: model real ( pr ) :: n ( 2 ) real ( pr ) :: Z real ( pr ) :: Z_scaled real ( pr ) :: Z_dilute model = PengRobinson76 ([ 19 0.564_pr , 30 5.32_pr ], & [ 4 5.99_pr , 4 8.72_pr ], [ 0.01142_pr , 0.0995_pr ]) n = [ 0.3_pr , 0.7_pr ] Z = z_factor ( model , n , 1.0_pr , 32 0.0_pr ) Z_scaled = z_factor ( model , 1 0.0_pr * n , 1 0.0_pr , 32 0.0_pr ) Z_dilute = z_factor ( model , n , 1000 0.0_pr , 32 0.0_pr ) if ( abs ( Z - Z_scaled ) > 1.0e-10_pr ) error stop \"Scaling check failed\" if ( abs ( Z_dilute - 1.0_pr ) > 1.0e-3_pr ) & error stop \"Dilute-gas check failed\" write ( * , '(A,F12.8)' ) \"Z at the test state: \" , Z write ( * , '(A,F12.8)' ) \"Z in the dilute limit: \" , Z_dilute end program check_property_helper The error stop checks are convenient in this demonstration application. They\nare not a recommendation to terminate every host process from a public\nlibrary routine. A production API needs deliberate invalid-input and failure\nreporting, especially when called from Python or another language. 3. Choose tests that can catch your own mistakes This example checks two independent properties: Scaling: multiplying amounts and total volume by ten must leave unchanged. This catches accidental normalization of only one input. Dilute behavior: at a sufficiently large volume, a suitable EOS should\n  approach ideal-gas behavior. This catches some unit and formula errors. Neither check proves the EOS is accurate for methane–ethane at all conditions.\nAdd an ordinary regression state and a known reference calculation if the\nproperty becomes public. Also test the one-component case, different amount\nbases, component reordering with corresponding model reordering, and relevant\ninvalid inputs. Use an appropriate tolerance for each claim, not one absolute\ntolerance for every physical quantity. For a bug fix, preserve the smallest input that previously failed. Keep the\nregression test after the fix, and explain the failure mechanism in the change\ndescription. See testing for native test-program and Python\ntest conventions. 4. Decide where the core feature belongs There are two plausible designs: a standalone utility that accepts class(ArModel) , or a shared type-bound method that uses the existing pressure\nbinding. Neither needs a separate implementation for each cubic EOS. If choosing a type-bound method, inspect src/models/residual_helmholtz/ar_models.f90 for the surrounding conventions.\nIf choosing a standalone public utility, check the relevant facade exports.\nDocument units, the amount basis, whether pressure is computed or supplied,\nand whether a volume-root choice exists. Do not simultaneously change all neighboring code to a new style. Keep the\nfunctional change small enough that a reviewer can distinguish its contract\nfrom unrelated refactoring. 5. Add language exposure only when it is needed A Python-only convenience method can already calculate this quantity using\nthe existing pressure wrapper: import numpy as np from yaeos import PengRobinson76 model = PengRobinson76 ( [ 190.564 , 305.32 ], [ 45.99 , 48.72 ], [ 0.01142 , 0.0995 ] ) moles = np . array ([ 0.3 , 0.7 ]) volume = 1.0 temperature = 320.0 pressure = model . pressure ( moles , volume , temperature ) gas_constant = 0.08314462618 # bar L / (mol K), matching the Fortran library z = pressure * volume / ( moles . sum () * gas_constant * temperature ) This is a complete Python example when an appropriate yaeos installation is\navailable. A user-level convenience calculation does not automatically justify\nanother compiled entry point. For a feature that does need a new native wrapper, make an explicit plan: Layer Change to consider Evidence to add Fortran kernel/shared property Implement the mathematical contract Native regression and limiting cases Fortran public API Add binding/export if needed A small external caller compiles yaeos_c wrapper Adapt handles, kinds, dimensions, and optional results Wrapper matches native values and shapes Python method Convert inputs and preserve result/derivative conventions Python regression and invalid-input tests Package/build metadata Export or install new modules if necessary A fresh installation imports them FORD and Sphinx guides Explain usage, restrictions, and references Both documentation builds and links checked Use existing wrappers as patterns, but verify their assumptions. Avoid adding\na supposedly C-callable routine solely by using real(c_double) : interoperability\nalso depends on binding, shapes, argument passing, and ownership. See the C-facing interface guide . 6. Review compatibility before changing an existing signature Adding a native optional argument can affect a generated wrapper’s signature,\neven if old Fortran calls still compile. Changing array orientation can\nsilently transpose a result in Python. Renaming a result field can break\nplotting and fitting tools that never call the Fortran routine directly. Search both native and Python call sites, examples, tests, and documentation.\nFor a new model type, also check constructors, facade exports, handle storage,\nPython package exports, source-install lists in Meson, and object lifetime. 7. Submit evidence, not just a passing screenshot Explain the intended behavior, the affected layers, the numerical basis of the\ntests, and the validation commands actually run. State any unavailable check,\nsuch as an unbuilt Python extension. A helpful review summary distinguishes\n“native Fortran tests pass”, “Python wrapper tests pass”, and “documentation\nexamples compile”; none is a substitute for the others. Use workflow for the build commands and the developer landing page to choose the next area to learn.","tags":"","url":"page/developer/walkthrough.html"},{"title":"Contributing – yaeos","text":"Start with a small, reproducible change You can contribute without implementing a new EOS. A clarified unit, a runnable\nexample, a regression test, or an explanation of a contract that is currently\nonly implied by the source are all useful contributions. For a first change,\nreproduce one existing example, identify one confusing or incorrect behavior,\nand keep the fix focused. Discuss a new model or a public interface change with\nmaintainers before investing in its implementation. This page covers how to contribute: which layer a change belongs to, how to\nbuild and test it, and how to write documentation that teaches. The developer handbook covers the codebase itself — the\nrepository map, the Fortran contracts, the numerical algorithms, the language\ninterfaces, and the detailed build and test procedures. Read that handbook for where things live and how the tooling is configured; read this page for what a good contribution looks like. Start with a small, reproducible change Find the right layer Build and test before you change anything What a good test looks like Documentation workflow Write for a learner Example of the declaration style Before requesting review Project contribution policy Find the right layer Goal Start here Learn how a calculation is set up example/basics/ and doc/page/usage/ Add a thermodynamic equation src/models/ and the new-model guides Understand derivative checks src/consistency/ and the existing model tests Fix equilibrium behavior Reduce the state first, then inspect src/equilibria/ and related tests Improve user instructions doc/page/ , which becomes the FORD user guide Explain a public routine’s contract Documentation comments beside its declaration in src/ Read the actual interface and a current caller together. In particular, do not\ninfer whether a binding is a function or a subroutine from its name: the\nhyperdual and Tapenade energy interfaces differ, and the Tapenade routines are\nsubroutines with a strict argument order. Configuring an existing model is\nalmost always preferable to introducing an unnecessary new type. The architecture page maps each of these\nlayers to the files that implement it. Build and test before you change anything Establish a working baseline first. An unchanged build and an unchanged example\nthat runs tell you whether a later failure is yours. From the repository root, a GNU-toolchain loop consistent with the CI\nconfiguration in .github/workflows/CI.yml is: fpm build --profile release --flag \"-g -ffree-line-length-0\" --compiler gfortran --c-compiler gcc\nfpm run --example basics --profile release --flag \"-g -ffree-line-length-0\" --compiler gfortran --c-compiler gcc\nfpm test --profile release --flag \"-g -ffree-line-length-0\" --compiler gfortran --c-compiler gcc Run these sequentially, never as competing builds of the same tree. The basics target is registered explicitly in fpm.toml ; automatic example\ndiscovery is disabled, so a file dropped into example/extra/ is not a\nrunnable target until it is registered. Compiler flags above are\nGNU-specific, not a portable interface requirement. Useful first diagnoses: a missing module during the first build usually means\nan earlier dependency or compiler failure, so read the first error rather\nthan the last. Undefined BLAS or LAPACK symbols point at library\ninstallation and linking, not at thermodynamic algebra. Report compiler\nversions, flags, and the first failing command when asking for help. The workflow page documents the dependencies,\nthe separate Python build, the documentation build, and the pitfalls that\ncost time. The testing page shows how the tests\nare structured and how to add a standalone regression program. What a good test looks like Do not only demonstrate that a flash converges. For a model, check energy\nunits and extensivity, derivatives against numerical differences, each\noptional output requested on its own, and the thermodynamic identities before\ntesting any equilibrium calculation. Use physically meaningful states away\nfrom singularities first, and explain the tolerance you assert: compare\nquantities that carry the same units, and say whether the threshold is\nabsolute or relative. A regression test should fail for the original defect. If it passes both\nbefore and after your change, it is not testing the change. The testing handbook gives the standalone-program pattern\nand the tolerance rules used by the existing suite. Documentation workflow The Fortran documentation is generated by FORD. ford.md selects src/ for\nAPI extraction, doc/page/ for guide pages, doc/media/ for assets, and doc/ford_site/ for generated output. Edit the source Markdown and the\nsource comments, never the generated HTML. Preserve page names and anchors\nwhere possible; the existing analtical.md spelling, for instance, is part of\nthe link structure of the guide. With FORD and its rendering prerequisites installed (the documentation workflow\ninstalls Graphviz, Pandoc, and the Python packages), build from the\nrepository root: ford ford.md Then open the generated page and read it. A zero exit status does not mean the\npage is correct: check cross-references, mathematics, code blocks, tables,\nnavigation, and images, and read the generation warnings. An unresolved [[symbol]] link is not repaired by ordinary Markdown rendering. Do not build\nthe documentation while another process is writing the same output tree. The Python API has a separate Sphinx workflow driven by .github/workflows/docs.yml ; it is not rebuilt by FORD alone. Write for a learner Use this sequence for a new guide or a substantial example: Purpose and prerequisite. State the physical question and what the\n   reader should already know. Say when to choose a different model or\n   workflow instead. Meaning before syntax. Define the thermodynamic potential, the reference\n   state, the units, and which variables a derivative holds fixed. Distinguish\n   a total from a molar quantity, and a residual from an absolute property. A small runnable example. Include the imports, the declarations, the\n   parameter setup, and the property call. Label partial snippets as\n   fragments; never present an abstract type or pseudocode as a complete\n   program. Declare one variable per line, as shown in the style guide . Interpretation. Say what each output means and what the reader should\n   check. A residual close to zero is a diagnostic, not proof that a model is\n   accurate. The failure path. Explain the likely unit, domain, shape,\n   initialization, and convergence errors, and where to look next. Evidence and the next step. Compile the examples where possible, state\n   the assumptions, and link the actual implementations and tests. Never\n   invent numerical results or references. Distinguish a published equation\n   from an illustrative parameter set. Keep API comments and guide prose complementary. The API reference gives the\nexact arguments, ranks, optionality, and units of a routine; the guide\nexplains why a reader would call it and how to interpret the answer. Describe why a numerical safeguard exists, not merely that the code contains one. When a page needs to point at the implementation, link to the source path and\nto the developer handbook rather than repeating the codebase tour. A page\nshould answer one reader’s question well. Example of the declaration style New and modified declarations use one variable per line, so that each quantity\ncan carry its own unit or physical meaning: use yaeos , only : pr implicit none real ( pr ), intent ( in ) :: n (:) !! Component mole amounts [mol] real ( pr ), intent ( in ) :: V !! Total volume [L], not molar volume real ( pr ), intent ( in ) :: T !! Temperature [K] real ( pr ), intent ( out ) :: P !! Pressure [bar] real ( pr ) :: tol !! Absolute comparison tolerance [bar] This is a declaration fragment inside a procedure, not a standalone program.\nDo not group these as real(pr) :: n(2), V, T, P or integer :: i, j . The style guide states the rule and its rationale. Before requesting review Summarize the behavior that changed and why the change belongs at this\n  layer. Include a reproducer and the commands you actually ran. Identify anything\n  you could not test, and anything that was already failing before your\n  change. Confirm that the documentation examples match the current interfaces and\n  units, and that they compile. Check the diff for unrelated edits, generated files, and accidental changes\n  to parameter data. Do not discard another contributor’s work. For generated automatic-differentiation routines, record the primal source,\n  the generator and its version, and any integration edits, so that the\n  derivatives can be regenerated and re-checked. Keep the commit focused. One logical change per branch or commit makes the\n  difference between a reviewable patch and a merge conflict. Project contribution policy For contribution policy and issue-reporting guidance, read the repository’s CONTRIBUTING.md .\nFor a guided tour of the implementation, continue with the developer handbook .","tags":"","url":"page/contributing/index.html"},{"title":"Style guide – yaeos","text":"Readability is part of the numerical interface Thermodynamic code is easier to review when readers can connect each quantity\nto an equation, its units, and its independent variables. Prefer descriptive\nnames for algorithms and conventional symbols for equations; define the latter\nwhere they first appear. Use three-space indentation, implicit none , explicit\nimports where practical, and named closing statements. One variable per declaration In new code and pedagogical examples, put one variable on each declaration\nline , including scalars, arrays, loop indices, and derived-type components.\nThis leaves room for a useful unit or physical meaning and makes changes easy\nto review. For example: use yaeos , only : pr implicit none real ( pr ), intent ( in ) :: n (:) !! Component mole amounts [mol] real ( pr ), intent ( in ) :: V !! Total volume [L], not molar volume real ( pr ), intent ( in ) :: T !! Temperature [K] real ( pr ), intent ( out ), optional :: ArV !! (dAr/dV) at fixed n,T [bar] real ( pr ), allocatable :: kij (:, :) !! Dimensionless interaction matrix integer :: i !! Component index integer :: j !! Component index This is a declaration fragment inside a procedure, not a standalone program.\nDo not group these as real(pr) :: n(2), V, T or integer :: i, j . The\nrepository guide included below contains older grouped-array examples; use the\none-variable convention for new declarations rather than copying those groups.\nDo not reformat unrelated source as part of a small fix. The same rule applies to derived-type components and to dummy arguments: type :: MyModel real ( pr ), allocatable :: ac (:) !! Pure-component attraction parameters [bar L&#94;2/mol&#94;2] real ( pr ), allocatable :: b (:) !! Pure-component covolumes [L/mol] contains procedure :: residual_helmholtz => my_ar !! Evaluate A&#94;r and its derivatives procedure :: get_v0 => my_v0 !! Volume scale for the liquid root [L] end type MyModel A reader comparing your code with the Fortran core guide of the developer\nhandbook should find the same conventions in\nboth. Comments should add information: “total volume [L]” is useful; “variable V” is\nnot. Array comments should explain index order when it matters, especially for\ninteraction matrices and Hessians. Precision, units, and derivative names Import pr and use real(pr) and kind-qualified real literals such as 1.0_pr . Import R instead of hard-coding a gas constant with different units. Use the library’s bar, L, K, mol basis. An extensive energy is in bar L, not\n  bar L/mol. State any conversions at external data boundaries. Use thermodynamic input order n, V, T or n, P, T . Name optional outputs\n  at call sites when positional lists would obscure the derivative requested. Energy derivatives use ArV , ArT , ArTV , ArV2 , Arn , Arn2 , etc.;\n  general properties use names such as dPdV . State what stays fixed rather\n  than relying on a short variable name alone. The shape of a mole gradient is size(n) ; the mole Hessian has two component\n  indices. A normalized composition derivative is not a mole derivative. Match contracts rather than improving them accidentally Specify intent for procedure arguments when defining a new interface. When\noverriding an existing deferred binding, however, match its declared attributes\nexactly. The hyperdual Ar passed-object dummy has no intent ; adding one can\nbreak the override. Analytic residual_helmholtz , hyperdual Ar , and Tapenade ar are different contracts, despite related names. Guard optional outputs with present . Calculate shared working quantities in\nlocal variables, not in an optional output that a caller may omit. Every\nrequested output must be assigned regardless of which others are present.\nDocument required initialization, array sizes, admissible domains, and any\nassumptions such as symmetric parameter matrices. Explain numerical choices and test them Explain why a bound, step size, or tolerance is appropriate and give its units\nor scale. Avoid unexplained “magic” constants. Test ordinary states before\nedge cases, then add dilute, pure-component, and near-boundary cases relevant\nto the model. Do not turn a failed convergence check into a successful result\nby silently replacing nonfinite values. For documentation, pair a mathematical definition with one minimal call and\nan interpretation of its output. Preserve existing examples and references\nwhen expanding a page. Compile runnable snippets against the current interface\nand label sketches honestly. See the contributing workflow for the\nbuild, test, and FORD preview steps, and the developer handbook for the surrounding codebase. Link to the rendered documentation In guide text, link to the generated page, for example [Mixing rules](../usage/eos/cubics/mixing.html) , rather than a local .md file. Resolve the path relative to the rendered page, and check any #anchor against the generated HTML. FORD does not automatically rewrite ordinary\nMarkdown links from .md to .html . This differs from FORD’s ordered_subpage front matter and include directives:\nthose identify source files and must retain their .md filenames.\nAn explicitly labelled external GitHub source-file link can also legitimately\nend in .md ; it is not an internal documentation navigation link. Repository-wide style reference yaeos Style Guide Adopting a consistent style can improve code legibility through the choice of good naming conventions.\nIn addition, style checks will be run during CI to flag any severe non-conformance.\nThis allows code review discussions to focus on semantics and substance rather than pedantry.\nConsistent whitespace usage, and not polluting line endings with trailing white space makes git diff s considerably more legible.\nThis style guide is a living document and proposed changes may be adopted after discussing them and coming to a consensus. Use (modern) standard Fortran Do not use obsolescent or deleted language features\n  E.g., common , pause , entry , arithmetic if and computed goto Do not use vendor extensions in the form of non-standard syntax and vendor supplied intrinsic procedures\n  E.g., real*8 or etime() , use real(pr) Naming of variables and constructs Variable and procedure names, as well as Fortran keywords, should be written in lowercase All constants (like the R gas constant) should be upper case All variables should be in lowercase unless they are represented in uppercase in the bibliography.\n  for example, the molar volume would be v and the total volume would be V . In the case of derivatives, for general properties they should be written as dXdy for first derivatives and dXdyz or dXdy2 for second order derivatives. The only exception is with energetic properties, like Ar , where derivatives are written like ArV , ArVn , etc. All thermodynamic variables that are input of a subroutine/function should\n  have the order: n, V, T n, P, T Variable and procedure names should be made up of one or more full words separated by an underscore,\n  for example has_failed is preferred over hasfailed Where conventional and appropriate shortening of a word is used then the underscore may be omitted,\n  for example linspace is preferred over lin_space For derived types use CamelCase, like ArModel File naming conventions Source files should contain at most one program , module , or submodule The filename should match the program or module name and have the file extension .f90 or .F90 if preprocessing is required module names should include its subdirectory, using yaeos__ for the parent src directory. For example the module in src/equilibria/flash.f90 is named yaeos__equilibria_flash . If the module name is longer than the accepted number of columns of the compiler, it is possible to simplify to just initials of each word: yaeos__p_e_f If the interface and implementation is split using submodules the implementation submodule file should have the same name as the\n  interface (parent) module but end in _implementation E.g., string_class.f90 and string_class_implementation.f90 Tests should be added in the test subdirectory and have the same name as the module they are testing with the test_ prefix\n  added E.g., string_class.f90 and test/test_string_class.f90 Indentation & whitespace By setting and following a convention for indentation and whitespace, code\nreviews and git-diffs can focus on the semantics of the proposed changes rather\nthan style and formatting. We recommend ~~enforce~~ the use of findent to format your files. The body of every Fortran construct should be indented by three (3) spaces Line length should be limited to 80 characters and must not exceed 132 Do not use Tab characters for indentation Remove trailing white space before committing code Attributes Always specify intent for dummy arguments. Don’t use dimension attribute to declare arrays because it is more verbose.\n  Use this: real, allocatable :: a(:), b(:,:) instead of: real, dimension(:), allocatable :: a real, dimension(:,:), allocatable :: b When defining many arrays of the same dimension, dimension can be used as an exception if it makes the code less verbose. If the optional attribute is used to declare a dummy argument, it should follow the intent attribute. End block closing statements Fortran allows certain block constructs or scopes to include the name of the program unit in the end statement.\nThe convention adopted herein is to include procedure names, module names and program names in the end statement Document public API code with FORD Documentation strings should be provided for all public and protected entities and their arguments or parameters.\nThis is currently accomplished using the FORD tool .\nFor help writing FORD style documentation please see the FORD wiki .\nThe following sections are most relevant for contributing new code: Writing Documentation Documentation Meta Data Limitations To write the “spec” (specification) for a new proposal, please place it in the FORD “pages” directory at doc/page/ .\nTo get help please see the “Writing Pages” and “Writing Documentation” pages\non the FORD wiki .","tags":"","url":"page/contributing/styleguide.html"}]}